(window.GAMES = window.GAMES || {})[1991] = [{"t":"2nd Super Robot Wars","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Winkysoft","u":"Banpresto, Nintendo","g":"Tactical role-playing","r":"December 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2nd_Super_Robot_Wars"},{"t":"3D Construction Kit","p":["Acorn Archimedes","Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","MS-DOS","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Incentive Software","u":"Domark, Incentive Software","g":"Game creation system","r":"May 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3D_Construction_Kit"},{"t":"4-D Boxing","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","Legacy Computer","macOS"],"d":"Distinctive Software","u":"Mindscape, Electronic Arts Victor, Electronic Arts","g":"Sport","r":"June 15, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/4-d-boxing","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coa6id.jpg","s":"4-D Boxing leaves behind any pretences of being a pure arcade game based on boxing, and aims to recreate the sport in full detail. The graphics engine allows for multiple camera angles and viewpoints, and considerably detailed visuals. These required more advanced hardware than was common at the time, but a stick-figure mode was included as a compromise. The moves on offer include all the uppercuts and hooks of a real fight, and the players are designed to move realistically to implement them.\n\nYou progress through the game by taking on a succession of increasingly difficult fighters, and get to train your boxer in between. Advanced action replays are included as well, so you can review all that happened."},{"t":"4D Sports Boxing","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","macOS","FM Towns"],"d":"Distinctive Software","u":"Mindscape, Electronic Arts","g":"Sports","r":"June 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/4D_Sports_Boxing"},{"t":"64th Street: A Detective Story","p":["Arcade"],"d":"C. P. Brain","u":"Jaleco","g":"Fighting, Hack and slash/Beat 'em up","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/64th-street-a-detective-story","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co9fj9.jpg","s":"Players One and Two start with the two selectable protagonists, Rick and Allen. Each private detective uses different fighting styles, attempt to solve cases by beating up each criminal they encounter. Lots of special items can be found by throwing enemies into the background and breaking things, typical of this popular genre in the 1990s arcades. Along the way they are harangued by all manner of thugs and toughs, whose costumes range from 1980s hip-hop wear to stereotypical pirates. The bosses are tough by way of strange special attacks."},{"t":"7 Colors","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Gamos","u":"Infogrames, HOT-B","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/7_Colors"},{"t":"8 Man","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/8_Man"},{"t":"A320 Airbus","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Thalion Software","u":"Thalion Software","g":"Flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A320_Airbus_(video_game)"},{"t":"Aa Eikō no Kōshien","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aa_Eik%C5%8D_no_K%C5%8Dshien"},{"t":"ABC Wide World of Sports Boxing","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga"],"d":"Acme Interactive, Cinemaware","u":"Data East, Mindscape","g":"Versus fighting, sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Wide_World_of_Sports_Boxing"},{"t":"Acrobat Mission","p":["Arcade"],"d":"UPL, Micronics","u":"Taito, Teichiku","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acrobat_Mission","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2oz4.jpg","s":"The players takes controls on one of two surviving fighters pilots, 'Jet' a male pilot and 'Nova' a female pilot and both flying the Icarus, a fast and powerful space-fighter, which comes equipped with a Standard laser. Two more weapons are available throughout the game - the wide shot (W) which spreads out in front of the ship and the Hurricane shot (H) which fires clusters of rotating balls of energy. Holding the fire button charges up a special attack and each weapon features a different energy blast and damage factor. The ships can also hold a maximum of two smart-bombs, one under each wing - although they can be activated by the players, they also do fall off easily and can be detonated by enemy shots. Interestingly, the Icarus can't collide with other ships or any part of the scenery - instead, it bounces off and only suffers damage from enemy shots. However, once the shield is depleted, the ship will fly out of control for a few seconds and grant the players a last chance to inflict damage to any remaining enemies before it explodes."},{"t":"Advanced Dungeons & Dragons: Secret of the Silver Blades","p":["Amiga"],"d":"SSI (Strategic Simulations, Inc.)","u":"SSI (Strategic Simulations, Inc.)","g":"Role-playing (RPG), Turn-based strategy (TBS), Adventure","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/advanced-dungeons-and-dragons-secret-of-the-silver-blades","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6jal.jpg","s":"The frigid valleys of the Dragonspire Mountains echo with the desperate plea of terrified miners: \"Heroes of the Forgotten Realms, save us from evil!\"\n\nUnwittingly digging in unhallowed ground, they have disturbed an ancient vault and released its terrible content. Scores of horrific creatures now infest the mine shaft - and threaten to escape into the world above!\n\nYou and your heroes must find the way to contain this scourge. All hope depends upon your ability to unravel the age-old mysteries surrounding this evil infestation.\n\nSecret of the Silver Blades improves on the award-winning game system used in Pool of Radiance and Curse of the Azure Bonds. Continue your exciting saga in the FORGOTTEN REALMS’ game world - transfer your characters from Curse of the Azure Bonds. Or create all-new characters. Either way, explore the largest 3-D adventuring expanse ever in an AD&D computer game. Battle monsters you've never before encountered. Use higher character levels, and invoke new, wondrous spells.\n\nAll this plus spectacular graphics add up to unequalled excitement in AD&D computer fantasy role-playing!"},{"t":"Advantage Tennis","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Infogrames","u":"Infogrames","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advantage_Tennis"},{"t":"Adventure Island II","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Now Production","u":"Hudson Soft, Konami","g":"Platform, Adventure","r":"February 1, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/adventure-island-ii","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5x41.jpg","s":"Adventure Island II is a side-scrolling platform game developed by Now Production and published by Hudson Soft that was released for the Nintendo Entertainment System in 1991. It is the sequel to Adventure Island and the second game in the series for the NES. While the first game was an adaptation of Westone's Wonder Boy arcade game, Adventure Island II is an entirely original work, as were all the subsequent Adventure Island games."},{"t":"Adventures of Beetlejuice: Skeletons in the Closet","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adventures_of_Beetlejuice:_Skeletons_in_the_Closet"},{"t":"Aerostar","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Sigma Pro-Tech","u":"Vic Tokai","g":"Shoot 'em ups","r":"June 28, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerostar_(video_game)"},{"t":"Aldynes","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Produce!","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Horizontal-scrolling shooter","r":"February 22, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldynes"},{"t":"Alice: An Interactive Museum","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Synergy Inc., Toshiba EMI Ltd","u":"Synergy Interactive Corp.","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alice:_An_Interactive_Museum"},{"t":"Alien Breed","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Team17","u":"Team17, MicroLeague","g":"Run and gun","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alien_Breed_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1xxd.jpg","s":"You've landed in a space station only to discover it is overrun by alien life. You must complete the missions in each floor in order to advance to the next. Make good use of you ammo and don't run out of health!"},{"t":"Alpha Mission II","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"March 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha_Mission_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2ku8.jpg","s":"Alpha Mission II is a 2D vertical scrolling shooter with a great deal of depth for two players moving vertically up the screen boards shooting anything that moves. Equip up to three pieces of armor, which also serve as weapons, for a variety of attacks such as the laser and flamethrower. Use them to defeat the evil “Fulvar” and save mankind!"},{"t":"Altered Space","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Software Creations","u":"Sony Imagesoft, Epic/Sony Records, Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Puzzle, Adventure","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altered_Space"},{"t":"Amarillo Slim Dealer's Choice","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"John Comeau","u":"Villa Crespo Software","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amarillo_Slim_Dealer's_Choice"},{"t":"Amazing Tater","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Atlus","u":"Atlus","g":"Puzzle","r":"August 2, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazing_Tater"},{"t":"American Gladiators","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Atari ST"],"d":"Incredible Technologies, Imagitec Design","u":"GameTek","g":"Sports","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_Gladiators_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co308z.jpg","s":"American Gladiators for NES is based off of the popular American TV show and allows the player to participate in a number of the typical events that were included in the show."},{"t":"Amnios","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Flying Chicken Software","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Multidirectional shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amnios"},{"t":"Ancients 1: Death Watch","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Farr-Ware Software","u":"Epic MegaGames","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancients_1:_Death_Watch"},{"t":"Another World","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Apple IIGS","Sega CD","Linux"],"d":"Delphine Software, Interplay, The Removers","u":"Delphine Software, U.S. Gold, Interplay, Virgin Games","g":"Action, platform","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Another_World_(video_game)"},{"t":"Aquales","p":["Sharp X68000"],"d":"Exact","u":"Exact","g":"Action role-playing, run and gun","r":"September 12, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aquales"},{"t":"Arachnophobia","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Commodore 64","MS-DOS"],"d":"BlueSky Software","u":"Disney Software","g":"Shoot 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arachnophobia_(video_game)"},{"t":"Arctic Adventure","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS"],"d":"Apogee Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Platform","r":"October 9, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arctic_Adventure_(video_game)"},{"t":"Arcus Odyssey","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Sharp X68000"],"d":"Wolf Team","u":"Wolf Team, Renovation Products, Sammy Corporation","g":"Action role-playing game","r":"June 14, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arcus_Odyssey"},{"t":"Are We There Yet?","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Manley & Associates","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Are_We_There_Yet%3F_(video_game)"},{"t":"Aretha II","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Japan Art Media","u":"Yanoman","g":"Role-playing game","r":"September 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aretha_II"},{"t":"Armada 2525","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Robert T. Smith","u":"Interstel Corporation","g":"4X","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armada_2525"},{"t":"Armadillo","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"AIM","u":"IGS","g":"Platform","r":"August 9, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armadillo_(video_game)"},{"t":"Armageddon (MUD)","p":[],"d":"Cat Rambo","g":"Multi-user dungeon","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armageddon_(MUD)"},{"t":"Armor Alley","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Information Access Technologies","u":"Three-Sixty Pacific","g":"Scrolling shooter, wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armor_Alley"},{"t":"Armour-Geddon","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Psygnosis","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armour-Geddon"},{"t":"Asmik-kun Land","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Graphic Research","u":"Asmik","g":"Action","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asmik-kun_Land"},{"t":"Asmik-kun World 2","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Cyclone System","u":"Asmik Ace Entertainment","g":"Action","r":"May 24, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asmik-kun_World_2"},{"t":"Attack of the Killer Tomatoes","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Imagineering, Equilibrium","u":"Toy Headquarters, Altron","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attack_of_the_Killer_Tomatoes_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6nsk.jpg","s":"Attack of the Killer Tomatoes is a platformer game released for the Nintendo Entertainment System, and later ported to the Gameboy. It was developed by Imagineering and based on the children's cartoon Attack of the Killer Tomatoes: The Animated Series, which in kind was based off of the movie franchise Attack of the Killer Tomatoes."},{"t":"Aussie Rules Footy","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"Laser Beam","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aussie_Rules_Footy"},{"t":"Auto Upturn","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auto_Upturn"},{"t":"Avenging Spirit","p":["Arcade"],"d":"C.P.Brain","u":"Jaleco","g":"Platform","r":"May 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avenging_Spirit","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cobv09.jpg","s":"Avenging Spirit, known in Japan as Phantasm, is a 1991 2-player platform arcade game developed by C.P. Brain and published by Jaleco. Players attack (or possess) enemies, collect power-ups, and defeat bosses to advance. Players can possess one of four characters with unique abilities at the start of the game, but the library of enemies expands and changes with each level."},{"t":"Awesome Golf","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Hand Made Software","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Sports","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Awesome_Golf"},{"t":"Ax Battler: A Legend of Golden Axe","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Aspect Co.","u":"Sega","g":"Action-adventure","r":"November 1, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ax_Battler:_A_Legend_of_Golden_Axe"},{"t":"Bahamut Senki","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Tactical role-playing","r":"March 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahamut_Senki"},{"t":"Banana Prince","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"KID","u":"Takara","g":"Platform","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banana_Prince","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coa56w.jpg","s":"Banana Prince was developed by KID and released in Japan by Takara on December 20, 1991. It was also released in Germany in February 1992, with slightly different graphics and gameshow questions based on the region. It is a single player platform game designed for the Nintendo Entertainment System."},{"t":"Banzai: Death Sortie of the Yamato","p":[],"u":"General Quarters Software","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banzai:_Death_Sortie_of_the_Yamato"},{"t":"Barbarian II","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Psygnosis","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbarian_II"},{"t":"Barbie","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS"],"d":"Imagineering","u":"Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Platform","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbie_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6nsr.jpg","s":"Barbie is a video game developed by Imagineering Inc based after the famous Barbie doll, created by Mattel Inc. It was created specifically as an attempt to entice more girls to play video games, and was one of the very few Nintendo Entertainment System games with girl-oriented themes. It was not a particularly well-received game at the time, but has received some recognition for having some quality puzzles and gameplay style. Others have criticized it for being an example of \"pink\" software; which refers to software that was designed for male audiences that is re-skinned for female gamers, without taking into account true female gamer preferences."},{"t":"Barcode Battler","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcode_Battler"},{"t":"Bart Simpson's Escape from Camp Deadly","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Imagineering","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Action, platform","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bart_Simpson's_Escape_from_Camp_Deadly","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4avf.jpg","s":"School's out and Bart's ready for some summer fun in the sun! Until he got the news - Homer and Marge were sending him and Lisa off to summer camp. Not just any camp, but the infamous Camp Deadly! How bad could it be? Well, with Ironfist Burns as head counselor and Nelson and his band of bullies as bunkmates, it's not exactly paradise. Bart and Lisa are determined to get out - but first they've got to survive outrageous food fights, killer bees and a life-threatening game of capture the flag. Help Bart and Lisa escape, at least in time for school!"},{"t":"Bases Loaded 4","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Jaleco","g":"Sports","r":"November 22, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bases_Loaded_4"},{"t":"Batman","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Atari Games","u":"Atari Games","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batman_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Batman: Return of the Joker","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sunsoft","u":"Sunsoft","g":"Run and gun, Platform","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batman:_Return_of_the_Joker","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2pij.jpg","s":"Batman: Return of the Joker was developed and published by Sunsoft as the second in their Batman series on the Nintendo Entertainment System. It departed from the original game by focusing on the comic-book version of Batman instead of the movie-inspired conception. The story itself was self-contained, and did not depict a pre-existing storyline. The game is a side-scrolling platformer with beat 'em up features."},{"t":"Battle Golfer Yui","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Santos","u":"Sega","g":"Sports, RPG","r":"February 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Golfer_Yui"},{"t":"Battle Isle","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Blue Byte","u":"Blue Byte, Ubi Soft","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Isle_(video_game)"},{"t":"Battle Storm","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Bullet Proof Software","u":"Yonezawa PR21","g":"Action, Strategy","r":"December 21, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_Storm"},{"t":"Battletoads","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Rare","u":"Tradewest, Masaya, Sega, Nintendo","g":"Beat 'em up, platform","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battletoads_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4i2w.jpg","s":"Battletoads is the first game of the Battletoads series. The game is a platform scrolling beat 'em up, with varying elements of racing, climbing and vehicle-based obstacle courses. Players start with three lives each time the game is started, which get replenished every time the player continues after getting defeated. The game contains no saving system or password features. The player has a maximum of six hit points that can be replenished by eating flies.\n\nIt is by some considered to be one of the hardest games of all time, and it have previously been listed at 8th place in IGN's \"Top 10 Most Difficult Games to Beat.\""},{"t":"Battletoads","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Rare","u":"Tradewest, Nintendo, Masaya","g":"Action Beat 'em up","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battletoads_(Game_Boy_video_game)"},{"t":"Beast Wrestler","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Riot","u":"Riot, Renovation Products","g":"Fighting","r":"November 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beast_Wrestler"},{"t":"Bible Adventures","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive","Windows"],"d":"Wisdom Tree","u":"Wisdom Tree","g":"Side scroller/Platform game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bible_Adventures","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5xp0.jpg","s":"Bible Adventures is an unlicensed religious game released for the Nintendo Entertainment System in 1990. It was developed and published by Wisdom Tree in 1990. It was later ported to the Sega Mega Drive in 1995.\nIn this Single or Multiplayer game, there are three different stories that can played throughout, including Baby Moses, Noah's Ark and David and Goliath, all being from the Old Testament of the Bible. The game has a gameplay style similar to Super Mario Bros. 2."},{"t":"Big Karnak","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Gaelco","u":"Gaelco","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Karnak"},{"t":"Big Run","p":["Arcade","Amiga","Atari ST","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Jaleco","u":"Jaleco","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Run_(video_game)"},{"t":"Bill & Ted's Excellent Adventure","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Al Baker & Associates","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_&_Ted's_Excellent_Adventure_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Bill & Ted's Excellent Game Boy Adventure: A Bogus Journey!","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Action, puzzle","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_&_Ted's_Excellent_Game_Boy_Adventure:_A_Bogus_Journey!"},{"t":"Bill & Ted's Excellent Video Game Adventure","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Rocket Science Productions","u":"LJN","g":"Action-adventure","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_&_Ted's_Excellent_Video_Game_Adventure"},{"t":"Bill Elliott's NASCAR Challenge","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","macOS","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Distinctive Software","u":"Konami","g":"Racing","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Elliott's_NASCAR_Challenge"},{"t":"Bill Laimbeer's Combat Basketball","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Hewson Consultants","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Sport","r":"December 1, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/bill-laimbeers-combat-basketball","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4xrv.jpg","s":"The game predicts a science-fiction version of the year 2030 in which there are only robot basketball players (excluding Bill Laimbeer). Basketball teams play in gruelling league matches where new players are bought and sold. Within this future, basketball uses a dedicated robot to perform the toss up at the start of each match as referees had been fired by Bill Laimbeer sometime prior to the year 2030. As a result, players now wear armor to their games and weapons are thrown from the audience."},{"t":"Bimini Run","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Microsmiths","u":"Nuvision Entertainment","g":"Shooter","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bimini_Run"},{"t":"Birds of Prey","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Argonaut Software","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Flight simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birds_of_Prey_(video_game)"},{"t":"Black Heart","p":["Arcade"],"d":"NMK","u":"UPL","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"May 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Heart_(video_game)"},{"t":"Blade Master","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Irem","u":"Irem","g":"Hack and slash, beat 'em up","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blade_Master"},{"t":"Blade Warrior","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","Atari ST"],"u":"Image Works","g":"Beat 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blade_Warrior"},{"t":"Blaster Master Boy","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Aicom","u":"Sunsoft","g":"Action","r":"August 23, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blaster_Master_Boy"},{"t":"Block Block","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Breakout","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Block_Block"},{"t":"Blue Almanac","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Hot-B","u":"Kodansha","g":"Role-playing","r":"June 22, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Almanac"},{"t":"Bo Jackson Baseball","p":["MS-DOS","Nintendo Entertainment System","PC Engine"],"d":"Beam Software, Acme Interactive, Cinemaware","u":"Data East, Mindscape","g":"Sports","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bo_Jackson_Baseball"},{"t":"Bomberman II","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Maze","r":"June 28, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bomberman_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4o46.jpg","s":"Bomberman II follows the popular Famicom/NES game Bomberman, incorporating a number of new features to the series. The game maintained the classic Bomberman game-type, requiring the player to navigate a maze of blocks using bombs to make a path. It added a two-player and three-player mode, and had a more developed storyline. The game was developed by Hudson Soft and released on the Family Computer and the NES. It was marketed as Dynablaster in Europe."},{"t":"Bonk's Revenge","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Red Company, Mutech","u":"Hudson Soft, NEC","g":"Platform","r":"July 19, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonk's_Revenge","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6v24.jpg","s":"In Bonk's Revenge, King Drool hijacks half the moon in an attempt for world domination! Of course it's up to our favorite prehistoric hero to stop him, and so Bonk embarks on an all-new quest to save the world.\n\nBonk’s second adventure leads him through 7 exotic levels, from sunny beaches to winter wonderlands, including 8 special bonus stages. Bonk’s new enemies include bad-attitude dinosaurs, vicious piranhas, oversized insects and many more treacherous foes. However, as always, Bonk comes packed with an arsenal of his very own dangerous moves.\n\nStaying true to his name, Bonk’s ultimate weapon is still his gigantic head! Bonk can pummel his enemies into submission with his signature headbutt or shake the very ground they walk on by pile-driving straight into the earth. A master of his terrain, Bonk can also climb various objects using his razor sharp teeth. Of course, what would Bonk be without his favorite caveman power-ups? That’s right, the roasted meat is back, but this time with an all new Bonk!\n\nBy eating one small slab of meat, Bonk can now transform into Zombie Bonk, allowing him to petrify his enemies with a powerful pile driver or fireball. But by eating two small slabs or one large slab, Bonk will transform into Butthead Bonk, an all-powerful being who breathes fire, eradicating his enemies at will."},{"t":"Boppin'","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Accursed Toys","u":"Karmasoft, Apogee","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boppin'"},{"t":"Brandish","p":["PC-9800 series","FM Towns"],"d":"Nihon Falcom, Mantra, NEC, Koei","u":"Nihon Falcom, Mantra, NEC, Koei","g":"Action role-playing","r":"October 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brandish_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2mku.jpg","s":"The game is an action RPG, a dungeon crawler with plenty of creatures to defeat and many weapons, armor, and other treasure to collect. You can also use magic spells. The camera rotates automatically according to your movements. You also have the ability to jump forwards."},{"t":"Brat","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Foursfield","u":"Image Works","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brat_(video_game)"},{"t":"Brigade Commander","p":["Amiga"],"d":"TTR Development","u":"TTR Development","g":"Real-time strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brigade_Commander_(video_game)"},{"t":"Brix","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Epic MegaGames","u":"MicroLeague Interactive Software","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brix_(video_game)"},{"t":"Bubble Bath Babes","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"C&E","u":"Hacker International, American Video Entertainment","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bubble_Bath_Babes"},{"t":"Bubblegum Crash!","p":["PC Engine"],"u":"Naxat","g":"Adventure","r":"December 6, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bubblegum_Crash!"},{"t":"BurgerTime Deluxe","p":["Game Boy"],"g":"Platform, Puzzle, Adventure, Arcade","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BurgerTime_Deluxe","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6aso.jpg","s":"Based on BurgerTime, the Arcade classic from 1982, BurgerTime Deluxe once again puts you in the role of chef Peter Pepper, a guy who can really build a burger. You maneuver the cook up and down ladders and left and right across floors in order to walk across various parts of giant hamburgers, such as large lettuce, big buns and massive meat patties. After you cross any section of any hamburger, it will drop one level and push any other burger part within that stack down a floor as well. The object of the game is to build all the hamburgers in each stage by dropping all the makings of each burger into completed stacks at the bottom of the screen.\n\nAs you go about your burger building business, Food Foes Mr. Pickle, Mr. Egg and Mr. Hot dog (also known as Frank N. Furter) will chase after you. You can destroy these culinary cutups by trapping them in falling burger parts or by tricking them into walking on a burger part you are in the process of dropping. To freeze the Food Foes, you can sprinkle a dash of pepper on them. You begin the game with a small pepper supply and can pick up additional pepper shakers that appear throughout the levels. You can also pick up bonus points (in the guise of cups and saucers) and extra lives.\n\nUnlike the original BurgerTime, which had less than 10 different non-scrolling screens, BurgerTime Deluxe has seven levels with four stages in each level for a grand total of 28 screens, most of which scroll to some degree. A password feature enables you to return to the beginning of last level you played. Also, this version of the game features a two-player competitive mode (as long as you have two copies of the game, an extra Game Boy, and a link cable)."},{"t":"Burning Fight","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"May 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burning_Fight","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/n7x7qeqtdlxcswpmf5uv.jpg","s":"Duke and Billy, two popular and renowned New York City Police detectives, are hot on the trail of a dangerous Japanese crime syndicate. Their investigation has led them to the mean streets of Japan where they meet a police officer and martial arts expert named Ryu. Ryu agrees to join the NYPD detectives as they track down the crime syndicate bosses and end their reign of terror once and for all."},{"t":"BushBuck Charms, Viking Ships & Dodo Eggs","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Reckon","u":"PC Globe","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BushBuck_Charms,_Viking_Ships_&_Dodo_Eggs"},{"t":"Captain America and The Avengers","p":["Arcade","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Data East","u":"Data East, Mindscape","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Captain_America_and_The_Avengers","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co98ie.jpg","s":"Captain America and the Avengers is an arcade game released by Data East in 1991. It features the Marvel Comics characters The Avengers in a side-scrolling brawling and shooting adventure to defeat the evil Red Skull."},{"t":"Captain Commando","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Captain_Commando_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co8sq7.jpg","s":"Captain Commando is a futuristic side-scrolling beat 'em up video game. The game stars the titular superhero who was originally conceived as a fictional spokesman used by Capcom USA in the company's console games during the late 1980s. Among its unique features are the option for 4 simultaneous co-op play and the ability to control mecha suits scattered on the stages."},{"t":"Captain Planet and the Planeteers","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Amiga","Atari ST","Amstrad CPC"],"d":"Chris Gray Enterprises, NovaLogic","u":"Mindscape, Sega","g":"2D action platformer","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Captain_Planet_and_the_Planeteers_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6ntc.jpg","s":"Play as Captain Planet and his Planeteers is this platform game."},{"t":"Cardiaxx","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Eclipse Design","u":"Electronic Zoo","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardiaxx"},{"t":"Castle of Dr. Brain","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","macOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Educational, puzzle adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_of_Dr._Brain","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co31gr.jpg","s":"Castle of Dr. Brain is the first in a two-part series of educational puzzle-games from Sierra. This brain-blasting game is set, how surprisingly, in Dr. Brain's castle, where the player will have to beat Dr. Brain's traps and puzzles in order to fulfill the assignment given."},{"t":"Castles","p":["MS-DOS","Atari ST","FM Towns"],"d":"Quicksilver Software, Silicon & Synapse","u":"Interplay Productions","g":"Simulation, strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castles_(video_game)"},{"t":"Castlevania II: Belmont's Revenge","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platform","r":"July 12, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castlevania_II:_Belmont's_Revenge","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cob9a8.jpg","s":"Fifteen years after vanquishing the emperor of evil, Count Dracula, Christopher Belmont now faces a vampire swearing vengeance. Drac's back. And he's turned your son, Soleiyu Belmont, into a deadly demon to do his dirty deeds. Now not only do you have to rescue your kid from the Count's clutches, you have to first track him down in four creep packed Castles. Fortunately, you posses weapons like armor piercing Battle Axes, beast burning Holy Water, and the Legendary Mystic Whip. And you can increase your arsenal with power up items like Crystal Balls, Hearts, and Candles. Strap on your shield and step into the shadows. That is, if you're prepared for the dwellers of darkness."},{"t":"Catacomb 3-D","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"id Software","u":"Softdisk","g":"First-person shooter","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catacomb_3-D"},{"t":"Cave Noire","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Roguelike, role-playing","r":"April 19, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave_Noire"},{"t":"Caveman Ninja","p":["MS-DOS","Arcade"],"d":"Eden Entertainment Software, Elite Systems, Data East, MotiveTime Ltd","u":"Takara, MotiveTime Ltd, New World Computing, G-MODE, Tec Toy, Elite Systems, Data East","g":"Platform","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/caveman-ninja","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5uz4.jpg","s":"Back in the distant past (way back - like when “rock and roll” was just rock and fast food was dinner you couldn’t catch), lived two cave dudes named Joe and Mac. Whether it was dinosaur demolition or pterodactyl hang-glidin’, life for the boys was pretty cool.\n\nCool, that is, until a bogus bunch of neanderthal nerds crashed their village and scared off all the cave-babes. Now the cave-babes are out there - SOMEWHERE, lost and reeeeally lonely… so what are you waitin’ for? It’s up to Joe and Mac to get ‘em back.\n\n* Fight flying pterodactyls in one or two-player simultaneous play.\n* 12 cliff-hangin’ levels with more than 6 bonus levels to find…\n* Amazing arcade graphics will keep you clubbin’ to find your babes…\n* Joe & Mac’s pre-hysterical escapades will keep you comin’ back for more!"},{"t":"Celtic Legends","p":[],"d":"Ubi Soft","u":"Ubi Soft","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celtic_Legends"},{"t":"Champion of the Raj","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Level 9 Computing","u":"Personal Software Services","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Champion_of_the_Raj"},{"t":"Championship Rally","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Human Entertainment","u":"HAL Laboratory, Kaken","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Championship_Rally_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Chaos World","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Natsume","u":"Natsume","g":"Role-playing","r":"October 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaos_World"},{"t":"Checkered Flag","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Atari Corporation","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Checkered_Flag_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Chessmaster 3000","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"The Software Toolworks","u":"The Software Toolworks","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chessmaster_3000"},{"t":"Choplifter II","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"Victor Musical Industries, JVC Musical Industries","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Choplifter_II"},{"t":"Choujin Sentai Jetman","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Natsume Co., Ltd.","u":"Angel","g":"Platform","r":"December 21, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/choujin-sentai-jetman","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5ysw.jpg","s":"In the year 199X, Earth Defense Sky Force orbits the planet. They survey the Earth 24 hours a day to maintain world peace. When problems arise they send in agents to help out. SF-W6 and SF-W3 are two such agents. Upon completing a mission they are promoted to J-project status. Aboard the ship, The Chief explains that when hit with Birdronic waves, they will turn into Choujin Sentai. Immediately after Tendou Ryu's conversion into a Jetman, the enemy Vyram attack. Radiguet, the leader of the Vyram, announces that they have conquered other detentions and now plan to conquer the 3rd dimension.\n\nDuring this attack, Ryu loses his girlfriend Rie through a hole in the ship. The Birdronic wave machine shatters and the remaining 4 waves are sent down to Earth at random. After the attack Ryu and The Chief search the Earth to find the remaining members of the group."},{"t":"Chuck Rock","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Acorn Archimedes"],"d":"Core Design","u":"Core Design, Krisalis Software, Sony Imagesoft, Sega","g":"Platform","r":"April 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuck_Rock","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2h91.jpg","s":"Caveman Chuck Rock must save his wife from dinosaurs by beating down enemies with his stomach and by hurling rocks in this side-scrolling action game."},{"t":"Chuck Yeager's Air Combat","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Brent Iverson","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Air combat simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuck_Yeager's_Air_Combat"},{"t":"Civilization","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","PC-9800 series","Atari ST","Windows","macOS","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"MicroProse","u":"MicroProse, Asmik Ace, Koei","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Civilization_(video_game)"},{"t":"CJ's Elephant Antics","p":["Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum","Atari ST","Amiga","Nintendo Entertainment System","Windows"],"d":"Genesis, Big Red","u":"Codemasters","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CJ's_Elephant_Antics"},{"t":"Clutch Hitter","p":["Arcade","Game Gear"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clutch_Hitter"},{"t":"Cobra Mission: Panic in Cobra City","p":["PC-9800 series","MS-DOS"],"d":"INOS","u":"Hard, Megatech Software","g":"Adventure, eroge","r":"October 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobra_Mission:_Panic_in_Cobra_City"},{"t":"Cocoron","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"K2","u":"Sur Dé Wave","g":"Platform","r":"May 3, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cocoron"},{"t":"Commander Keen in Aliens Ate My Babysitter","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"id Software","u":"FormGen","g":"Side-scrolling platformer","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commander_Keen_in_Aliens_Ate_My_Babysitter","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co25s5.jpg","s":"A mean bunch of hungry aliens have your babysitter and they're planning to make her their main course. Now it's up to you, as Billy's alter ego Commander Keen, to climb into your homemade Megarocket and save her. Or risk explaining what happened to your parents. The only question is: Can you complete your mission before dinnertime?"},{"t":"Commander Keen in Goodbye, Galaxy","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"id Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Side-scrolling platformer","r":"December 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commander_Keen_in_Goodbye,_Galaxy"},{"t":"Commander Keen in Keen Dreams","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"id Software","u":"Softdisk Publishing","g":"Side-scrolling platformer","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commander_Keen_in_Keen_Dreams","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co25s2.jpg","s":"Trapped in a land where giant vegetables rule the day, you must fight your way through an army of vicious broccoli beasts, savage asparagus warriors and a bushel of other leafy green nasties before battling the fiercest spud of all, King Boobus Tuber. Unless you want a one way trip to the salad bar in the sky, you'll have to finish all your veggies. Before they finish you!"},{"t":"Commander Keen: Goodbye Galaxy!","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"id Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Platform, Adventure","r":"December 15, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/commander-keen-goodbye-galaxy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4wnm.jpg","s":"Following the Invasion of the Vorticons, the story arc of Goodbye Galaxy! takes Commander Keen to a planet to rescue the Keepers of the Secret of the Oracle, which know the location of the Shikadi's doomsday device, The Armageddon Machine."},{"t":"Conan the Cimmerian","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Synergistic Software","u":"Virgin Games","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conan_the_Cimmerian_(video_game)"},{"t":"Conan: The Mysteries of Time","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Eastridge Technology","u":"Mindscape","g":"Action","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conan:_The_Mysteries_of_Time"},{"t":"Conflict: Middle East","p":[],"d":"Strategic Simulations","u":"Strategic Simulations","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict:_Middle_East"},{"t":"Conquests of the Longbow: The Legend of Robin Hood","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conquests_of_the_Longbow:_The_Legend_of_Robin_Hood","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6z2w.jpg","s":"Conquests of the Longbow is an adventure game and a follow-up to Conquests of Camelot. It uses an icon-based interface common to most contemporary Sierra adventures. Like its spiritual predecessor, it differs from most other games in the genre by reducing traditional inventory-based puzzles in favor of exploration and varied tasks dictated by the situation at hand. These include talking to characters, gathering information, making decisions, solving riddles, or participating in mini-games such as combat, archery contest, and Nine Men's Morris (with adjustable difficulty)."},{"t":"Cool Croc Twins","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Commodore 64","MS-DOS"],"d":"Arcade Masters","u":"Empire Software","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cool_Croc_Twins"},{"t":"Coryoon: Child of Dragon","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Naxat Soft","u":"Naxat Soft","g":"Horizontal-scrolling shooter","r":"November 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coryoon:_Child_of_Dragon"},{"t":"Cosmic Cop","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Irem","u":"Irem","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmic_Cop"},{"t":"Cosmic Fantasy 2","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Nippon Telenet","u":"Nippon Telenet, Working Designs","g":"Role-playing","r":"April 5, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmic_Fantasy_2"},{"t":"Cotton: Fantastic Night Dreams","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Success","u":"Sega, Hudson Soft, Turbo Technologies Inc., Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotton:_Fantastic_Night_Dreams"},{"t":"Cover Girl Strip Poker","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","Commodore 64","Amiga CDTV"],"d":"Emotional Pictures","u":"Emotional Pictures","g":"Card game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cover_Girl_Strip_Poker"},{"t":"Creepy Castle","p":["macOS"],"u":"Reactor Inc.","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Creepy_Castle_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Crisis Force","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Vertically scrolling shooter","r":"August 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crisis_Force","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6v2t.jpg","s":"The denizens of Atlantis have arisen after a slumber of 10,000 years, and only Asuka and Maya can save the world from destruction in their Aurawing aircraft! Crisis Force was Konami's last shoot-'em-up game for the Famicom, and it pushes the hardware to its absolute limits."},{"t":"Crisis in the Kremlin","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Barbu Corporation, Spectrum HoloByte","u":"Spectrum HoloByte, MicroProse","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crisis_in_the_Kremlin"},{"t":"Crossed Swords","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"Alpha Denshi","u":"SNK, ADK","g":"Action role-playing, hack and slash","r":"July 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crossed_Swords_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1uk1.jpg","s":"Crossed Swords is a hack & slash action RPG arcade game developed by Alpha Denshi and published by SNK for the Neo Geo arcade system in 1990 and Neo Geo console in 1991. Its gameplay was similar to SNK's earlier 1990 first-person shooter and beat 'em up game, The Super Spy, but with role-playing game elements and hack & slash combat instead of shooting and fist-fighting.[1]\n\nThe game allowed cooperative gameplay with up to two players on the same screen.[1] As only the player characters' silhouettes are visible on screen as wire-frame models, much like Punch-Out!!,[2] the game's perspective may be considered either first-person[1] or third-person.[3] The game was later cited as an inspiration for the 2010 first-person fighting game Rage of the Gladiator.[4] A sequel of the game entitled Crossed Swords 2 was released in Japan on May 2, 1995 for the Neo-Geo CD, and was one of the few games designed specifically for the Neo-Geo CD, rather than being an arcade port.[5]"},{"t":"Cruise for a Corpse","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Delphine Software","u":"Erbe Software, Interplay Productions, U.S. Gold","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cruise_for_a_Corpse","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co25jn.jpg","s":"Cruise for a Corpse features the exclusive Cinematique operating system, designed by Delphine to offer a user friendly point and click playing style. Cinematique has been further refined since the release of Operation Stealth to offer even more depth of gameplay and a wider range of options.\n\nTake part in a high seas murder mystery!\nAs inspector Raoul Dusentier you have been invited on a dream cruise in the Mediterranean, but no sooner has the cruise begun when you are summoned to investigate a scandalous crime- the murder of your host!"},{"t":"Crystal Caves","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Apogee Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Platform","r":"October 23, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_Caves","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2ptx.jpg","s":"Enter an underground world of treasures and traps in Crystal Caves, a colorful EGA game that rivals any commercial game with its arcade quality, puzzles and graphics. As Mylo Steamwitz, enter cave after cave of adventure, gathering teasures and evading countless hazards.\n\nCrystal Caves is a mixing of Nintendo's Mario Brothers, Commander Keen, and Indiana Jones. As with all Apogee games, a high priority has been placed on solving ingenious puzzles built into the game.\n\nEpisodes include \"Trouble with Twibbles\", \"Slugging it Out\", and \"Mylo Verses the Supernova\""},{"t":"Crystal Warriors","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Strategy","r":"December 13, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal_Warriors","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6gbt.jpg","s":"The continent of Tyramus has lived in peace for almost a century, thanks to the four elemental crystals of the kingdom of Arliel. However, peace is threatened by the invading armies of the Jyn Empire, led by Emperor Grym, who wants to use the four crystals to rule over Tyramus. Fortunately, Princess Iris can escape with one of the crystals and now the player has to protect her and the crystal and somehow find a way to drive back the hordes of Jyn.\n\nGameplay in Crystal Warriors is quite similar to Shining Force. The player fights in turn-based battles controlling different characters like fighters, healers, rangers and mages, all with different strengths and skills. Whenever two units engage in battle, they enter a duel mode lasting for up to two turns. In this mode the player can choose between four commands: \"battle\" (attack), \"retreat\", \"spell\" and \"monster\". While the first three commands are quite self-explanatory, the \"monster\" command is interesting. Whenever a non-human enemy is defeated, it can be tamed and be used in battles. While most monsters do not have many HP they can serve as good attackers for physically weak characters or take some hits in order to protect his master. In-between battles the player can visit towns to rest, buy weapons and spells or recruit new party members."},{"t":"Cyber Citizen Shockman 2: A New Menace","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Winds","u":"Masaya Games, NCS Corporation, Hudson Soft, Ratalaika Games","g":"Platform","r":"April 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyber_Citizen_Shockman_2:_A_New_Menace"},{"t":"Cyber City Oedo 808","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyber_City_Oedo_808"},{"t":"Cyber Stadium Series—Base Wars","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Ultra Games","g":"Sports","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyber_Stadium_Series%E2%80%94Base_Wars"},{"t":"D-Force","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Cream","u":"Asmik","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D-Force"},{"t":"D. D. Crew","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Sega AM3","u":"Sega","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D._D._Crew"},{"t":"D/Generation","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","Atari ST"],"u":"Mindscape","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D/Generation"},{"t":"Daffy Duck, P.I.: The Case of the Missing Letters","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daffy_Duck,_P.I.:_The_Case_of_the_Missing_Letters"},{"t":"Dahna: Megami Tanjō","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"IGS","u":"IGS","g":"Action","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dahna:_Megami_Tanj%C5%8D"},{"t":"Danan: The Jungle Fighter","p":["Sega Master System"],"d":"Whiteboard","u":"Sega","g":"Action adventure","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danan:_The_Jungle_Fighter"},{"t":"Dangerous Dave in the Haunted Mansion","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Gamer's Edge, id Software","u":"Softdisk Publishing","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dangerous_Dave_in_the_Haunted_Mansion","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7wdd.jpg","s":"You're a one-of-a-kind dangerous guy named Dave. Born and raised in the hill country of Backwater, USA, you're quite deft with the shotgun, and afraid of nothing. Then again, you haven't been in the haunted mansion up on the hill. As you hunt for your lost little brother, Delbert, you must enter a house full of zombies, spooks, and bad guys and turn them into hamburger meat, before they make a meal out of you in Dangerous Dave in the Haunted Mansion!"},{"t":"Danny Sullivan's Indy Heat","p":["Arcade","Atari ST"],"d":"Leland Corporation, Rare, Sales Curve","u":"Leland Corporation, Tradewest, Sales Curve","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danny_Sullivan's_Indy_Heat","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/qne2ofyoro0cw0eooukw.jpg","s":"A 3-player, Indy style racing game with 5 cars that each race around 15 of Danny Sullivan's favorite tracks. Players can turbo, crash, catch fire, and lose fuel, but they may pit stop for repair. Danny himself always races (yellow car) and players can earn money for more turbo, faster pit crew, acceleration, brakes, and tires."},{"t":"Darius Twin","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"March 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darius_Twin","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2isi.jpg","s":"Darius Twin is a shoot 'em up video game released for the Super NES in 1991. It was the first new installment in the Darius series to not be originally designed for the arcades, and also the first to have a normal 4:3 screen-ratio. Interestingly, while it's a unique game in the Darius series, it appears to reuse sprites and background designs from Darius and Darius II. Also, while the names of the bosses are new, they really are reworkings of previous bosses for the most part."},{"t":"Dark Ages","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS"],"d":"Scenario Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Platform","r":"February 1, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Ages_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Darkman","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Commodore 64","Amstrad CPC","Amiga","Game Boy","ZX Spectrum","Atari ST"],"d":"Ocean Software, Painting by Numbers","u":"Ocean Software, Hit Squad","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darkman_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2mx2.jpg","s":"The game's plot is loosely based on the film of the same name. In this side-scrolling platformer, the player controls Darkman, a superhero who can jump, kick and punch, as well as swing from a rope during action sequences between levels. In each level, Darkman disguises himself as the boss of the level, whom he must defeat before the time limit elapses."},{"t":"Dead Moon","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Zap","u":"TSS, Natsume Inc.","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"February 22, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dead_Moon_(video_game)"},{"t":"Death Knights of Krynn","p":["Amiga","Commodore 64","MS-DOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Strategic Simulations","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Role-playing video game, tactical RPG","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_Knights_of_Krynn","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5xq1.jpg","s":"Even after defeating Myrtani and putting an end to the draconian threat, evil still lurks in Krynn. Your daring group of adventurers now face a far greater danger...the threat of the dreaded lord Soth and his growing undead army, plotting to destroy all that is good in Krynn."},{"t":"Deathbringer","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Oxford Digital Enterprises","u":"Empire","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deathbringer_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Decap Attack","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Vic Tokai","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"October 9, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decap_Attack"},{"t":"Derby Stallion: Best Keiba","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"ASCII Corporation","u":"ASCII Corporation","g":"Simulation","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derby_Stallion:_Best_Keiba"},{"t":"Desert Assault","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Data East","u":"Data East","g":"Run and gun","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desert_Assault"},{"t":"Detana!! TwinBee","p":["Arcade","Sharp X68000"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Detana!!_TwinBee"},{"t":"Deuteros","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Activision","u":"Activision","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deuteros"},{"t":"Devilish","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Opera House","u":"Genki, Sega, Sage's Creation, Inc.","g":"Arcade","r":"March 29, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/devilish","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co920c.jpg","s":"A prince and princess were in love with each other until a jealous demon turned the happy couple into a set of stone paddles; resembling that of the pinball kind.Suddenly, a ball came into existence that could use the prince and princess in order to defeat the demons that now rule over the kingdom. Players have to use two paddles; the upper one is used to defeat demons while the lower one helps to prevent the player from dropping to its demise at the bottom of the board. By defeating bad guys and smashing through breakable blocks, the player advances towards the boss of each level.\nResembling a harder version of Arkanoid, the vertically scrolling screen forces the ball to move constantly forward in an attempt to reach the boss.\nOnly the Sega Mega Drive version has a two-player option. Players can choose between three difficulty levels in the Game Gear version and can also select an option to do a time trial."},{"t":"Dezaemon","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Athena","u":"Athena","g":"Scrolling shooter, game creation system","r":"September 13, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dezaemon"},{"t":"Die Kathedrale","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Weltenschmiede","u":"Software 2000","g":"Interactive fiction","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Die_Kathedrale"},{"t":"Dino Land","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Sharp X68000"],"d":"Wolf Team","u":"Wolf Team, Renovation Products","g":"Pinball","r":"August 2, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dino_Land"},{"t":"Disciples of Steel","p":["MS-DOS","Atari ST"],"d":"Megasoft Entertainment","u":"FormGen","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disciples_of_Steel"},{"t":"Discworld MUD","p":[],"d":"David \"Pinkfish\" Bennett, Craig \"Furball\" Richmond, Sean A. \"Lynscar\" Reith, Evan Scott, Derek \"Ceres\" Harding, Jacqui \"Sojan\" Greenland, project community","g":"Comic fantasy MUD","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discworld_MUD"},{"t":"Disney's TaleSpin","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Shooter","r":"December 11, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/disneys-talespin--4","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3lgz.jpg","s":"TaleSpin is a scrolling shooter video game based on the Disney television series TaleSpin. The game was developed by Capcom for the NES in 1991 and was ported to the Game Boy in 1992. The Game Boy version is essentially a slightly stripped-down version of the game.\n\nThe NES version of the game was included in The Disney Afternoon Collection compilation for PC, PlayStation 4, and Xbox One in April 2017.\n\nThe gameplay consists of maneuvering Baloo's plane \"The Sea Duck\" through each level, fending off incoming enemies and avoiding obstacles. Items can be collected for extra lives or to add to the total cash score. The plane can be rotated upside to traverse back through the level, but only on horizontally scrolling areas. At the end of each level, the player is required to fight a boss enemy by repeatedly shooting its weak points. After beating a level, the player has the option to buy upgrades for Baloo's plane with the money collected, before proceeding to the next level. In bonus levels the player controls Kit on an airfoil to pop balloons for bonus points."},{"t":"Disney's The Little Mermaid","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Platform","r":"July 19, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/disneys-the-little-mermaid--1","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1vyv.jpg","s":"In this version of Disney's The Little Mermaid you play as Ariel the mermaid.\n\nAriel has already met Eric, and they plan to wed, but the sea-witch Ursala has taken control of the ocean! So Ariel (After explaining what's going on to Eric) becomes a mermaid once more and sets off to rescue the sea.\n\nThe game takes place from a side view and Ariel (Swimming most of the time, but hopping around on the land occasionally) can shoot bubbles to trap her foes and can then throw them at each other. She can also dig through sand to find treasure and pick up sea shells to help her break chests open."},{"t":"Dizzy Down the Rapids","p":["Amstrad CPC","ZX Spectrum","Commodore 64"],"u":"Codemasters","g":"Action","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dizzy_Down_the_Rapids"},{"t":"Dizzy Panic","p":["Amstrad CPC","ZX Spectrum","Commodore 64","Sega Master System","Game Gear"],"d":"Big Red Software","u":"Codemasters","g":"Puzzle","r":"May 14, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dizzy_Panic"},{"t":"Dizzy: Prince of the Yolkfolk","p":["Amstrad CPC","ZX Spectrum","Commodore 64","Atari ST","Amiga","MS-DOS","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Big Red Software","u":"Codemasters","g":"Adventure, platform","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dizzy:_Prince_of_the_Yolkfolk","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6nuq.jpg","s":"Grand Dizzy was feeling very down one day, so Dizzy and Daisy decided to cheer him up by baking him his favourite food, cherry pie. \"Pogie pinched the cheeries!\" she cries, as she saw Pogies pawprint in the cupboard. Pogie promptly darted of into the enchanted forest to escape Daises rage.\n\nDizzy and Daisy decided to go into the forest to catch Pogie and gather some more cheeries at the same time. Whilst seaching for Pogie they got lost and found a castle, where they thought they would find help, but it seemed deserted. They looked in the castle for pogie, but Daisy caught herself on a mystic spinning wheel. She started feeling really sleepy, so she found a bed and went to sleep.\n\n\"Wake up Daisy, wake up!\" shoued Dizzy. But before he could wake her up, Rockwart the troll caught him and locked him up underground."},{"t":"Doki! Doki! Yūenchi: Crazy Land Daisakusen","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"KID","u":"VAP, American Softworks","g":"Action","r":"August 9, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doki!_Doki!_Y%C5%ABenchi:_Crazy_Land_Daisakusen"},{"t":"Doraemon: Nobita's Dorabian Nights","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doraemon:_Nobita's_Dorabian_Nights"},{"t":"Double Axle","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Racing","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_Axle"},{"t":"Double Dragon III: The Sacred Stones","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Technōs Japan","u":"Technōs Japan, Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"February 22, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_Dragon_III:_The_Sacred_Stones","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3wuo.jpg","s":"Billy, Jimmy, and the whole Double Dragon gang fight for the Sacred Stones to save in the world in yet another beat-em-up."},{"t":"Download 2","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Alfa System","u":"NEC Avenue","g":"Horizontal-scrolling shooter","r":"March 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Download_2"},{"t":"Dr. Sbaitso","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dr._Sbaitso"},{"t":"Dracula the Undead","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Hand Made Software","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dracula_the_Undead_(video_game)"},{"t":"Dragon's Lair: The Legend","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Elite Systems","u":"CSG Imagesoft, Elite Systems, Epic/Sony Records","g":"Platform","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dragon's_Lair:_The_Legend"},{"t":"Driver's Eyes","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Driving simulator","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Driver's_Eyes"},{"t":"Duke Nukem","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Apogee Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Platform","r":"July 1, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duke_Nukem_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co52uz.jpg","s":"Duke Nukem is the debut of the world’s greatest wise-cracking, alien-punching, gun-toting badass - a man who would later go on to combine both ass-kicking and bubblegum in a revolutionary way. In a world ravaged by Dr. Proton’s villainous ambitions, the original video game action hero must rise against the evil scientist’s army of sinister Techbots. And although Duke isn’t exactly what the Doctor ordered, he’s exactly what the world needed."},{"t":"Dusk of the Gods","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Event Horizon Software","u":"Interstel Corporation","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dusk_of_the_Gods"},{"t":"Dvorak on Typing","p":["Windows","Amiga","macOS"],"d":"Presage Software, Park Place Productions. Silicon & Synapse","u":"Interplay Productions","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dvorak_on_Typing"},{"t":"Dyna Blaster","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Hudson Soft","g":"Puzzle, Arcade","r":"February 1, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/dyna-blaster","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/iudfxu9meztn2ozzq0sh.jpg","s":"Dyna Blaster or Dynablaster is a name that has been given to the European releases of the game of the Bomberman. It's a strategic, maze-based video game franchise originally developed by Hudson Soft. \n \nMost games in the Bomberman franchise largely revolve around two modes of play; single player campaigns where the player must defeat enemies and reach an exit to progress through levels, and multiplayer modes where players must attempt to eliminate each other and be the last one standing. Gameplay involves strategically placing down bombs, which explode in multiple directions after a certain amount of time, in order to destroy obstacles and kill enemies and other players. The player can pick up various power-ups, giving them benefits such as larger explosions or the ability to place more bombs down at a time. The player is killed if they touch an enemy or get caught up in a bomb's explosion, including their own, requiring players to be cautious of their own bomb placement."},{"t":"E.S.S. Mega","p":["MS-DOS","Atari ST","Amiga CDTV"],"d":"Coktel Vision","u":"Tomahawk","g":"Simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/E.S.S._Mega"},{"t":"Earl Weaver Baseball II","p":["MS-DOS"],"u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earl_Weaver_Baseball_II"},{"t":"Earth Defense Force","p":["Arcade","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Jaleco","u":"Jaleco","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth_Defense_Force_(video_game)"},{"t":"EcoQuest: The Search for Cetus","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line, Coktel Vision","g":"Educational, adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EcoQuest:_The_Search_for_Cetus"},{"t":"Egypt","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Human Creative","u":"Human","g":"Puzzle","r":"May 31, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egypt_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Eight Man","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"Pallas","u":"SNK","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"June 7, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eight_Man_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7yk5.jpg","s":"Murdered by criminals, Detective Yokoda's body is retrieved by Professor Tani and taken to his laboratory. There, Tani performs an experiment that has failed seven times; Yokoda is the latest subject to have his life force transferred into an android body. For the first time, the experiment succeeds. Yokoda is reborn as the armor-skinned android 8 Man, able to dash at impossible speeds, as well as shape-shift into other people. He shifts himself into Yokoda, this time christening himself as \"Hachiro Azuma\". He keeps this identity a secret, known only to Tani and his police boss Chief Tanaka. Even his girlfriend Sachiko and friend Ichiro do not know he is an android. As 8-Man, Hachiro fights crime (even bringing his murderers to justice). He smoked \"energy\" cigarettes, to rejuvenate his powers, that he carried in a cigarette case on his belt."},{"t":"El Viento","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Wolf Team","u":"Wolf Team, Renovation Products","g":"Platform","r":"September 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_Viento","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co289o.jpg","s":"El Viento is a platform game developed and published by Wolf Team for the Sega Genesis game console in 1991. It is the first in a trilogy of games, which includes El Viento, Earnest Evans and Anett Futatabi"},{"t":"Elendor","p":[],"g":"MUSH","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elendor"},{"t":"Elf","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Ocean Software","u":"Ocean Software","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elf_(video_game)"},{"t":"Eliminator Boat Duel","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sculptured Software, Radioactive Software","u":"Electro Brain, Storm","g":"Racing","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eliminator_Boat_Duel","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co61vi.jpg","s":"Drivers earn thousands of dollars by competing in hydroplane races, setting track records, rescuing stranded people, and collecting cash bonuses. With these winnings they can repair or upgrade various components of the hydroplane to improve racing performance and remain competitive in the increasingly-difficult races. Eliminator Boat Duel offers three difficulty levels: Easy, Normal, and Expert. Completing a race at a lower difficulty level advances the player to the next higher one. At each difficulty level, the player's hydroplane can be damaged from impact with animals, the opponent's hydroplane, and various stationary objects. A false start is penalized with a $2,000 fine. For most of a race, players control their hydroplanes from a bird's-eye view, and the screen scrolls in multiple directions; but in one segment of the race, the graphical perspective changes to a third-person tracking mode. In a single-player game, the player competes first with Seasick Sidney (in Easy mode), with Aquarius Rex (in Normal mode), or with Surfer Bob (in Expert mode). The player then competes against Vicious Vicky, Weird Willy, Mangler Mike, Veronica Alabaster, and Disaster Don (the reigning World Champion), respectively. To win, the player must defeat each opponent one-against-one on three unique race courses. Eliminator Boat Duel has 24 unique race courses."},{"t":"Elite Plus","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Microplay Software","u":"Microplay Software","r":"April 17, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elite_Plus"},{"t":"Elvira: The Arcade Game","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS","Commodore 64"],"d":"Flair Software","u":"Flair Software","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elvira:_The_Arcade_Game"},{"t":"Escape from Colditz","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escape_from_Colditz"},{"t":"Escape Kids","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Racing","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escape_Kids"},{"t":"Eternal Legend","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"JAM","u":"Sega","g":"Role-playing","r":"August 9, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eternal_Legend"},{"t":"Exodus: Journey to the Promised Land","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Boy","MS-DOS"],"d":"Color Dreams","u":"Wisdom Tree","g":"Puzzle/Christian","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exodus:_Journey_to_the_Promised_Land"},{"t":"Extra Innings","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sting","u":"Sony Music Entertainment Japan, Sony Imagesoft","g":"Sports","r":"August 9, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extra_Innings_(video_game)"},{"t":"Eye of the Beholder","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","Sega CD","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Westwood Associates","u":"Strategic Simulations, Inc., Capcom, Sega, Pony Canyon, Inc.","g":"Dungeon crawler","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_of_the_Beholder_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co28s7.jpg","s":"Eye of the Beholder is an all 3-D, Legend Series computer role-playing adventure based on the popular Advanced Dungeons & Dragons 2nd Edition rules and on an original story created for this game . The action takes place in the sewers and catacombs beneath the City of Waterdeep located in TSR's Forgotten Realms game world."},{"t":"Eye of the Beholder II: The Legend of Darkmoon","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga"],"d":"Westwood Associates","u":"Strategic Simulations, Cybille, Capcom","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_of_the_Beholder_II:_The_Legend_of_Darkmoon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co28s8.jpg","s":"Eye of the beholder II: The legend of Darkmoon is an all-3D leisure series computer role-playing adventure based on the popular AD&D 2nd edition game rules and on an original story created for this game. THe action takes place in and around the dreaded temple Darkmoon located in TSR's Forgotten Realms gameworld.\n\nEverything in Eye of the Beholder II is from your point of view. Watch the trees pass by as you move through the forest around the temple. Pick up items and take them with you. Open door with keys, by pressing buttons or by pulling release levers. See monsters draw nearer to you and close in for battle. Attack with the weapons your characters have in their hands. Cas spells with your Cleric's or Paladin's hoily symbols and your mages' spell books. Those in the front of the party may attack with weapons such as swords and maces, while characters in the rear ranks may attack with spells or ranged weapons such as bows and slings."},{"t":"F-117A Nighthawk Stealth Fighter 2.0","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series"],"d":"MPS Labs, Retroism","u":"MicroProse, Nightdive Studios","g":"Flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-117A_Nighthawk_Stealth_Fighter_2.0"},{"t":"F-22 Interceptor","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Lerner Research","u":"Electronic Arts, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Air combat simulation","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-22_Interceptor"},{"t":"F1 Exhaust Note","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Racing","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F1_Exhaust_Note"},{"t":"Factory Panic","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Japan System House","u":"Sega","g":"Action","r":"June 21, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Factory_Panic"},{"t":"Falcon 3.0","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Sphere Inc.","u":"Spectrum HoloByte, MicroProse","g":"Combat flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falcon_3.0"},{"t":"Famicom Jump II: Saikyō no Shichinin","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Chunsoft","u":"Bandai","g":"Action role-playing game","r":"December 2, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Famicom_Jump_II:_Saiky%C5%8D_no_Shichinin"},{"t":"Fantasia","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Infogrames","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantasia_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co47d3.jpg","s":"In the single-player game, the player controls Mickey Mouse as the Sorcerer's Apprentice through various side-scrolling levels in an attempt to collect musical notes that somehow went missing whilst he was asleep. Each of the four levels were based on amalgamations of the segments of Fantasia, each one around the elements: water (The Sorcerer's Apprentice, Dance of the Reed Flutes and Arabian Dance), earth (The Rite of Spring), air (Russian Dance, Pastoral Symphony and Dance of the Hours) and fire (Night on Bald Mountain, Toccata and Fugue in D Minor). The player defeats various enemies by jumping on them or by collecting magical bubbles that could be used to shoot at enemies as projectiles. In each level, the player collects a certain number of hidden magical notes in order for the song to play once again."},{"t":"Fantastic Dizzy","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS","Sega Master System","Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Gear","Amiga"],"d":"Codemasters","u":"Camerica, Codemasters","g":"Adventure, platform","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fantastic_Dizzy"},{"t":"Fascination","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Tomahawk","u":"Coktel Vision, Digital Integration","g":"Graphic adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fascination_(video_game)"},{"t":"Fastest 1","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Human Entertainment","u":"Human Entertainment","g":"Formula One racing simulation","r":"June 28, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fastest_1"},{"t":"Fatal Fury","p":["Neo Geo AES","Arcade"],"d":"SNK, Gaibrain, Takara","u":"D4 Enterprise, Sega, Tec Toy, Magical Company, SNK","g":"Fighting","r":"November 25, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/fatal-fury","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co9lf7.jpg","s":"Fatal Fury is a fighting game developed by SNK and released for arcades in 1991. Players choose from a roster of martial artists and compete in one-on-one battles using punches, kicks, and special moves. The game introduced a two-plane fighting system that allows characters to move between foreground and background lanes. Its story follows Terry Bogard and his allies as they challenge the crime lord Geese Howard."},{"t":"Fatal Fury: King of Fighters","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK, Takara, Sega, Magical Company","g":"Fighting","r":"November 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fatal_Fury:_King_of_Fighters"},{"t":"Fate: Gates of Dawn","p":["Amiga"],"d":"reLINE Software","u":"reLINE Software","g":"Role-playing video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fate:_Gates_of_Dawn"},{"t":"Fighter Duel: Corsair vs. Zero","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Jaeger Software","u":"Jaeger Software","g":"Simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fighter_Duel:_Corsair_vs._Zero"},{"t":"Fighting Masters","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Aicom, ALU","u":"Treco","g":"Fighting","r":"December 6, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fighting_Masters","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5mu4.jpg","s":"A Giant Red Sun will go Nova within hours... 12 Star systems will be destroyed... One contest will decide who, or what, survives... Climb into the interstellar ring! Go One-on-One with another player, or battle for the survival of your civilization! 12 different Alien champions to select! From the snapping, tearing claws of Zygrunt to the bludgeoning ecto-clubs of Rotundo, each warrior has a deadly force all their own!"},{"t":"Final Fantasy Adventure","p":["Game Boy","Mobile phone"],"d":"Square","g":"Action role-playing","r":"June 28, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_Adventure","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1vpk.jpg","s":"In the Empire of Glaive, a gladiator-slave is forced to fight evil monsters controlled by the Dark Lord. But when his best friend is mortally wounded, he learns of a secret pendant which will give him great power. As the hero, you must battle your way out of the Dark Knight's castle and search through a dangerous world for the girl who has the pendant. But you discover she has been captured by the Dark Knight's men. To free her, you must find the legendary sword and fight off the Dark Knight's beasts and overlords through deserts, snowfields, lakes, forests, and caves. Are you ready for the adventure of your life?"},{"t":"Final Fantasy IV","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Square","u":"Square, Square Electronic Arts, Sony Computer Entertainment, Square Enix","g":"Role-playing","r":"July 19, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_IV","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co31c0.jpg","s":"FINAL FANTASY IV is the fourth main installment in the FINAL FANTASY series, developed and published by Squaresoft. It was released in July 1991 for the Super Nintendo Entertainment System in Japan, and released as FINAL FANTASY II in North America in October 1991 with alterations made due to Nintendo of America's guidelines at the time."},{"t":"Final Fantasy Legend III","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Square","u":"Square, Square Enix","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 13, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Fantasy_Legend_III","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1vpm.jpg","s":"Millennia ago, a fierce battle was waged for control of an idyllic world known as Pureland. After much fighting, the brave warriors of Pureland were protectively sealed in their own dimension, away from the rest of the universe. This was done by means of Talon, a powerful space craft capable of traveling through both time and space. Centuries have passed and the would-be master of Pureland have managed to create a rift in the galaxy, sending the Pureland Water Entity to devour all that stands in its path. You must journey through past, present, and future to find the 13 pieces of the now-divided Talon. Only then can you save the universe from total destruction."},{"t":"Final Match Tennis","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Human Entertainment","u":"Human Entertainment","g":"Sports","r":"March 1, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Match_Tennis"},{"t":"Final Soldier","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Now Production","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Vertical-scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Final_Soldier"},{"t":"FireHawk","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Atari ST","Amiga"],"d":"Codemasters","u":"Camerica","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FireHawk_(video_game)"},{"t":"Fireteam 2200","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"SimSystems","u":"SimSystems","g":"Computer wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fireteam_2200"},{"t":"First Samurai","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Vivid Image","u":"Image Works, Ubi Soft, Kemco","g":"Platform, beat 'em up","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Samurai","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2gem.jpg","s":"A 2D action-platforming game that begins in a mystical representation of feudal Japan but travels all over time. It was originally released on European home computers, though Kemco's 1993 SNES port was published worldwide."},{"t":"Flames of Freedom","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Maelstrom Games","u":"Rainbird / MicroProse","g":"First-person shooter, role-playing, simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flames_of_Freedom"},{"t":"Flying Warriors","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Culture Brain","u":"Culture Brain","g":"Action","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flying_Warriors"},{"t":"Formula One Grand Prix","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"MicroProse","u":"MicroProse","g":"Racing simulator","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Formula_One_Grand_Prix_(video_game)"},{"t":"Fortified Zone","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Jaleco","u":"Jaleco","g":"Shooter, action","r":"February 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortified_Zone","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2nxc.jpg","s":"He has the offense. She has the defense."},{"t":"Franchise Football League: Fantasy Football","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franchise_Football_League:_Fantasy_Football"},{"t":"Frankenstein: The Monster Returns","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Bandai America","g":"Action","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frankenstein:_The_Monster_Returns"},{"t":"Free D.C!","p":[],"d":"Cineplay Interactive","u":"Cineplay Interactive","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_D.C!"},{"t":"Full Contact","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Team17","u":"Team17","g":"Fighting","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Full_Contact_(video_game)"},{"t":"Fun House","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS"],"d":"Realtime Associates","u":"Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Action/Shooter","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fun_House_(video_game)"},{"t":"Fuzzical Fighter","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Make Software","u":"Sigma Enterprises","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"May 17, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuzzical_Fighter"},{"t":"G.I. Joe","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"KID","u":"Taxan USA","g":"Action, platform, run and gun","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G.I._Joe_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"G.I. Joe: A Real American Hero","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"KID","u":"Taxan","g":"Shooter, Platform","r":"January 1, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/gi-joe-a-real-american-hero--1","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6nzy.jpg","s":"G.I. Joe is a NES game from 1991 published by Taxan which later spawned a sequel called G.I Joe the Atlantis factor."},{"t":"Galaga '91","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Namco, Now Production","u":"Namco, Sega","g":"Fixed shooter","r":"October 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galaga_'91"},{"t":"Galaxy 5000","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Activision","u":"Activision","g":"Racing game","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galaxy_5000"},{"t":"Game Boy Wars","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Nintendo R&D1, Intelligent Systems","u":"Nintendo","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"May 21, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_Boy_Wars"},{"t":"Ganbare Goemon: Sarawareta Ebisumaru!","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platformer","r":"December 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganbare_Goemon:_Sarawareta_Ebisumaru!"},{"t":"GATE","p":["Apple IIGS","macOS"],"d":"Bright Software","u":"Toolbox, Seven Hills Software","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GATE_(video_game)"},{"t":"Gateway to the Savage Frontier","p":["Commodore 64","Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Beyond Software","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Role-playing, tactical RPG","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gateway_to_the_Savage_Frontier","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6jtp.jpg","s":"As the story opens, you and your companions are celebrating in triumph. The caravan you've been guarding has just completed its dangerous passage to the trading town of Yartar. But the realms are full of danger and there is tension in the air. Great armies are on the move, and the relative peace of recent times will soon be shattered. Your party becomes a problem to the evil forces whose plans lie behind the current troubles. Only by the swiftness of your sword--on land and water--will you live to save the region from certain destruction."},{"t":"Gauntlet III: The Final Quest","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Tengen, Software Creations","u":"U.S. Gold","g":"Hack and slash, dungeon crawl","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauntlet_III:_The_Final_Quest"},{"t":"Gdleen","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gdleen"},{"t":"Gem'X","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Commodore 64"],"d":"Kaiko","u":"Demonware, Global Software","g":"Puzzle","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gem'X"},{"t":"Gemfire","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","PC-8800 series","PC-9800 series","MSX"],"d":"Koei","u":"Koei","g":"Turn-based strategy, role-playing","r":"August 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemfire"},{"t":"Genocide 2","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genocide_2"},{"t":"GG Aleste","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Compile","u":"Compile","g":"Vertically scrolling shooter","r":"December 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GG_Aleste"},{"t":"Ghost Battle","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Interactive Design","u":"Thalion Software","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_Battle"},{"t":"Ghost Pilots","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"January 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_Pilots"},{"t":"Ghox","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Toaplan","u":"Taito","g":"Action","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghox"},{"t":"Glider","p":["macOS"],"u":"Soft Dorothy Software","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glider_(video_game)"},{"t":"Gobliiins","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS","macOS","Windows"],"d":"Coktel Vision","u":"Coktel Vision, Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure, puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gobliiins","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2ibj.jpg","s":"Gobliiins is an adventure game where the player controls a team of three goblins: Oups, Ignatius and Asgard (Hooter, Dwayne, and BoBo in the US release) to find four magic components. A voodoo doll has turned their king Angoulafre mad and by collecting the components they can track down Niak the Wizard, the only one who can put things right. The player needs to solve various puzzles by combining the different skills of the three goblins. It's possible switch between them and control them separately. Most of the puzzles are based on coordinated actions. One can cast powerful but erratic spells, another can collect and use objects, and the last can hit things. Action icons are activated at the bottom of the screen. The game takes in 22 landscapes, each of which has puzzles which must be cleared. The characters have limited energy, which can be sapped by traps."},{"t":"Gods","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Acorn Archimedes","MS-DOS","PC-9800 series","Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"The Bitmap Brothers","u":"Renegade Software, Mindscape","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gods_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coa6vh.jpg","s":"Who would have thought that a man would be so foolish or so daring as to accept a challenge set down by the Gods? And yet one came forward to claim the right to test his skills and strengths against a city so legendary its name alone struck fear in the hearts of all who heard it spoken."},{"t":"Golden Axe II","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"December 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Axe_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2qgu.jpg","s":"The Lord of Darkness, Dark Guld, has arisen again. Wielding the famous Golden Axe, he has destroyed entire countries with his evil clan, forcing the world into chaos. Once again, three brave warriors stand up to face the oppressors, and won’t rest until the Golden Axe is returned to its rightful place."},{"t":"Golden Axe Warrior","p":["Sega Master System"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Action-adventure","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Axe_Warrior","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2b8f.jpg","s":"World peace is at stake! Death Adder, a wicked giant, has stolen the nine crystals from the kingdom of Firewood and has hidden them in nine labyrinths that he has created. These nine magic crystals protected the people from evil – especially from Death Adder's grasp. But now that the crystals are lost, the world is defenseless!\n\nSomeone must put an end to the fiend's realm of darkness, death and destruction! Do you have what it takes to bring back peace and happiness? Trek through deep, dark forests and sail across vast, stormy seas. Chop down trees with your Battle Axe and move rocks with your magic spells to find secret caves. When you take on Death Adder's gruesome monsters, think only of winning – or else you won't find the nine crystals. And when you do, you'll find the Golden Axe – the only weapon that can crush Death Adder!"},{"t":"Goldfish Warning!","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldfish_Warning!"},{"t":"Golf Grand Slam","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Hect, Atlus","g":"Sports","r":"January 31, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golf_Grand_Slam_(video_game)"},{"t":"Golly! Ghost!","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Light gun shooter","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golly!_Ghost!"},{"t":"Gorby no Pipeline Daisakusen","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","MSX","FM Towns"],"d":"Compile","u":"Tokuma Shoten","g":"Puzzle","r":"April 12, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gorby_no_Pipeline_Daisakusen"},{"t":"Gorillas","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Microsoft","u":"Microsoft","g":"Artillery","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gorillas_(video_game)"},{"t":"Gradius: The Interstellar Assault","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"August 9, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gradius:_The_Interstellar_Assault","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7i5m.jpg","s":"Gradius: The Interstellar Assault retains the traditional horizontal scrolling gameplay from the Gradius series. Once again the player takes control of the Vic Viper and flies through five different stages destroying Bacterion's army.\n\nThe game retains the traditional power-up bar from the original Gradius. The player can speed-up multiple times, use missiles, shoot double firepower or lasers, use several options at a time and use the classic shield (although it is referred to as a forcefield). However, before each game is started or continued, the player is given a \"Weapon Select\" screen. Here the player can choose between one of three settings for the missiles, double firepower, and lasers."},{"t":"Growl","p":["Arcade","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Taito, I.T.L Co., Ltd.","u":"Taito","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Growl_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2eyd.jpg","s":"The player begins the game by choosing from one of four different forest rangers, each with a different amount of health, attack strength, and jumping height. The game's controls consists of an eight-way joystick for moving the character and two action buttons (attack and jump). The player can perform a variety of different attacks (punches, kicks, and finishing blows) depending on the position of an enemy. By pressing both buttons while surrounded by enemies, the player can perform a special attack that strikes every enemy around him. By pressing both buttons while holding the joystick upwards, the player will perform a longer jump.\n\nThe player can procure weapons by destroying barrels and wooden crates or disarming certain enemies. There are a total of eight weapons which can be obtained: three melee weapons (a pipe, a sword and a whip), two throwing weapons (knives and hand grenades), and three firearms (a pistol, a machine gun, and a rocket launcher). The barrels and crates can also be picked up and thrown at enemies as well. When the machine gun or rocket launcher runs out of ammunition, these are still wielded by the player as melee weapons. The pistol, on the other hand, is thrown by the player after all of its bullets are used. The player can drop his current weapon by pressing down and attack while wielding it. If a weapon lies on the ground after a certain period, it will vanish completely.\n\nThere are seven regular stages (called rounds) and a bonus game, for a total of eight stages. The locations include a city, a moving train, a boat, a jungle, a cavern, and the hideout of the poachers. There are six types of enemy characters throughout the game, excluding the final boss (who has two forms). There are also animal helpers that will help out the player after they're rescued from a poacher, such as an eagle, a herd of deer, and an elephant."},{"t":"Gun Frontier","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gun_Frontier_(video_game)"},{"t":"GunForce","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Irem, Bits Studios","u":"Irem","g":"Run and gun","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GunForce","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co30ri.jpg","s":"GunForce: Battle Fire Engulfed Terror Island or simply titled as GunForce, is a side-scrolling action shooting game produced by Irem for the arcades in 1991. The game was later ported by Bits Studios and published by Irem to the Super Famicom exclusively in Japan in 1992.\n\nOutside of Japan, it later received a sequel titled GunForce II, originally known in Japan as Geo Storm.\n\nThe player is armed with a gun that fires rapid-fire bullets. Each direction it shoots can be fixed toward it so the player doesn't have to hold the joystick toward it. Players may find motorcycles to speed across enemy territory faster in addition to helicopters and cable cars. After scoring over any high score, whether they win or lose, players can enter their name into the high score list after the game over screen is \"achieved\". A strict time limit keeps the game going at a steady pace throughout; the consequence involves losing a life."},{"t":"Gunship 2000","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"MicroProse","u":"MicroProse","g":"Air combat simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunship_2000"},{"t":"GX Games","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GX_Games"},{"t":"Gynoug","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"NCS Corporation","u":"Masaya, DreamWorks, Sega","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"January 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gynoug"},{"t":"Hacha Mecha Fighter","p":["Arcade"],"d":"NMK","u":"NMK","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hacha_Mecha_Fighter"},{"t":"Hal's Hole in One Golf","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"HAL Laboratory","u":"HAL Laboratory","g":"Sports","r":"February 23, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hal's_Hole_in_One_Golf"},{"t":"Hana Tāka Daka!?","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Taito/Natsume","u":"Taito","g":"Horizontal-scrolling shooter","r":"August 9, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hana_T%C4%81ka_Daka!%3F"},{"t":"Hare Raising Havoc","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"BlueSky Software","u":"Disney Software","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hare_Raising_Havoc"},{"t":"Hat Trick Hero","p":["Arcade","Amiga","Atari ST","Commodore 64","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Sports","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hat_Trick_Hero"},{"t":"Head Buster","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"NCS Corporation, Winds Co.","u":"Masaya","g":"Strategy involving breeding/constructing","r":"March 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Head_Buster"},{"t":"Headline Harry and the Great Paper Race","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Davidson & Associates","u":"Davidson & Associates","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Headline_Harry_and_the_Great_Paper_Race"},{"t":"Heart of China","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heart_of_China","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cobj5x.jpg","s":"Kate, the daughter of a wealthy businessman named E.A. Lomax, is abducted by a Chinese warlord named Li Deng near Chengdu, where she was volunteering as a nurse. Lomax recruits the former World War I fighter pilot Jake \"Lucky\" Masters for a dangerous mission: travel to Hong-Kong, locate a mysterious ninja, and find a way to infiltrate Li Deng's fortress and rescue Kate.\n\nHeart of China is an adventure game with a romantic theme somewhat reminiscent of films such as Raiders of the Lost Ark. The game is set during the 1930's in China as well as a few other locations. It uses a simple point-and-click system for interaction and object manipulation, and is very similar visually and gameplay-wise to Rise of the Dragon.\n\nSome of the tasks in the game have multiple solutions, allowing the player to pursue different methods and choose different responses in branching dialogues. However, some choices may lead to the protagonist's death or bring the investigation to a dead end. There are also a few arcade sequences, which can be skipped if the player fails to complete them after several tries. The game utilizes digitized photos of live actors superimposed on hand-painted graphics."},{"t":"Heimdall","p":["Amiga","Acorn Archimedes"],"d":"The 8th Day","u":"Core Design, Virgin Games","g":"Action role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heimdall_(video_game)"},{"t":"Hellcats over the Pacific","p":["macOS"],"d":"Parsoft Interactive","u":"Graphic Simulations","g":"Combat flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hellcats_over_the_Pacific"},{"t":"Heroes of the 357th","p":["MS-DOS"],"u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Combat flight simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heroes_of_the_357th"},{"t":"HeroQuest","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","MS-DOS","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Gremlin Interactive","u":"Gremlin Interactive","g":"Turn-based tactics","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HeroQuest_(video_game)"},{"t":"High Speed","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Rare","u":"Tradewest","g":"Simulation","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High_Speed_(video_game)"},{"t":"Highway Patrol 2","p":["Atari ST","Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Microids","u":"Microids","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Highway_Patrol_2"},{"t":"Home Alone","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy","Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Bethesda Softworks, Brian A. Rice, Inc., Imagineering Inc., Probe Software","u":"THQ, Altron, Sega, Capstone Software","g":"Action","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Home_Alone_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5x09.jpg","s":"Harry and Marv, the bumbling \"Wet Bandits,\" have paid their debt to society and are now ready to get revenge on the youngster that caused them their humiliating defeat Kevin McCallister. They have arranged for Kevin's family to be out of the house when they come calling again, leaving him home alone for their rematch. This time, the sneaky thieves have beefed up their gang with robbers and crooks that are more than eager to help themselves to the McCallister valuables. Only the resourceful Kevin stands between the new Wet Bandit gang and his family's fortune."},{"t":"Hot Slots","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Idea-Tek","u":"Hacker International/Panesian","g":"Simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Slots"},{"t":"House of Tarot","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Japan System Supply","u":"Sega","g":"Non-game","r":"March 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/House_of_Tarot"},{"t":"Hovertank One","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"id Software","u":"Softdisk","g":"Vehicular combat, first-person shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hovertank_One"},{"t":"Hugo II, Whodunit?","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Gray Design Associates","u":"Gray Design Associates","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugo_II,_Whodunit%3F"},{"t":"Hunter","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Activision","u":"Activision","g":"Action-adventure","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunter_(video_game)"},{"t":"Hyperspeed","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"MPS Labs","u":"MicroProse","g":"Space combat, role-playing video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperspeed"},{"t":"HyperZone","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"HAL Laboratory","u":"HAL Laboratory","g":"Rail shooter","r":"August 31, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HyperZone"},{"t":"Illusion City","p":["MSX"],"d":"Microcabin","u":"Microcabin","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 14, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illusion_City"},{"t":"Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Software Creations","u":"Taito America","g":"Action","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indiana_Jones_and_the_Last_Crusade_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4qtz.jpg","s":"Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade is a video game for the NES based on the 1989 action and adventure movie of the same name. In the game, the player controls protagonist Indiana Jones, going through levels taken directly from the film, albeit with a shortened and simplified plot."},{"t":"Inindo: Way of the Ninja","p":["PC-8800 series","PC-9800 series","MSX","FM Towns","Sharp X68000","MS-DOS","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Windows"],"d":"Koei","u":"Koei","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inindo:_Way_of_the_Ninja","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2x8i.jpg","s":"\"Inindo: Way of the Ninja\" is a console-style RPG with a touch of strategy. You navigate your hero on the world map, visit towns, and descend into dungeons. The battles are turn-based and are viewed from a third-person perspective, like in Final Fantasy games. You can move on the battle field and use melee or long-ranged attacks. You'll be able to recruit characters of various classes to your party, who will join you for profit or for other reasons. After you gain enough experience, you'll also be able to talk to daimyos (feudal rulers) of various castles and to convince them to work together against Nobunaga. You can also perform various tasks for the daimyos, such as spying.\n\n1581, Japan. Fearing a rebellion and striving to conquer the entire country, the warlock Nobunaga orders to attack the Iga ninja clan. The ninjas bravely defend their village, but, being vastly outnumbered, are mercilessly slain by Nobunaga's men. Only one survived: you, the hero of the game. Shortly before the assault you were sent to a nearby village to warn the elder of Nobunaga's attack. However, the other village is also destroyed by Nobunaga's army. You swear to avenge his crime. But for now, you are just an inexperienced young men, and are \"wanted\" by Nobunaga's men and ruthless bounty hunters. Will you be able to unite Japan in the ultimate struggle against Nobunaga?"},{"t":"Isolated Warrior","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"KID","u":"VAP, NTVIC, Nintendo","g":"Action, scrolling shooter","r":"February 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isolated_Warrior"},{"t":"Itadaki Street","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Login Soft","u":"ASCII Corporation","g":"Board game","r":"March 21, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Itadaki_Street_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"J. R. R. Tolkien's Riders of Rohan","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Beam Software, Papyrus Design Group","u":"Personal Software Services, Konami, Mirrorsoft","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J._R._R._Tolkien's_Riders_of_Rohan"},{"t":"James Bond Jr.","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Bond_Jr."},{"t":"James Pond 2","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Genesis / Mega Drive","Commodore 64","Acorn Archimedes","MS-DOS","Game Gear","Game Boy","Sega Master System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Vectordean, Intellectual Software Consultants Limited","u":"Millennium Interactive, Electronic Arts, U.S. Gold, Play It","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Pond_2"},{"t":"James Pond 2: Codename - RoboCod","p":["Amiga","Genesis / Mega Drive","Atari ST"],"d":"Vectordean","u":"Electronic Arts, Tec Toy, Ocean Software, Eclipse Productions, Millennium Interactive, Electronic Arts Victor, American Softworks Corporation, Victor Entertainment","g":"Platform","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/james-pond-2-codename-robocod","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co84wq.jpg","s":"In his second adventure James Pond must retrieve the toys Dr Maybe has stolen. Pond has been armed with an Inspector Gadget-style stretch device, which he can use to view higher areas or claw onto ceilings so as to slide across them.\n\nThe gameplay takes place across worlds themed around particular types of toys, such as sporting goods, candy and aircraft. The levels scroll sideways, although a small amount of vertical movement is included. On each level Pond must collect 2 penguins and reach the exit, although there are usually multiple exits and lots of secret areas to explore. After completing each pair of two worlds (each of which has three sub levels), a boss must be faced."},{"t":"Jankenman","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jankenman"},{"t":"Jewel Master","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Action-adventure","r":"August 30, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jewel_Master"},{"t":"Jimmy White's 'Whirlwind' Snooker","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"u":"Virgin Games","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jimmy_White's_'Whirlwind'_Snooker"},{"t":"Joe & Mac","p":["Arcade","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS"],"d":"Data East","u":"Data East, Elite Systems, New World Computing, Takara","g":"Platform","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_&_Mac","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coahfs.jpg","s":"The Super NES version of Joe & Mac is a reworked game which features an overworld map used to choose the levels (unlike in other versions where all of them have to be played), which were longer, plus some bonus stages (either in the levels or out in the world map) and a different final boss."},{"t":"John Madden Football '92","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Park Place Productions","u":"Electronic Arts, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Sports","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Madden_Football_'92"},{"t":"John Madden Football II","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"D.C. True, Ltd.","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Madden_Football_II"},{"t":"Jones in the Fast Lane","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Social simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jones_in_the_Fast_Lane","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5v8n.jpg","s":"Jones in the Fast Lane is a life simulation game developed and published by Sierra Entertainment in 1990. The objective of the game is to attain certain amounts of money, happiness, status, and education. The exact amounts needed are defined by the player(s) when the game begins. The game's name and goals are a play on the concept of keeping up with the Joneses. As of 2006, the game was unofficially ported to Adobe Flash."},{"t":"Judge Dredd","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Random Access","u":"Virgin Mastertronic","g":"Platform, shoot 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judge_Dredd_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Just Another War in Space","p":["Atari ST"],"u":"Azeroth Publishing","g":"Computer wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Just_Another_War_in_Space"},{"t":"Karate Blazers","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Video System","u":"Tecmo, McO'River","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karate_Blazers"},{"t":"Kero Kero Keroppi no Daibouken","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kero_Kero_Keroppi_no_Daibouken"},{"t":"Kid Icarus: Of Myths and Monsters","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Nintendo R&D1, Tose","u":"Nintendo","g":"Action, platform","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kid_Icarus:_Of_Myths_and_Monsters","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5v48.jpg","s":"A lighthearted adventure with mythical challenges. The \"8-bit Summer\" series continues with this classic action game from 1991. Angel Land is up to its halos in woes as mythical monsters are running rampant. Pit, the original Kid Icarus, must save the realm again. Armed with his trusty bow, he'll zap the bad guys and find the Three Sacred Treasures hidden by Palutena. Only after this grueling training will he be strong enough to take on the dark forces behind Angel Land's troubles. Pit will encounter old friends and new enemies - from helpful Centurions to the dreaded Eggplant Wizard - as he journeys from the depths of the underworld to the towers of the sky palace. Find hammers to shatter special walls and reveal helpful items. Collect hearts by fighting off Pit's foes. Train hard and battle harder. Pit's challenges make the labors of Hercules look like a walk in the park."},{"t":"Killerball","p":["Atari ST","Amiga","Amstrad CPC","MS-DOS"],"d":"Microids","u":"Microids","g":"Action, sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killerball"},{"t":"King of Kings: The Early Years","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_Kings:_The_Early_Years"},{"t":"King of the Monsters","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK, Takara, Sega","g":"Fighting","r":"February 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_of_the_Monsters_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co26nd.jpg","s":"Players get to choose any one of six monsters (four in the 16-bit ports) for battle, and two players can join forces to fight the monsters together. Battles end when one of the monsters is pinned for a three count or if time expires (in which case the player loses).\n\nThe game consists of 12 total levels: the player first must defeat all six monsters, with the last monster being oneself, but in a different palette. Then the player must defeat the six monsters again, in the same order, but this time in different cities"},{"t":"Kingdom Crusade","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Sculptured Software","u":"Electro Brain, Ocean Software","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_Crusade"},{"t":"Knightmare","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Mindscape International Ltd.","u":"Mindscape International Ltd.","g":"Role-playing video game, Dungeon crawler","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knightmare_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Knights of the Round","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Beat 'em up, action role-playing","r":"November 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knights_of_the_Round_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7k33.jpg","s":"The gameplay is very similar to other Capcom beat 'em ups, such as Final Fight and Capcom's previous hack-and-slash fantasy title The King of Dragons. There are seven stages, each with its own boss and a variety of generic enemies that try to stop the players' progress. The fights rely strongly on blocking ability, which is triggered by pressing the attack button, then pressing the joystick away as the opponent strikes. If successful, the player gains a few seconds of invincibility with which to counter-attack. However, if no one strikes the player's character while holding the block, they will tire and drop their guard, making them vulnerable to attack. Blocking is vital in certain cases, since some bosses will be vulnerable only after their attack has been blocked by the player.\n\nAs in most beat 'em ups, a desperation attack is performed by pressing both the attack and jump buttons simultaneously. This knocks out most enemies on the screen, but the player loses a little bit of health every time he uses it."},{"t":"Knights of Xentar","p":["MS-DOS","PC-9800 series","FM Towns","PC Engine CD"],"d":"ELF Corporation, NEC Avenue","u":"Megatech Software","g":"RPG","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knights_of_Xentar","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cob2rb.jpg","s":"Dragon Knight III is an eroge role-playing video game released on many Japanese PCs in 1991. It is part of the Dragon Knight series of games created by Japanese game developer ELF, who originally released the game for the NEC PC-9801 computer in 1991, followed by ports for the Sharp X68000 and PC Engine CD platforms.\n\nThe game is a sequel of Dragon Knight and Dragon Knight II. Its gameplay system is different from that of previous installments of the Dragon Knight series, resembling this of the early Final Fantasy (even more in the PC version) series instead of first-person-view dungeon crawler, and enabling the player to explore the entire world of the game. Some of the characters were renamed in the English and German localized Knights of Xentar release, including the protagonist Takeru's name changed to Desmond."},{"t":"Koutetsu no Kishi","p":["PC-9800 series"],"d":"General Support, Dual","u":"General Support, Asmik Ace Entertainment","g":"Strategy","r":"January 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koutetsu_no_Kishi"},{"t":"Kyatto Ninden Teyandee","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tecmo","u":"Tecmo","g":"Platform, Adventure","r":"July 19, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/kyatto-ninden-teyandee","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4v3t.jpg","s":"Players take the role of the three main cats and otasuke (rescue team) members, who can be switched to at any time and have special abilities to progress through the game. The game features most of the characters in the series as well as an additional villain, a mysterious scientist named Dr. Purple (Dr. パープ)."},{"t":"L-Dis","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Dual Corporation","u":"Masaya","g":"Horizontal-scrolling shooter","r":"April 5, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L-Dis"},{"t":"La malédiction","p":["Amstrad CPC"],"d":"Lankhor","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_mal%C3%A9diction"},{"t":"Lagrange Point","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Role-playing game","r":"April 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagrange_Point_(video_game)"},{"t":"Laser Invasion","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Shooter","r":"March 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laser_Invasion"},{"t":"Last Ninja 3","p":["Commodore 64","Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"System 3","u":"Commodore Gaming, Kixx","g":"Action-adventure","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last_Ninja_3","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1p6r.jpg","s":"Real hatred is timeless.\n\nNinja 3 is here and ready to explode onto your screen with the most glorious display of graphics and animated fight sequences ever seen in this type of game.\n\nWith a massive introduction sequence, incredibly detailed '3D' background graphics and a more than generous helping of all-out action, Ninja 3 displays a degree of excellence never before experienced by even the most seasoned Ninja fan. Ninja 3- A game of epic proportions.\n\nFeel the realism, allow yourself to witness the magical world of Ninjitsu spiritualism, experience the mystical atmosphere of the Tibetan Temples. Remember, always remain in control of your stealth and cunning as you do battle once again with the most treacherous purveyor of evil, Kunitoki."},{"t":"Leander","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Traveller's Tales","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leander_(video_game)"},{"t":"Legend of Success Joe","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"Wave Corporation","u":"SNK","g":"Beat 'em up, fighting, sports","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legend_of_Success_Joe"},{"t":"Leisure Suit Larry 1: In the Land of the Lounge Lizards","p":["Windows","Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Point-and-click, Adventure","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/leisure-suit-larry-1-in-the-land-of-the-lounge-lizards","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5xpx.jpg","s":"A remake of the original Leisure Suit Larry in the Land of the Lounge Lizards featuring redrawn, VGA graphics and a point-and-click interface using Sierra's Creative Interpreter (SCI) engine."},{"t":"Leisure Suit Larry 5: Passionate Patti Does a Little Undercover Work","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","macOS","Windows"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure game","r":"September 7, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leisure_Suit_Larry_5:_Passionate_Patti_Does_a_Little_Undercover_Work","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2kch.jpg","s":"Passionate Patti Does a Little Undercover Work! is (despite the number) the fourth game in Al Lowe's Leisure Suit Larry series. The middle-aged would-be-womanizer Larry Laffer fell off a boat during a cruise and sustained amnesia, forgetting how he and his sweetheart Patti got separated, how Larry got a job in LA, how Patti got a job with the FBI, and what happened in (the never released) Larry 4. Now Larry and Patti are working independently on two cases that are connected to each other, even though the heroes aren't aware of that. Larry's new bosses are involved in shady business, while Patti agrees to take a break from her career as a performing pianist and become an undercover agent. Will the two be together ever again?"},{"t":"Leisure Suit Larry in the Land of the Lounge Lizards","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","Apple II","Apple IIGS","macOS","Atari ST","TRS-80"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leisure_Suit_Larry_in_the_Land_of_the_Lounge_Lizards"},{"t":"Lemmings","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","macOS","Atari ST","ZX Spectrum","Acorn Archimedes","PC-9800 series","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"DMA Design, Sunsoft, Probe Software, Psygnosis","u":"Psygnosis, Sunsoft (SNES, SMD, NES), Sega, Atari Corporation","g":"Puzzle, strategy","r":"February 14, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemmings_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co25u8.jpg","s":"Lemmings is a puzzle-platform game developed by DMA Design and published by Psygnosis in 1991. Players guide a group of lemmings through hazardous levels to a designated exit. The gameplay involves assigning specific skills, such as digging or building, to overcome obstacles and avoid traps. Each level has a time limit and requires saving a minimum number of lemmings to progress. The game was released on multiple platforms and is regarded as a classic of the puzzle genre."},{"t":"Les Manley in: Lost in L.A.","p":["MS-DOS"],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Manley_in:_Lost_in_L.A."},{"t":"Lethal Xcess","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"X-Troll, Gigabyte Crew","u":"Eclipse Software","g":"Shoot 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lethal_Xcess"},{"t":"Lexi-Cross","p":["MS-DOS"],"u":"Interplay Productions","g":"Word","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lexi-Cross"},{"t":"Lightning Swords","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Nanao Corporation","u":"Irem","g":"Hack and slash, beat 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lightning_Swords"},{"t":"Llamatron","p":["Atari ST","Amiga"],"u":"Llamasoft","g":"Multidirectional shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Llamatron"},{"t":"Lord Monarch","p":["PC-9800 series","FM Towns","Windows"],"d":"Nihon Falcom, AIM","u":"Epoch Co., Ltd.","g":"Real-time strategy wargame","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lord_Monarch"},{"t":"Lost Admiral Returns","p":["Windows"],"d":"Fogstone","g":"Turn-based strategy","r":"January 18, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lost_Admiral_Returns"},{"t":"Lotus Turbo Challenge 2","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lotus_Turbo_Challenge_2","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2jmy.jpg","s":"The second Lotus game shifted focus to arcade-oriented gameplay. Being the first of the series released for a game console (for Sega Genesis/Mega Drive under the title Lotus Turbo Challenge), the fuel limit and difficulty levels were dropped, and the lap-based levels were replaced with course-based time trials, (not unlike arcade games such as Out Run), with the player required to complete each course within a specified time to qualify for the next one.\n\nIn addition to the Esprit Turbo SE, Lotus Turbo Challenge 2 also featured the Lotus Elan SE, hence 'Esprit' being dropped from the title. There was no choice of car however; the player runs with the Elan SE on odd levels, and with the Esprit on even levels.\n\nLotus 2's single-player mode uses all of the game screen instead of half, and opponent cars appear in a variety of colors (opponent cars in the original game were all white). However, music is absent from racing altogether; the player instead hears the car's engine sound.\n\nLotus 2 was developed for the Amiga and then converted for the other platforms."},{"t":"Lyriq Crosswords","p":["Windows","macOS"],"d":"Lyriq International","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lyriq_Crosswords"},{"t":"Mad TV","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","Atari ST"],"d":"Rainbow Arts","u":"Rainbow Arts","g":"Business simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mad_TV_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co8628.jpg","s":"Mad TV is a television station management simulation computer game produced in 1991 by Rainbow Arts. It is loosely based on Mad Magazine, and the game's introduction animation features the magazine's mascot, Alfred E. Neuman, smiling on a television. The game puts the player in the role of a new program director for a TV station. The player is in charge of selecting programming and earning advertising for the station, while simultaneously trying to marry Betty, an attractive woman working in the building."},{"t":"Magic Darts","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"SETA Corporation","u":"SETA Corporation, Romstar","g":"Sports","r":"April 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magic_Darts"},{"t":"Magic Pockets","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Acorn Archimedes","MS-DOS"],"d":"Bitmap Brothers","u":"Renegade","g":"Platform","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magic_Pockets","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7wev.jpg","s":"Magic Pockets is a platform game developed by the Bitmap Brothers and published by Renegade in October 1991. It was released for the Atari ST, Amiga, Acorn Archimedes and PC. The title track of the game is the instrumental version of a hit single \"Doin' The Do\", by Betty Boo, originally released in 1990 on the Rhythm King label."},{"t":"Magical Chase","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Quest Corporation","u":"Palsoft, Turbo Technologies Inc., Bothtec, Microcabin","g":"Horizontal-scrolling shooter","r":"November 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magical_Chase"},{"t":"Magician","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Eurocom","u":"Taxan","g":"Action role-playing game","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magician_(video_game)"},{"t":"Main Battle Tank: Middle East","p":[],"u":"Simulations Canada","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_Battle_Tank:_Middle_East"},{"t":"Mamono Hunter Yōko: Dai 7 no Keishō","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Klon","u":"Masaya","g":"Platform game","r":"March 22, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mamono_Hunter_Y%C5%8Dko:_Dai_7_no_Keish%C5%8D"},{"t":"Manchester United Europe","p":["Amiga","Acorn Archimedes","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Krisalis Software","u":"Krisalis Software","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manchester_United_Europe"},{"t":"Mario Andretti's Racing Challenge","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Distinctive Software","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mario_Andretti's_Racing_Challenge"},{"t":"Martian Memorandum","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Access Software","u":"Access Software","g":"Adventure","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martian_Memorandum"},{"t":"Medieval Lords: Soldier Kings of Europe","p":["Commodore 64","MS-DOS"],"d":"Strategic Simulations","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medieval_Lords:_Soldier_Kings_of_Europe"},{"t":"Medieval Warriors","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Merit Software","u":"Merit Software","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medieval_Warriors"},{"t":"Mega Man 4","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom, Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"December 6, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_4","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5ssy.jpg","s":"For a year the city has been quiet, but a new robotic terror has gripped the city! That scheming scientist, Dr. Cossack has arrived in town with eight new metal maniacs who are bigger and badder than anything Dr. Wily dreamed of. It's going to be a cybernetic showdown as the streets of the city erupt with the sizzling sounds of molten metal! Armed with the new Mega Buster, Mega Man runs, jumps and dodges his way through mazes of metallic munchkins on his way to the Siberian citadel of Dr. Cossack for the final cataclysmic clash!"},{"t":"Mega Man II","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Thinking Rabbit","u":"Capcom, Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man_II_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5572.jpg","s":"The second handheld installment of the Mega Man series has him (along with his dog, Rush) defeating eight more revived Robot Masters (from Mega Man 2 and Mega Man 3), while finding the whereabouts of Dr. Wily (who has traveled into the future to steal a weapon to counter Mega Man)."},{"t":"Mega Man: Dr. Wily's Revenge","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Minakuchi Engineering","u":"Capcom, Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"July 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega_Man:_Dr._Wily's_Revenge","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co55bv.jpg","s":"Dr. Wily's most challenging robot masters will be there when you pump up the GameBoy. And knowing the maniac he is, he's probably concocted a new one or two. Can you survive the eight empires before you break into Dr. Wily's lab? Well, if you experienced MegaMan before, you know it's no day at the beach. But good reason to get going on MegaMan in Dr. Wily's Revenge. Now.\n\n* When the stompin' cyclops springs into action, you better step on it.\n* The swooping copterheads just might be your greatest challenge yet.\n* If the giant fan is blowing, only a ledge-jump will save you from the spikes."},{"t":"Mega-Lo-Mania","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","MS-DOS","Sharp X68000","PC-9800 series","FM Towns"],"d":"Sensible Software","u":"Image Works, Ubi Soft, Virgin Games, CRI","g":"Real-time strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega-Lo-Mania"},{"t":"Megafortress","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga"],"d":"Artech Digital Entertainment","u":"Three-Sixty Pacific","g":"Simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megafortress"},{"t":"Megalit","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"ASCII Corporation","u":"Takara, Asmik Ace Entertainment","g":"Strategy/Puzzle","r":"August 9, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megalit"},{"t":"MegaTraveller 2: Quest for the Ancients","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga"],"d":"Paragon Software","u":"Paragon Software","g":"Role-playing video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MegaTraveller_2:_Quest_for_the_Ancients"},{"t":"Metal Black","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Black_(video_game)"},{"t":"Metal Masters","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Bit Managers, Incal","u":"Electro Brain, Infogrames","g":"Fighting","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Masters"},{"t":"Metal Max","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Crea-Tech, Data East, Kuusoukagaku","u":"Data East","g":"Role-playing video game","r":"May 24, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Max_(video_game)"},{"t":"Metal Mutant","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Silmarils","u":"Silmarils","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Mutant"},{"t":"Metal Slader Glory","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"HAL Laboratory","u":"HAL Laboratory","g":"Adventure","r":"August 30, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Slader_Glory"},{"t":"Metal Stoker","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Sankindo","u":"Face","g":"Multidirectional shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Stoker"},{"t":"Metal Storm","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tamtex","u":"Irem","g":"Action, platform","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metal_Storm_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5pj9.jpg","s":"Battle station cyberg's massive computer that keeps peace throughout the solar system is no longer user friendly. It's gone wildly out of control. The LaserGun it operates has already destroyed Neptune. Earth is its next target. As a high-tech hot shot, you must enter Cyber's core and activate the system's self-destruct mechanism. Mankind is reyling on your know-how and toughness to see this thing through. But time is running out..."},{"t":"Metroid II: Return of Samus","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Nintendo R&D1","u":"Nintendo","g":"Action-adventure","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metroid_II:_Return_of_Samus","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1wwx.jpg","s":"A Sinister Planet Threatens the Galaxy!\n\nBattle through a sprawling underworld, seeking out every last Metroid, and build up your arsenal by discovering mysterious artefacts.\n\nEnjoy new abilities like the Spider Ball, and powerful new weapons like the Plasma Beam, as you attempt to eradicate the Metroids once and for all."},{"t":"Mickey's Dangerous Chase","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Now Production","u":"Capcom, Kemco, Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"May 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mickey's_Dangerous_Chase"},{"t":"Micro Machines","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Codemasters, Big Red Software, Merit Studios, Paragon 5","u":"Camerica, Codemasters, Ocean Software","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Micro_Machines_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3b7a.jpg","s":"Micro Machines is a classic racing game. Simple, fast-paced and lots of fun. You are given the control of miniature cars, choppers, speedboats, racecars, tanks, jeeps, formulas and many more vehicles to choose and about 20 different tracks to drive on. You'll have to race AI opponents or your friends on various locations such as kitchen desks, pool tables, workshops, school desks, gardens, bathtubs etc."},{"t":"MicroLeague Baseball: The Manager's Challenge","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"MicroLeague Sports Association","u":"MicroLeague Sports Association","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MicroLeague_Baseball:_The_Manager's_Challenge"},{"t":"MicroProse Golf","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"The Thought Train","u":"MicroProse","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MicroProse_Golf"},{"t":"Microsoft FreeCell","p":["Windows"],"g":"Strategy, Turn-based strategy (TBS), Card & Board Game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_FreeCell","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7mii.jpg","s":"Microsoft FreeCell is a computer game included in Microsoft Windows, based on a card game with the same name. Microsoft has included a FreeCell computer game with every release of the Windows operating system since 1995, greatly contributing to the game's popularity among users of personal computers, even leading to the creation of several websites devoted to FreeCell."},{"t":"MiG-29M Super Fulcrum","p":["Acorn Archimedes","Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"u":"Domark","g":"Combat flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MiG-29M_Super_Fulcrum"},{"t":"Might and Magic III: Isles of Terra","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","macOS","FM Towns","PC-9800 series","Sega CD","PC Engine CD","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"New World Computing","u":"New World Computing","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Might_and_Magic_III:_Isles_of_Terra","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2zhb.jpg","s":"The third installment of the roleplaying series, with a multi-faceted plot full of twists and turns. Ease of use was central to the gameplay and interface, and the plot included piles of new twists and turns.\n\nLead a party of adventurers to rendezvous with the legendary wizard Corak. From dark moldy dungeons, through corridors of hewn stone and timber, and onward to the lofty frozen peaks of Terra you will uncover the mysteries that surround the exotic islands in the Great Sea."},{"t":"Mike Ditka Ultimate Football","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Accolade","u":"Accolade","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Ditka_Ultimate_Football"},{"t":"Mikeneko Homuzu no Kishido","p":["Game Boy"],"u":"Ask Kodansha","g":"Adventure","r":"February 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mikeneko_Homuzu_no_Kishido"},{"t":"Mission: Impossible","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Distinctive Software","u":"Konami","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mission:_Impossible_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Mixed-Up Fairy Tales","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure, Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mixed-Up_Fairy_Tales"},{"t":"Mokowe","p":["Amstrad CPC"],"u":"Lankhor","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mokowe_(video_game)"},{"t":"Monkey Island 2: LeChuck's Revenge","p":["Amiga","FM Towns","macOS","MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"LucasArts","u":"LucasArts, U.S. Gold","g":"Graphic adventure","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monkey_Island_2:_LeChuck's_Revenge","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co30yr.jpg","s":"Monkey Island 2: LeChuck's Revenge is a point-and-click graphic adventure and the second entry in the Monkey Island series. Players guide the pirate Guybrush Threepwood through a comedic Caribbean setting, using a verb-based command interface and an inventory of items to explore locations, talk with characters, and solve puzzles. The game offers a choice of puzzle difficulty at the start, letting players pick a standard experience or a streamlined one."},{"t":"Monopoly","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Sculptured Software","u":"Parker Brothers, Majesco","g":"Strategy, board game","r":"May 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monopoly_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3sp1.jpg","s":"Monopoly is a video game based on the board game Monopoly, released on Genesis, NES, and SNES. Developed by Sculptured Software and published by Parker Brothers. This title was one of many inspired by the property."},{"t":"Monster Business","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Eclipse Software Design","u":"Ascon","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monster_Business"},{"t":"Monster Truck Rally","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Realtime Associates","u":"INTV Corp.","g":"Off-road racing","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monster_Truck_Rally_(video_game)"},{"t":"Monuments of Mars","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Scenario Software","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Puzzle-platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monuments_of_Mars"},{"t":"Moonstone: A Hard Days Knight","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Rob Anderson","u":"Mindscape","g":"Action role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonstone:_A_Hard_Days_Knight","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4xmr.jpg","s":"Moonstone: A Hard Days Knight, is a video game developed and published by Mindscape in 1991. It was released on the Amiga and one year later converted to MS-DOS with alternate sound and music. The title is a play on A Hard Day's Night. Moonstone's gameplay blends several different genres, including strategy, real time fighting and role-playing video games. It allows up to four players to participate in a basic turn based Role Playing Game with real time combat for any encounters. It is also notable as one of the goriest games of its time, with some exceptionally bloody fight and death scenes."},{"t":"Ms. Pac-Man","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"General Computer Corporation (GCC)","u":"Tengen","g":"Puzzle, Arcade","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/ms-pac-man--1","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co53ly.jpg","s":"Ms. Pac-Man has 36 ways to drive you totally wild and crazy with more fun than you've ever imagined! She gets to run around in 36 unique, mind-boggling mazes! Some small ones, some so big you'll have to scroll around. Some that are mighty strange and some that are straight out of the arcades! Whether you choose Easy, Normal, Hard or Crazy speeds, Ms. Pac-Man will have you running ragged! There's even a Pac Booster button to really let her chake those pesky ghosts. Play Ms. Pac-Man by yourself or take turns with a friend. Better yet, two can play at the same time - one as the divine Ms. Pac-Man; the other as the original Pac-Man!"},{"t":"Multi-Player Soccer Manager","p":["ZX Spectrum","Commodore 64","Amstrad CPC","Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"John Atkinson","u":"D&H Games","g":"Traditional football simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-Player_Soccer_Manager"},{"t":"Multiplayer BattleTech: EGA","p":[],"d":"Kesmai","u":"GEnie","g":"MMORPG","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplayer_BattleTech:_EGA"},{"t":"MUME","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MUME"},{"t":"Musashi, the Samurai Lord","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Musashi,_the_Samurai_Lord"},{"t":"Mutant Fighter","p":["Arcade","FM Towns","Sharp X68000"],"d":"Data East","u":"Data East, I'MAX","g":"Fighting game","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mutant_Fighter_(arcade_game)"},{"t":"Mysterium","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Maxis Software","u":"Asmik Ace Entertainment","g":"Role-playing","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mysterium_(video_game)"},{"t":"Mystic Wand","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"Alpha Denshi","u":"Alpha Denshi","g":"Puzzle-platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mystic_Wand"},{"t":"Mystical Fighter","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"KID","u":"Taito, DreamWorks","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"October 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mystical_Fighter_(video_game)"},{"t":"Nadia: The Secret of Blue Water","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"List, Advance Communication Company, Namco, Gainax","u":"List, Toho, Namco, Gainax","g":"Various","r":"March 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nadia:_The_Secret_of_Blue_Water_(video_game)"},{"t":"Nakajima Satoru F-1 Hero 2","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Human Entertainment","u":"Varie","g":"Formula One racing","r":"September 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nakajima_Satoru_F-1_Hero_2"},{"t":"Nakajima Satoru F-1 Hero GB World Championship '91","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Natsu System","u":"Varie","g":"Formula One racing","r":"December 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nakajima_Satoru_F-1_Hero_GB_World_Championship_'91"},{"t":"Nakajima Satoru Kanshū F1 Grand Prix","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Varie","u":"Varie","g":"Formula One racing","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nakajima_Satoru_Kansh%C5%AB_F1_Grand_Prix"},{"t":"Namachūkei 68","p":["Sharp X68000"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Sports","r":"July 30, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Namach%C5%ABkei_68"},{"t":"Napoleon I: The Campaigns 1805–1814","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Storm Computers Ltd.","u":"Internecine","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleon_I:_The_Campaigns_1805%E2%80%931814"},{"t":"NES Open Tournament Golf","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo R&D2, HAL Laboratory","u":"Nintendo","g":"Sports","r":"September 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NES_Open_Tournament_Golf","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co21kv.jpg","s":"NES Open Tournament Golf is an NES game featuring Mario characters. As the name of the game implies, it is a golf game and was one of the first golfing games in the Mario series. You can play a round of golf on one of three different courses. One or two players can play in the U.S., Japan, or the U.K. and choose a stroke or match play game. Gameplay is from two point of views, starting with an overhead view allowing you to set up the shot and choose a club, then switching to a 3-D point of view. Hitting the ball uses the common power meter where tapping the button starts the meter, tap the button a second time to select the amount of power in the shot, then tap the button a third time to select the amount of slice or hook."},{"t":"Neutopia II","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft, Turbo Technologies, Inc.","g":"Action-adventure","r":"September 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neutopia_II"},{"t":"Neverwinter Nights","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Beyond Software","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Role-playing","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neverwinter_Nights_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"NHL Hockey","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Park Place Productions","u":"Electronic Arts, Electronic Arts Victor","g":"Sports","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NHL_Hockey","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cocq5h.jpg","s":"NHL Hockey (titled EA Hockey outside North America) is an ice hockey game by Electronic Arts."},{"t":"Nibbles","p":["MS-DOS"],"u":"Microsoft","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nibbles_(video_game)"},{"t":"Niji no Silkroad","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Advance Communication Company","u":"Victor Musical Industries","g":"Role-playing video game","r":"February 22, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niji_no_Silkroad"},{"t":"Ninja Boy 2","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Culture Brain","u":"Culture Brain","g":"Action RPG","r":"November 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninja_Boy_2"},{"t":"Ninja Gaiden","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Japan System House","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"November 1, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninja_Gaiden_(Game_Gear)"},{"t":"Ninja Gaiden III: The Ancient Ship of Doom","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tecmo","u":"Tecmo, Atari Corporation","g":"Platform","r":"June 21, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninja_Gaiden_III:_The_Ancient_Ship_of_Doom","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5ips.jpg","s":"Thrilling new cinema screen! Follow the story of Ryu's epic adventure through Tecmo's movie-like Cinema Screen Animation. As you complete each level, a new part of the story unfolds. Exciting new powers and weapons! Over the years Ninja Ryu's fighting skills have improved to include fantastic new powers and weapons. Action-packed Ninja adventure! Can you get Ryu through his most challenging adventure as he battles the hideous ancient forces of darkness to save mankind? The Ultimate Ninja Challenge!"},{"t":"Ninja Gaiden Shadow","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Natsume Co., Ltd.","u":"Tecmo","g":"Platform, hack and slash","r":"December 13, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninja_Gaiden_Shadow"},{"t":"Ninja Rabbits","p":["Commodore 64","Atari ST","Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"MicroValue","u":"MicroValue","g":"Beat 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninja_Rabbits"},{"t":"Ninja Taro","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"NMK","u":"UPL, American Sammy","g":"Action-adventure","r":"March 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninja_Taro"},{"t":"No Buddies Land","p":["Atari ST"],"d":"Expose Software","u":"Loriciel","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No_Buddies_Land"},{"t":"No Greater Glory: The American Civil War","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Strategic Simulations","u":"Strategic Simulations","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No_Greater_Glory:_The_American_Civil_War"},{"t":"Nolan Ryan's Baseball","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Affect","u":"SETA Corporation, Romstar","g":"Sports","r":"July 2, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nolan_Ryan's_Baseball"},{"t":"Nostalgia 1907","p":["Sharp X68000","Sega CD","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Takeru","u":"Sur Dé Wave","g":"Adventure","r":"December 14, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nostalgia_1907"},{"t":"Nova 9: The Return of Gir Draxon","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nova_9:_The_Return_of_Gir_Draxon"},{"t":"Obitus","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Scenario Developments","u":"Psygnosis, Bullet-Proof Software","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obitus"},{"t":"Off the Wall","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Atari Games","u":"Atari Games","g":"Breakout","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Off_the_Wall_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Oh No! More Lemmings","p":["Amiga"],"d":"DMA Design","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Puzzle","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oh_No!_More_Lemmings","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co83rd.jpg","s":"Oh No! More Lemmings is the follow-up to Lemmings. The game features 100 new levels, and all-new graphics and music. It was released in both a stand-alone and an add-on version."},{"t":"Operation C","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami, Ultra Games, Palcom","g":"Run and gun","r":"January 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_C_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6fy1.jpg","s":"Operation C is an Action game, developed and published by Konami, which was released in 1991."},{"t":"Operation Neptune","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"The Learning Company","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Neptune_(video_game)"},{"t":"Operation: Desert Storm","p":["macOS"],"d":"Bungie","u":"Bungie","g":"Military strategy","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation:_Desert_Storm_(video_game)"},{"t":"Ork","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"WJS Design","u":"Psygnosis","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ork_(video_game)"},{"t":"Otaku no Seiza: An Adventure in the Otaku Galaxy","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otaku_no_Seiza:_An_Adventure_in_the_Otaku_Galaxy"},{"t":"Out of This World","p":["Atari ST","Amiga"],"d":"Delphine Software","u":"U.S. Gold, Interplay Entertainment, Delphine Software, FoxySofts","g":"Platform, Adventure","r":"November 1, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/out-of-this-world","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co9ssg.jpg","s":"Out of This World (Another World in Europe) is a cinematic platformer that uses vector-based graphics and rotoscoped animations to deliver a minimalistic yet expressive visual style. It features a combination of action, platforming, and puzzle-solving mechanics, with a deliberate lack of HUD and textual guidance. Gameplay emphasizes trial-and-error, timing, and exploration, with a limited checkpoint system.\n\nIn 2005, a homebrew port of the Atari ST version was released for the Game Boy Advance, officially allowed by Éric Chahi to be published with the original assets."},{"t":"Out Run Europa","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Probe Software","u":"U.S. Gold","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Out_Run_Europa"},{"t":"Outzone","p":["Atari ST","Amiga","Commodore 64"],"d":"Lankhor","u":"Lankhor","g":"Action, strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outzone"},{"t":"Over Horizon","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Pixel, Hot-B","u":"Hot-B, Takara","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"April 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Over_Horizon","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6osh.jpg","s":"Over Horizon is a Shoot-'Em-Up game, developed by Pixel and published by Hot-B, which was released in Europe in 1991."},{"t":"Override","p":["PC Engine","Sharp X68000"],"d":"Sting Entertainment","u":"Data East, Sting Entertainment","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"January 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Override_(video_game)"},{"t":"P. P. Hammer and his Pneumatic Weapon","p":["Amiga","Commodore 64"],"d":"Traveling Bits","u":"Demonware","g":"Puzzle-platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P._P._Hammer_and_his_Pneumatic_Weapon"},{"t":"Paganitzu","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Keith Schuler","u":"Apogee Software","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paganitzu"},{"t":"Paragliding Simulation","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS","Amstrad CPC"],"d":"Atreid Concept","u":"Loriciels","g":"Simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding_Simulation"},{"t":"Parasol Stars","p":["PC Engine","Game Boy"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito, Working Designs, Ocean Software","g":"Platform","r":"February 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parasol_Stars"},{"t":"Patton Strikes Back","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Chris Crawford","u":"Broderbund","g":"Computer wargame, Real-time tactics","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patton_Strikes_Back"},{"t":"Peter Pan and the Pirates","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Equilibrium","u":"Toy Headquarters","g":"Side-scrolling","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Pan_and_the_Pirates_(video_game)"},{"t":"Phalanx","p":["Sharp X68000"],"d":"ZOOM Inc., Kemco","u":"ZOOM Inc., Kemco","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"May 17, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phalanx_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4m8k.jpg","s":"A futuristic shooter, Phalanx is the name of the prototype starfighter flown by Wink Baufield, a genetically enhanced pilot. It is sent to protect and reclaim Delia IV, a planet in the Andromeda galaxy, which has been colonized by humans for interstellar research purposes. However, an unknown and powerful alien force has overtaken the planet, the military defenses have been completely overwhelmed, and all hope rests on the Phalanx.\n\nYou fly through eight levels, first through various locations in Delia IV, and then you invade the aliens themselves. Like most shooters, you can upgrade your weapons by picking up bonus items dropped by enemy craft."},{"t":"Pipi & Bibi's","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Toaplan","u":"Toaplan, Nova Apparate GMBH & Co., Romstar","g":"Action, eroge, platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pipi_&_Bibi's"},{"t":"Police Quest III: The Kindred","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Police_Quest_III:_The_Kindred","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co48z6.jpg","s":"The third game in Sierra's Police Quest series. Based again in Lytton, Sonny Bonds has been promoted, and is forced to deal with both the questionable ethics of a colleague and the attempted murder of his wife, as the sleepy California city is under attack by a deranged serial killer."},{"t":"Pollux","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Dooyong","u":"Atlus","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pollux_(video_game)"},{"t":"Pools of Darkness","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","Amiga","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Strategic Simulations, MicroMagic, Marionette","u":"Strategic Simulations, Pony Canyon","g":"Role-playing game, tactical RPG","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pools_of_Darkness","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6k39.jpg","s":"For ten years your glorious band has fought Evil wherever it has threatened the Realms. Lord Bane has suffered many defeats at your hands and new peace washes over the land. Now your path comes full circle - back to the thriving city of Phlan.\n\nAs your ship arrives, you see the towers of Phlan, where before only ruins stood. The slums and decay have given way to new growth. Boats bob in the harbor under the watchful protection of Sokal Keep. In large measure this prosperity and success was born of your defeat of Tyranthraxus so many years ago.\n\nAs the sail is furled, and the crew prepares to dock, you wonder \"Where could adventure be found among such peace ..."},{"t":"Popeye 2","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Copya System","u":"Sigma Enterprises, Activision","g":"Platform","r":"November 22, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Popeye_2"},{"t":"Popful Mail","p":["PC-8800 series"],"d":"Nihon Falcom","u":"Nihon Falcom, Sega, Working Designs, NEC Home Electronics","g":"Action-adventure, platform","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Popful_Mail"},{"t":"Popils","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Tengen","u":"Tengen","g":"Puzzle-platform","r":"July 12, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Popils"},{"t":"Populous II: Trials of the Olympian Gods","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","FM Towns","MS-DOS","macOS","Genesis / Mega Drive","PC-9800 series","Sharp X68000","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Bullfrog Productions, PanelComp","u":"Electronic Arts, Virgin Games","g":"God game","r":"August 31, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Populous_II:_Trials_of_the_Olympian_Gods","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2by5.jpg","s":"As the offspring of almighty Zeus, you demand a seat among the gods. But before ascending to the top of Mt. Olympus, you must first defeat some of the most powerful deities in the Greek pantheon. Such a task would be impossible for a puny mortal, but you wield the raw power of the universe in your hands, for you are destined to become a god! Learn to shatter the sky with lightning and shower the earth with fire. Summon heroes to aid your people, and plagues to destroy the enemy. Call forth tornadoes, hurricanes, tidal waves, and columns of flame.\n\nThe second installment of Bullfrog's Populous series follows the path set by its predecessor and remains one of the greatest god-games ever created. Populous 2: Trials of the Olympian Gods vastly improves the formula by adding new spells, better quality sound and graphics, and a character development system that allows you to customize your divine powers in any way you want. You really can’t go wrong with a game where you can kill gods, obliterate entire continents, and strike down upon the infidels with great vengeance and furious anger. Just as Peter Molyneux, those things simply never get old."},{"t":"Power Eleven","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Westone","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Traditional soccer simulation","r":"June 21, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Eleven"},{"t":"Power Spikes","p":["Arcade","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Video System","u":"Video System, McO'River, Ubi Soft","g":"Sports","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_Spikes"},{"t":"Powerball","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Sports","r":"February 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powerball_(video_game)"},{"t":"Prehistorik","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","MS-DOS","Amiga CDTV"],"d":"Titus France","u":"Titus France","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prehistorik","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co86g1.jpg","s":"Prehistorik is a side-view platform game. You control a neanderthal who must march through hostile pre-historic environments seeking food and keeping himself safe. Some food can be found in caves, but it mainly exists in living animal forms. These include fire-breathing Pyro-Tax dragons, the Turtosaurus, and the Bad Bat. Each of these must be clubbed to death, some taking up to 5 hits, and then walked over. Some other enemies are indestructible, with the monkeys who throw coconuts at you particularly fiendish. You will have to complete the level within the time limit, and avoid contact with these foes. However, bombs and improved weapons can be collected, as can extra lives, time bonuses and an improved jump."},{"t":"Princess Maker","p":["PC-9800 series","Windows","MSX","Sharp X68000","PC Engine CD"],"d":"Gainax","u":"Gainax","g":"Raising simulation","r":"May 24, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess_Maker_(video_game)"},{"t":"Princess Tomato in the Salad Kingdom","p":["PC-8800 series","PC-9800 series","MSX","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Adventure","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess_Tomato_in_the_Salad_Kingdom"},{"t":"Pro Tennis Tour 2","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Blue Byte Software","u":"Ubi Soft","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pro_Tennis_Tour_2"},{"t":"Profezia","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Trecision","u":"Genias","g":"Adventure","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Profezia"},{"t":"Provocator","p":["Acorn Archimedes"],"d":"Network 23","u":"Computer Tutorial Services","g":"Shoot 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provocator"},{"t":"Putt & Putter","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"SIMS","u":"Sega","g":"Sports","r":"September 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Putt_&_Putter"},{"t":"Puyo Puyo","p":["MSX","Nintendo Entertainment System","PC Engine CD"],"d":"Compile","u":"Compile, Tokuma Shoten, Sega, Banpresto","g":"Puzzle","r":"October 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puyo_Puyo_(video_game)"},{"t":"Pu·Li·Ru·La","p":["Arcade","FM Towns"],"d":"Taito, Goo!","u":"Taito, Xing Entertainment","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pu%C2%B7Li%C2%B7Ru%C2%B7La","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co66p8.jpg","s":"Pu·Li·Ru·La is a surreal arcade beat 'em up created by Taito, first released in 1991.\n\nPu·Li·Ru·La takes place in Radishland, a land where time is kept correctly flowing with a time key. However, \"a bad man appeared and stole the time key to stop the time flow. The towns were attacked one by one, the time flow was stopped and they received damage\", as the English translation explains. Zac and Mel, the playable characters, are requested by an old man to defend their town. The old man also gives the children a \"magic stick\" to fight with"},{"t":"Qbasic Gorillas","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"IBM","u":"IBM","g":"Shooter, Turn-based strategy (TBS)","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/qbasic-gorillas","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/odscjo6pjkr8mnfbuvat.jpg","s":"Gorillas is a video game first distributed with MS-DOS 5 and published in 1991 by IBM corporation. It is a turn-based artillery game consisting of two gorillas throwing explosive bananas at each other above a city skyline. The players can adjust the angle and velocity of each throw, as well as the gravitational pull of the planet."},{"t":"QuackShot","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Platform, metroidvania","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/QuackShot"},{"t":"QuackShot Starring Donald Duck","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega Enterprises, Ltd.","u":"Tec Toy, Sega Enterprises, Ltd.","g":"Platform","r":"September 5, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/quackshot-starring-donald-duck","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1y89.jpg","s":"QuackShot starring Donald Duck is a steady Disney run-and-jump platformer, with a little puzzle-solving element. Characters are mostly based on Disney's cartoon series Duck Tales.\n\nDonald and his three nephews get their hands on a treasure map and set out to hunt the big prize. Donald travels throughout the world, to places like Egypt, India, South Pole, and even Transylvania, while his nephews fly an airplane.\n\nAs a weapon Donald uses plungers that can freeze the enemies or stick to walls which can then be used for climbing. Other wacky weapons are popcorn and bubblegum."},{"t":"Quadrel","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Loriciels","u":"Loriciels","g":"Puzzle","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadrel"},{"t":"R.B.I. Baseball 3","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tengen","u":"Tengen","g":"Sports","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R.B.I._Baseball_3"},{"t":"Racing Damashii","p":["Game Boy","PC Engine"],"d":"Irem","u":"Irem","g":"Arcade motorcycle racing","r":"February 28, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Racing_Damashii"},{"t":"Radia Senki: Reimeihen","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tecmo","u":"Tecmo","g":"Action RPG","r":"November 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radia_Senki:_Reimeihen"},{"t":"Raiden Trad","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"u":"Tec Toy","g":"Shooter","r":"October 14, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/raiden-trad","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5p3x.jpg","s":"Raiden Trad is an over-head vertical-scrolling shooter, based on an arcade game of the same title. It features two forms of weapon upgrades and two types of missiles (normal or homing). You start the game with several bombs which you can use to destroy most enemies on the screen to get yourself out of a jam. Each level ends with a large boss or bosses."},{"t":"Ray-Thunder","p":["Game Boy"],"u":"Nichibutsu","g":"First-person shooter","r":"February 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ray-Thunder"},{"t":"Rayxanber II","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Data West","u":"Data West","g":"Horizontally scrolling shooter","r":"June 7, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rayxanber_II"},{"t":"Reader Rabbit 2","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"The Learning Company","g":"Educational video game","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reader_Rabbit_2"},{"t":"Realms","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Graftgold","u":"Virgin Games","g":"RTS","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Realms_(video_game)"},{"t":"Rent a Hero","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega AM2","u":"Sega","g":"Action role-playing","r":"September 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rent_a_Hero"},{"t":"Rescue Rover","p":["MS-DOS","Apple IIGS"],"d":"id Software","u":"Softdisk","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rescue_Rover"},{"t":"Rezon","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Allumer","u":"Taito","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rezon_(video_game)"},{"t":"Riot City","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Westone","u":"Sega","g":"Beat 'em up","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riot_City"},{"t":"Road Rash","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Electronic Arts, Peakstar Software, Probe Software, The Code Monkeys","u":"Electronic Arts, U.S. Gold, Ocean Software","g":"Racing, vehicular combat","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Road_Rash_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5p41.jpg","s":"Road Rash is a high-octane motorcycle racing game where players become bikers competing in illegal races across the U.S.A. The game focuses on a series of races in California, which players must win to progress to more challenging competitions. Along the way, they engage in both unarmed and armed combat to outmaneuver other racers."},{"t":"Road Riot 4WD","p":["Arcade","Atari ST","Atari Lynx"],"d":"Atari Games, Equilibrium","u":"Atari Games, THQ","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Road_Riot_4WD"},{"t":"Robin Hood: Prince of Thieves","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Sculptured Software, Bits Studios","u":"Virgin Games, Mindscape","g":"Action-adventure","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robin_Hood:_Prince_of_Thieves_(video_game)"},{"t":"Robo Army","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"October 30, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robo_Army","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co9dka.jpg","s":"In a futuristic city named Neo-Detroit, a scientist named Jeed kidnaps two police officers, Maxima and Rocky, and transforms them into cyborgs, but failed to implant on them a chip that would make them obey his will. They eventually escape his grasp and decide to defeat the mad scientist. He pits his army against the two, but they are no match for Maxima and Rocky. Maxima and Rocky eventually find out that Jeed kidnapped his own daughter to prevent her from stopping him. Maxima and Rocky defeated Jeed and saved his daughter. In gratitude for rescuing her, she returned them to normal."},{"t":"Robocco Wars","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Pixel","u":"IGS","g":"Action, Platformer","r":"August 2, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robocco_Wars"},{"t":"RoboCop 3","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","Atari ST"],"d":"Digital Image Design, Eden Entertainment Software, Ocean Software, Probe Software","u":"Ocean Software, Flying Edge","g":"Platform, shooter","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RoboCop_3_(video_game)"},{"t":"RoboSport","p":["Windows","macOS","Amiga"],"d":"Maxis, Presage Software","u":"Maxis","g":"Turn-based tactics","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RoboSport"},{"t":"Robot Battle","p":["macOS"],"d":"Toby Smith","u":"Blue Cow Software","g":"Programming game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robot_Battle_(Macintosh_game)"},{"t":"Rockin' Kats","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Arcade"],"d":"Atlus","u":"Atlus","g":"Platform","r":"April 5, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockin'_Kats","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4aoc.jpg","s":"It's a Dance with Danger when Willy tries to rescue Jill!\n\nThe hippest cool Kat in New York, Willy, is hot on the tail of local crime boss, Mugsy. The fur's gonna fly 'cause Mugsy just cat-nabbed Willy's girl, Jill, and stashed her away. To find her, Willy has to face hoods on the streets of the Big Apple, duel desperados in the wild west, dog-fight Ace Pilots at 40,000 feet, and avoid being de-railed at an amusement park. But he's not called the Rockin' Kat for nothing, and when he starts break dancin' and body slammin' his enemies, make room on the dance floor!"},{"t":"Rodent's Revenge","p":["Windows"],"u":"Microsoft","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodent's_Revenge"},{"t":"Roger Clemens' MVP Baseball","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Genesis / Mega Drive","Game Boy"],"d":"Sculptured Software","u":"Acclaim Entertainment, LJN, Flying Edge","g":"Traditional baseball simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Clemens'_MVP_Baseball"},{"t":"Rolling Thunder 2","p":["Arcade","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco, Sega","g":"Run and gun","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolling_Thunder_2"},{"t":"RPM Racing","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Silicon & Synapse","u":"Interplay Productions, Victor Musical Industries","g":"Racing","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RPM_Racing"},{"t":"Rules of Engagement","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Omnitrend Software","u":"Omnitrend Software","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rules_of_Engagement_(video_game)"},{"t":"Running Battle","p":["Sega Master System"],"d":"Opera House","u":"Sega","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Running_Battle"},{"t":"Saint Sword","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Cyclone System","u":"Taito","g":"Action, Hack-and-slash","r":"June 28, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Sword"},{"t":"Samurai Pizza Cats","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samurai_Pizza_Cats"},{"t":"Sandy's Circus Adventure","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy's_Circus_Adventure"},{"t":"Saori","p":["PC-9800 series","FM Towns","Sharp X68000"],"d":"Fairytale X-Shitei","u":"Kirara","g":"Adventure, adult, bishojo game","r":"October 18, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saori_(video_game)"},{"t":"Scooby-Doo and Scrappy-Doo","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"PAL Developments","u":"HI-Tec","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scooby-Doo_and_Scrappy-Doo_(video_game)"},{"t":"Scorched Earth","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Wendell Hicken","g":"Artillery","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scorched_Earth_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cobsbh.jpg","s":"Blast your enemies from the face of the Earth together with Earth itself. Buy bigger weapons to crush more enemies. Get more money for crushing more enemies. And finaly buy a much more bigger weapons to crush even more enemies.\n\nScorched Earth is an early classic shareware artillery game that featured hot-seat multiplayer for up to 10 players, and was the spiritual predecessor to games like the Worms series."},{"t":"Second Conflict","p":[],"u":"jSOFT","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Conflict"},{"t":"Secret Weapons of the Luftwaffe","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Totally Games","u":"Lucasfilm Games","g":"Combat flight simulator","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secret_Weapons_of_the_Luftwaffe","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co21h6.jpg","s":"After the success of the simulation titles Their Finest Hour: The Battle of Britain® and Battlehawks 1942 in the late 80s, LucasArts decided to follow up those games with another World War II simulation title: Secret Weapons of the Luftwaffe. Released in August 1991, the game followed the campaign by the US 8th Air Force to cripple the German Luftwaffe during the final years of World War II. Players could fly in either American or German warplanes. The game was remarkable because many of the playable airplanes were still under development during the war. In other words, the planes were never used extensively in battle, so players could explore “what if ” possibilities with the game. Those possibilities were further expanded by four Tour of Duty expansion packs. The planes in those expansions included the P-38 Lighting, a twin-engine escort fighter and the German Do335, an interceptor aircraft that featured a conventional tractor propeller in the nose as well as a pusher propeller behind the tail of the aircraft."},{"t":"Sega Chess","p":["Sega Master System"],"d":"Probe Software","u":"Sega","g":"Computer chess","r":"November 14, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sega_Chess"},{"t":"Seirei Senshi Spriggan","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Compile","u":"Naxat Soft","g":"Vertically scrolling shooter","r":"July 19, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seirei_Senshi_Spriggan"},{"t":"Sengoku","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"SNK","u":"SNK, Data East, Sammy","g":"Beat 'em up, hack and slash","r":"February 12, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sengoku_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1w4x.jpg","s":"Sengoku is a side scrolling action game where the player takes the role of a super warrior who must battle against ghosts from the past bent on taking over the present."},{"t":"Sex Olympics","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Free Spirit Software","u":"Free Spirit Software","g":"Adventure","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sex_Olympics"},{"t":"Seymour Goes to Hollywood","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","MS-DOS","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Big Red Software","u":"Codemasters","g":"Platform, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seymour_Goes_to_Hollywood"},{"t":"Shadow Knights","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"id Software","u":"Softdisk","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow_Knights"},{"t":"Shadow Sorcerer","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"U.S. Gold","u":"Strategic Simulations, U.S. Gold","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadow_Sorcerer"},{"t":"Shatterhand","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Arcade"],"d":"Natsume","u":"Angel, Jaleco","g":"Action/Platformer, beat 'em up","r":"October 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shatterhand"},{"t":"Sherlock Holmes: Consulting Detective","p":["FM Towns","Amiga CDTV","MS-DOS","PC Engine","Sega CD","PC Engine CD"],"d":"ICOM Simulations","u":"ICOM Simulations, Sega","g":"Adventure, interactive movie","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherlock_Holmes:_Consulting_Detective"},{"t":"Shining in the Darkness","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Climax Entertainment","u":"Sega","g":"Role-playing","r":"March 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shining_in_the_Darkness","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2xez.jpg","s":"Dark Sol has vowed to reign over the enchanted Kingdom of Thornwood, and only you can stop him. Find the powerful Arms of Light and battle the deadly beasts that keep your people desperate and starving in this epic journey for peace. Roaming the dark Labyrinth, you'll seek out enemies and collect weapons, all of which will help you pass the test of the Ancients and become a shining knight. Get into the adventure with a stunning 3-D perspective, amazing cinematic views and fast-paced scrolling. You'll move from battle to battle, solving puzzles and discovering secret items in this engrossing struggle between good and evil."},{"t":"Sid Meier's Civilization","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"MPS Labs","u":"MicroProse Software, Inc.","g":"Strategy, Turn-based strategy (TBS)","r":"September 30, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/sid-meiers-civilization","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1y9x.jpg","s":"Sid Meier's Civilization is a turn-based strategy computer game created by Sid Meier for MicroProse in 1991.The game's objective is \"...to build an empire that would stand the test of time\". The game begins in 4000 BC, and the players attempt to expand and develop their empires through the ages until modern and near-future times. It is also known simply as Civilization."},{"t":"Silent Debuggers","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Data East","u":"NEC International, Data East","g":"First-person shooter","r":"March 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silent_Debuggers"},{"t":"SimAnt","p":["macOS","MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Maxis","u":"Maxis","g":"Life simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SimAnt","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5xpt.jpg","s":"SimAnt is a simulation strategy game which makes you a head of ant colony. There are 3 ways to play SimAnt: Quick Games, Full Games, and Experimental games.\nIn Quick Games you are a black ant colony competing with a red ant colony for food and territory. Your goal is to defeat the red ants and take over your home turf - the local patch of ground in the backyard. Your task will be complicated by marauding spiders, voracious ant lions, torrential rains, crusing human feet and merciless lawn mowers.\n\nIn Full Games you must take over the entire backyard section by section. Then take over the house and drive out the humans. In addition to hordes of red ants and other pests, you will face the chemical death of inescticides\n\nIn Experimental Games, you are a human playing and experimenting with ants. Your tools let you build walls and barriers to run ants through mazes, add food, add ants, dig of fill in holes, test ants' reactions to various trail marking and alarm chemicals, and play with insecticide."},{"t":"Skate or Die: Tour de Thrash","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Realtime Associates","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Alternative sports","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skate_or_Die:_Tour_de_Thrash"},{"t":"SkiFree","p":["Windows"],"d":"Chris Pirih","u":"Microsoft, GearSprout","g":"Sports, casual","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SkiFree","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co987k.jpg","s":"SkiFree is a casual single-player sports simulator wherein the player uses the keyboard or the mouse to control a skier across a white background representing snow on a mountainside. The object of the game is to ski down an endless slope and avoid the obstacles (trees, stumps, dogs, etc.) The player can also opt to partake in three modes: slalom, freestyle, and tree slalom. In slalom, players must properly ski around flags in an attempt to complete the run with the shortest time possible. Tree slalom adds obstacles to the slalom run. In freestyle, players ski downhill and jump off ramps while racking up points by performing tricks. Deductions are imposed for colliding into obstacles or failing to land properly after a stunt. When the player passes the 2,000-meter mark, the Abominable Snowman appears and starts to chase the player, eating them if it catches them."},{"t":"Sky High Stuntman","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum"],"u":"Codemasters","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky_High_Stuntman"},{"t":"Slightly Magic","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum","Linux","macOS","Windows"],"d":"Astonishing Animations, Potassium Frog","u":"Codemasters, Potassium Frog","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slightly_Magic"},{"t":"Slordax: The Unknown Enemy","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Softdisk","u":"Softdisk","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slordax:_The_Unknown_Enemy"},{"t":"Smart Ball","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Game Freak, System Sacom","u":"Epic/Sony Records, Sony Imagesoft","g":"Action-adventure","r":"September 13, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smart_Ball","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5zai.jpg","s":"The game involves going through 8 stages (broken up into two parts each) of side-scrolling action. At the end of each stage is a boss monster that must be disposed of.\n\nJerry moves by sliding across the ground and can latch on to walls and climb them. Jerry can also jump, stretch out to cling to ceilings, and also move at a faster pace. Jerry can consume balls that he finds during a stage and can then fire them out of his body at enemies blocking his path. Enemies can also be defeated by jumping on them and then flattening Jerry's body on them. Also, there are flowers that contain items for Jerry to collect. Items include life, which refills health, a 1-up that adds an extra life, and a seed, which will grow a climbable beanstalk when planted. If the player collects the scattered letters that appear in every stage that spell out the name Jerry, the player will earn two extra lives."},{"t":"Soccer","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Tose","u":"Tonkin House, Bandai","g":"Traditional soccer simulation","r":"June 7, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soccer_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Solidarność","p":[],"d":"P.Z.Karen Co.","u":"California Dreams","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solidarno%C5%9B%C4%87_(video_game)"},{"t":"Solitary Fighter","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Fighting","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solitary_Fighter"},{"t":"Solvalou","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"First-person rail shooter","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solvalou"},{"t":"Somer Assault","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Atlus","u":"Atlus","g":"Action game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somer_Assault"},{"t":"Sonic Eraser","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_Eraser","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cobh5v.jpg","s":"Sonic Eraser is a video game in the Sonic the Hedgehog series that was available to owners of the Sega Meganet, a modem for the Sega Mega Drive in Japan. While it is a title in the Sonic series, other than featuring the titular character, there is not much that this game has in common with others in the franchise.\n\nSince the Meganet modem never achieved worldwide availability, Sonic Eraser became a \"lost\" Sonic game, until February 2004, when the Sonic CulT website got a hold of the ROM through Sega's Japan-only download service.\n\nThe game is a fairly simple puzzle game. In the versus mode, when a player gets a combination of three consecutive lineups of pieces, that player's Sonic attacks the other player's Sonic. The other player momentarily loses control of his pieces.\n\nThe game is on Sega's B-Club download service."},{"t":"Sonic the Hedgehog","p":["Sega Master System","Game Gear"],"d":"Ancient","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"November 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_the_Hedgehog_(8-bit_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coanlj.jpg","s":"Sonic the Hedgehog is a 2D side-scrolling platformer developed by Sonic Team and published by Sega for the Genesis/Mega Drive. Players control Sonic the Hedgehog, who can run at near supersonic speeds and sets out on a quest to defeat Dr. Robotnik, a scientist who has imprisoned animals in robots and seeks the powerful Chaos Emeralds."},{"t":"Sonic the Hedgehog","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Arcade"],"d":"Sonic Team","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonic_the_Hedgehog_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Space Quest I","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","Apple II","Apple IIGS","Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Quest_I"},{"t":"Space Quest I: Roger Wilco in the Sarien Encounter","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Point-and-click, Adventure","r":"August 20, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/space-quest-i-roger-wilco-in-the-sarien-encounter","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5vat.jpg","s":"Space Quest 1: The Sarien Encounter is an old DOS science fiction adventure game, developed by Sierra On-Line, designed by Mark Crowe - Scott Murphy and published by Sierra On-Line in 1986. It's available for download. Space Quest 1: The Sarien Encounter is also part of the Space Quest series."},{"t":"Space Quest IV","p":["MS-DOS","Windows","macOS"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Adventure","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Quest_IV"},{"t":"Space Quest IV: Roger Wilco and the Time Rippers","p":["macOS","MS-DOS","Amiga","Windows"],"d":"Sierra On-Line","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"Point-and-click, Adventure","r":"January 1, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/space-quest-iv-roger-wilco-and-the-time-rippers","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5v4t.jpg","s":"May the farce be with you! Get ready for a trek through time with everybody's favorite intergalactic sanitation engineer and freelance hero, Roger Wilco. In their latest spaced-out space opera, the Two Guys From Andomeda take on science fiction (and just about everything else!) is a spoofy sendup that will leave you laughing.\n\nRoger Wilco embarks on a topsy-turvy trip through space and time in this fourth chapter in the award-winning Space Quest series, Roger Wilco and the Time Rippers. From a nostalgic look at his past in Space Quest I to a terrifying visit to the future of Space Quest XII, Roger's on the run again.\n\nAnd hot on his ion trail are the Sequel Police, deadly crosstime assassins who are out to put an end to Roger and all of his Space Quest nonsense once and for all.\n\nOnce again it's up to Roger Wilco to save his future- or is that his past?- of the Space Quest series, and keep the universe safe for fair play, bad jokes, and lots more Space Quest Games."},{"t":"Space Shuttle Project","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Imagineering","u":"Absolute Entertainment","g":"Action","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Shuttle_Project"},{"t":"Spaceship Warlock","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Mike Saenz, Joe Sparks","u":"Reactor, Inc.","g":"Adventure game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaceship_Warlock"},{"t":"Spanky's Quest","p":["Game Boy","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Natsume","g":"Puzzle-platform","r":"April 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spanky's_Quest"},{"t":"Spartan X 2","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tamtex","u":"Irem","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"September 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spartan_X_2"},{"t":"Spectre","p":["macOS","MS-DOS","Windows","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Peninsula Gameworks","u":"Velocity Development","g":"Vehicular combat, first-person shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectre_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Spellbound Dizzy","p":["Amstrad CPC","ZX Spectrum","Commodore 64","Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Big Red Software","u":"Codemasters","g":"Adventure, platform","r":"November 11, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spellbound_Dizzy"},{"t":"Spellbound!","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"The Learning Company","u":"The Learning Company","g":"Educational","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spellbound!"},{"t":"Spellcasting 201: The Sorcerer's Appliance","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Legend Entertainment","u":"Legend Entertainment","g":"Interactive fiction","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spellcasting_201:_The_Sorcerer's_Appliance"},{"t":"Spelunx","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"Cyan","u":"Broderbund","g":"Educational/Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spelunx"},{"t":"Spider-Man vs. The Kingpin","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Sega Master System"],"d":"Technopop, Recreational Brainware","u":"Sega","g":"Action, platform","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spider-Man_vs._The_Kingpin"},{"t":"Spider-Man: The Video Game","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spider-Man:_The_Video_Game","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/cocxp2.jpg","s":"Spider-Man: The Video Game, also known as Spider-Man, is a 1991 arcade video game developed by Sega based on the Marvel Comics character Spider-Man.\n\nSpider-Man and his allies must retrieve a mystical artefact first from the Kingpin, then Doctor Doom."},{"t":"Spinal Breakers","p":["Arcade"],"d":"V-System","u":"V-System, Sammy Corporation","g":"Shooting gallery","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spinal_Breakers"},{"t":"Spirit of Adventure","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Commodore 64","MS-DOS"],"d":"Attic Entertainment","u":"Starbyte Software","g":"Role-playing, dungeon crawl","r":"February 12, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spirit_of_Adventure_(video_game)"},{"t":"Splash Lake","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Bits Laboratory","u":"NEC Avenue","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Splash_Lake"},{"t":"Spore","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Mike Snyder","u":"Flogsoli Productions","g":"Breakout clone, text adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spore_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Sports Talk Baseball","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Traditional baseball simulation","r":"August 30, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sports_Talk_Baseball"},{"t":"Spud's Adventure","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Atlus Co.","u":"Atlus","g":"Role-playing video game, action","r":"January 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spud's_Adventure"},{"t":"Star Wars","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Lucasfilm Games, Beam Software, NMS Software, Tiertex","u":"Victor, INT: JVC, Capcom USA, Nintendo of America","g":"Action-platform","r":"November 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Wars_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5ttm.jpg","s":"This game is based entirely on the first Star Wars movie, Episode IV: A New Hope. The opening of the game features an animated cinematic of the opening scene of the film. After that the player takes control of Luke Skywalker, driving the landspeeder across Tatooine in search of R2-D2.\n\nLuke Skywalker explores various locations throughout Tatooine, fighting numerous enemies including Jawas and Stormtroopers. The game features many locations from the movie including the Chalmun's Cantina in Mos Eisley where Luke meets Han Solo for the first time. This section of the game features a classic 8-bit rendition of the \"Cantina Band\" theme as played by the fictitious band, Figrin D'an and the Modal Nodes, in the feature film. The game also contains many famous lines from the movie including Obi-Wan Kenobi's plea to Luke to join him in his trip to Alderann and Han Solo's description of the smell from the Death Star's trash compactor.\n\nThe player eventually lands on the Death Star and has to destroy the tractor beam to escape. The game also features first person segments of piloting the Millenium Falcon through that Asteroid field that was once Alderaan, and shooting down Tie Fighters in both the Millenium Falcon and an X-Wing fighter. The finale of the game features a top down view of Luke Skywalker's X-Wing flying through the Death Star trenches. After the player successfully fires a torpedo into the Death Star's exposed exhaust port, a cinematic of the Death Star exploding and Luke Skywalker being awarded a medal on Yavin IV is shown.\n\nThe game featured the characters Luke Skywalker, Han Solo, Obi-Wan Kenobi, Princess Leia, R2-D2, and C-3PO. The game however does not feature Darth Vader or Chewbacca, both of whom are major characters in the film."},{"t":"Star-King","p":[],"d":"Walker Vanning","u":"Spacewar Simulations Company","g":"Strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star-King"},{"t":"Starblade","p":["Arcade","Mobile phone"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Rail shooter","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starblade_(1991_game)"},{"t":"Steel Talons","p":["Arcade","Atari ST","Atari Lynx","Genesis / Mega Drive","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Atari Games, NuFX, Polygames, Panoramic","u":"Atari Games, Sega, Atari Corporation, Tengen","g":"Combat flight simulator","r":"August 23, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steel_Talons"},{"t":"Stellar Agent","p":[],"d":"Robert Kraus","u":"Better Games","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stellar_Agent"},{"t":"Storm Master","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Silmarils","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Storm_Master"},{"t":"Strato Fighter","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Tecmo","u":"Tecmo","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strato_Fighter"},{"t":"Street Fighter II","p":["Arcade","MS-DOS","Commodore 64","Atari ST","Mobile phone"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom, Bandai, U.S. Gold, Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Fighting","r":"March 7, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Street_Fighter_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co55et.jpg","s":"A sequel to Street Fighter, Street Fighter II improved upon the many concepts introduced in the first game, including the use of command-based special moves and a six-button configuration, while offering players a selection of multiple playable characters, each with their own unique fighting style and special moves.\n\nStreet Fighter II is credited with starting the fighting game boom during the 1990s. Its success led to the production of several updated versions, each offering additional features and characters over previous versions, as well as many home versions. Some home versions of the Street Fighter II games have sold millions of copies, with the SNES port of the first Street Fighter II being Capcom's best-selling consumer game of all time as of 2008.\n\nStreet Fighter II follows several of the conventions and rules already established by its original 1987 predecessor. The player engages opponents in one-on-one, close-quarters combat in a series of best-of-two-out-of-three matches. The objective of each round is to deplete the opponent's vitality before the timer runs out. If both opponents knock each other out at the same time or the timer runs out with both fighters having an equal amount of vitality left, a \"double KO\" or \"draw game\" is declared. Additional rounds will be played until sudden death. In the first Street Fighter II, a match could last up to 10 rounds if there was no clear winner; this was reduced to 4 rounds in the Champion Edition and onward. If there is no clear winner by the end of the final round, then either the computer-controlled opponent will win by default in a single-player match, or both fighters will lose in a 2-player match.\n\nAfter every third match in single-player mode, the player will participate in a \"bonus game\" for additional points. The bonus games include (in order) a car-breaking event; a barrel-breaking bonus game where the barrels are dropped off from a conveyor belt above the player; and a drum-breaking bonus game where drums are flammable and piled over each other. The bonus games were removed from the arcade version of Super Street Fighter II Turbo.\n\nAs in the original, the game's controls use an eight-directional joystick and six attack buttons. The player uses the joystick to jump, crouch, move the character towards or away from the opponent, and guard against the opponent's attacks. There are three punch buttons and three kick buttons, each with varying strength and speed (Light, Medium, and Heavy). The player can perform a variety of basic moves in any position, including grabbing/throwing attacks, which were not featured in the original Street Fighter. Like in the original, the player can perform special moves by inputting a combination of directional and button-based commands.\n\nStreet Fighter II differs from the original in that it features multiple playable characters, each with distinct fighting styles and special moves. Additionally, the player can \"cancel\" an animation by performing another move, enabling combinations of basic and special moves. Both of these features would be expanded upon in subsequent installments."},{"t":"Street Rod 2","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"P.Z.Karen Co. Development Group, Logical Design Works","u":"California Dreams","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Street_Rod_2"},{"t":"Streets of Rage","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","Sega CD"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"August 2, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Streets_of_Rage_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co49sf.jpg","s":"Streets of Rage is a side-scrolling beat 'em up video game developed and published by Sega in 1991 for Mega Drive/Genesis. It is the first installment of the Bare Knuckle/Streets of Rage series which was followed by Streets of Rage 2 and Streets of Rage 3."},{"t":"Strike Force","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Midway","u":"Midway","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strike_Force_(video_game)"},{"t":"Strike Gunner S.T.G","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Athena","u":"Tecmo, Athena, NTVIC, Activision","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strike_Gunner_S.T.G"},{"t":"Sugoro Quest: Dice Heroes","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Technōs Japan","u":"Technōs Japan","g":"Role-playing game, board game","r":"June 28, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sugoro_Quest:_Dice_Heroes"},{"t":"Sumo Fighter: Tōkaidō Basho","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"KID","u":"I'Max, DTMC","g":"Platform, beat 'em up, action-adventure","r":"April 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sumo_Fighter:_T%C5%8Dkaid%C5%8D_Basho"},{"t":"Sunset Riders","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Run and gun","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunset_Riders","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2p0v.jpg","s":"Sunset Riders is a side-scrolling run and gun video game developed and released by Konami in 1991. It is set in the American Old West, where the players take control of bounty hunters who are seeking the rewards offered for various criminals. Each bad guy has a big reward on his head, and they're helped by hordes of bandits, gunslingers, and horse thieves, so pack loads of lead."},{"t":"Supaplex","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Think!Ware Development","u":"Dream Factory","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supaplex","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5fur.jpg","s":"Supaplex is the first Boulder Dash-like game that is not fully grid-based: while the playing field is an obvious grid, the objects do not \"snap\" from one grid position to another, but can be halfway or \"in between\" grid positions. Supaplex introduces a number of new elements that were not present in Boulder Dash, including bugs, pieces of base that randomly cause a life threatening electrostatic discharges, Ports, which limit Murphy's movement to specific directions, and terminals, which set off yellow Utility Disks.\n\nUtility Disks are explosive floppy disks and come in three different colors: Orange Disks work like Zonks, but explode when hit or when falling. Yellow Disks do not fall yet may be pushed in any direction, and explode when the Terminal is used. Red Disks can be carried and dropped when convenient, exploding seconds after."},{"t":"Super Baseball 2020","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"Pallas","u":"SNK, Electronic Arts, Electronic Arts Victor, K Amusement Leasing","g":"Sports","r":"September 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Baseball_2020","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co82w7.jpg","s":"Different from real baseball and other baseball video games, this game consists of both male and female characters, as well as robots. All three types of players have the same basic abilities and vary only on their personal strengths. In many cases, human players are better than their robot counterparts. One major difference between the human and robot characters is that the robot characters can blow up (or lose all their power) if they are worked too hard during a game. That can happen if you constantly make them dive for a ball, run extra bases, or even if they get hit by a pitch. Under no stress whatsoever, some robots, especially pitchers, will naturally run out of power as the game progresses. When this happens, they will lose all their abilities and be useless, both offensively and defensively. When that occurs, you can either power them up or replace them with another person on the roster. Human players do not blow up, but human pitchers can tire out. When they are tired, they will pant heavily and throw the ball very slowly. This can be remedied by either an upgrade or, as in real baseball, switching to a relief pitcher."},{"t":"Super Baseball Simulator 1.000","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Culture Brain","u":"Culture Brain","g":"Sports","r":"July 12, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Baseball_Simulator_1.000"},{"t":"Super Bases Loaded","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Jaleco","g":"Sports","r":"May 17, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Bases_Loaded"},{"t":"Super Cars II","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Magnetic Fields","u":"Gremlin Graphics","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Cars_II","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co85lz.jpg","s":"Produced by Magnetic Fields, this game was on the amiga in the uk and was the basis for super cars international on the pc. It is linked to other Magnetic Fields games including: Lotus Esprit Turbo Challenge, Lotus Esprit Turbo Challenge 2, Lotus Esprit Turbo Challenge 3 and Super Cars"},{"t":"Super Castlevania IV","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platform","r":"October 31, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Castlevania_IV","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co20dc.jpg","s":"A century of Transylvanian tranquility is about to come to a shocking end. Once again the mortifying screams of helpless villagers shake the ground as they huddle against new nightmarish horrors unleashed by the Duke of Darkness, Count Dracula. And this time he has a tombstone with your name on it, Simon Belmont.\nYou must descend into Castle of the Undead and its gruesome ground, accompanied by the most chilling sound effects to ever tingle your spine. Inside, a freshly dug 11 levels maze features the treacherous Terrace of Terror, the dangerous Rotating Dungeon, the Sunken Ruins of Lost Spirits, torture chambers and creature filled caves. Use your whip like a grappling hook and swing past hundreds of traps and a host of ghost freaks, living corpses and hidden goblins. All while dodging or destroying the unpredictable spitting lizards, carnivorous coffins, and more.\nFind the concealed weapons needed to defeat everything from eerie phantoms to haunted furniture. Then prepare to find yourself face-to-thing with hideous torments like the Tongue Lasher, the Darkest Knight, the Bone-Afide Horseman and the Granite Crusher. When your nerves get shot, use your password and take a breather. But forge on, Simon Belmont, because if you though Dracula was down for the count, he's only just begun to fright!"},{"t":"Super Chinese 3","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Culture Brain","u":"Culture Brain","g":"Action RPG","r":"March 1, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Chinese_3"},{"t":"Super Ghouls 'n Ghosts","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom, Nintendo","g":"Platform","r":"October 4, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Ghouls_'n_Ghosts","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7run.jpg","s":"As dauntless knight Arthur you are caught up in the most dangerous of quests! A spell has been cast upon the kingdom. Your fair maiden, Princess Prin Prin has been snatched up by the evil emperor. Only as Arthur can you get her back from the creatures of the undead that haunt the land. Test your courage and skill to the limit. You must keep up the quest. On your journey, your mightiest weapon of all awaits you... MAGIC!"},{"t":"Super Ninja Boy","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Culture Brain","u":"Culture Brain","g":"Action RPG","r":"December 28, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Ninja_Boy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4woq.jpg","s":"Super Ninja Boy is an action RPG for one or two players. In a two player game, each player controls either Jack or Ryu. In a one player game, only Jack appears. A second player may join at any time by visiting a convenience store.\n\nThe game contains familiar role-playing elements such as equipping differing kinds of weapons, equipment and gaining levels. The Ninja Boys are treated as a single entity and so these statistics are shared between Jack and Ryu with equal values. Battles with the enemy, either through random encounters or visible on the map is where the action starts. The characters enter a side-scrolling 'battle mode' where they may jump, fight, use their special techniques, items and spells, as well as activate various bonuses on the battlefield by breaking open the bonus boxes. A battle is over and experience points awarded when a pre-determined number of enemies are defeated or the Ninja Boys are defeated.\n\nWhen bosses are encountered, the gameplay changes to a turn-based system. Each turn, commands are given to Jack and Ryu, which include usage of special items and skills acquired during the game, who then perform actions based on those commands. The enemy's turn takes place the same way. The battle ends when one side of the conflict runs out of hitpoints."},{"t":"Super R-Type","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Irem","u":"Irem, Nintendo","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"July 13, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_R-Type","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/coc5ft.jpg","s":"In this SNES sequel to the arcade R-Type shooters, you are the R-9, Earth's most advanced form of defense against the evil BYDO Empire. There are seven stages you must go through, picking up power-up items to defeat the stage bosses which have returned from the arcade versions of R-Type and R-Type II. Your R-9 ship can also be united with FORCE, which are invincible units that surround your ship to protect you and can also power up and attack."},{"t":"Super R.C. Pro-Am","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Rare","u":"Nintendo","g":"Racing","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_R.C._Pro-Am"},{"t":"Super Soccer","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Human Entertainment","u":"Human Entertainment, Nintendo","g":"Sports, football","r":"December 13, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Soccer","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6bjg.jpg","s":"Let's play soccer - the world's favorite sport!\n\nIn Super Soccer you can choose between an Exhibition mode and a Tournament mode. While the first mode lasts a single game only the Tournament mode challenges you with 16 consecutive games, facing the Nintendo Allstar Team in the final round.\n\nThe field is shown in a top-down perspective. Scrolling is done by Mode 7 chip. Difficulty varies with the opponent team, ranging from easy to hard in later games in the tournament.\n\nAfter winning a match in the tournament mode, you will be rewarded witch a code in order to pick up the game in the same round of the tournament later on."},{"t":"Super Tennis","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tose","u":"Tonkin House, Nintendo, Playtronic","g":"Sports","r":"August 30, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Tennis","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6bjh.jpg","s":"Super Tennis is a 1991 tennis video game for the Super NES. It released at early points in the Super Nintendo's shelf lives and utilized mode 7."},{"t":"Super Tetris","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","Amiga"],"d":"Spectrum HoloByte","u":"Spectrum HoloByte","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super_Tetris"},{"t":"Switchblade II","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Gremlin Graphics, Core Design, Optimus Software","u":"Gremlin Graphics, Re-release, GBH Gold, Original release","g":"Action-platform, run and gun","r":"May 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Switchblade_II"},{"t":"SWIV","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Acorn Archimedes","ZX Spectrum","Amstrad CPC","Commodore 64","MSX"],"d":"The Sales Curve","u":"Storm","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SWIV","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2jl6.jpg","s":"SWIV is a vertical scrolling shooter and an unofficial follow-up to Sales Curve's conversion of the side scroller Tecmo arcade game Silkworm.\nOne player controls a helicopter and the other a jeep, with the jeep transforming into a boat when the vehicles pass over water. As well as a host of blaster fodder the game also includes a mid-level enemy inspired by the \"Goose\" enemy from the earlier title Silkworm. Pieces of this enemy would fly onto the screen and assemble into one vehicle before commencing to fire at the players' vehicles. When destroyed the \"goose\" would drop power-ups."},{"t":"T2: The Arcade Game","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Midway","u":"Midway, Tec Toy","g":"Shooter, Arcade","r":"October 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/t2-the-arcade-game","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4eb9.jpg","s":"As the title states, this is the home conversion of the arcade rail-shooter based on the film Terminator 2: Judgement Day. Up to two players shoot through future and present levels as robotic killers reprogrammed to serve the human resistance.\n\nBoth players wield a machine gun with infinite ammo that lowers its firing rate (overheats) as it is continuously fired. A secondary weapon (missile launchers in the future, shotguns in the present) has limited ammo but deals heavy damage. Powerups inside the game world include secondary weapon ammo and coolant for the machine guns, and are shot to be collected.\n\nT2: The Arcade Game features seven levels based on specific scenes or general concepts in the film. The first four levels act as a prelude, as the player guns down waves of metal Terminators across a post-apocalyptic Battlefield, a besieged Human Hideout, and through the security checkpoints of the enemy supercomputer SkyNet. After destroying the computer, players travel back in time to protect John and Sarah Connor, while also destroying every scrap of SkyNet research at the headquarters of its manufacturer. From there, players must fend off the indestructible T-1000 until the final showdown in the Steel Mill.\n\nAssets from the movie are used when possible, such as voice clips from Arnold Schwarzenegger, and close-up digital stills of Robert Patrick for the final battle against the T-1000. The Genesis and SNES versions also support lightguns (the Menacer and Super Scope, respectively)."},{"t":"Tail 'Gator","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Natsume","u":"VAP, Natsume","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tail_'Gator"},{"t":"TaleSpin","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TaleSpin_(Capcom_video_game)"},{"t":"Tangram","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Thalion Software","u":"Thalion Software","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tangram_(video_game)"},{"t":"Tank Force","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Namco","u":"Namco","g":"Multidirectional shooter","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tank_Force"},{"t":"Task Force Harrier","p":["Arcade","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"NMK, Jorudan","u":"UPL, Sammy, Treco","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Task_Force_Harrier"},{"t":"Team Suzuki","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Gremlin Graphics","u":"Gremlin Graphics, Gremlin / Konami","g":"Racing simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Team_Suzuki_(video_game)"},{"t":"Tecmo Super Bowl","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Tecmo","u":"Tecmo","g":"Sports","r":"December 13, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tecmo_Super_Bowl","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co515k.jpg","s":"The teams, the players, and the halftime shows are ready for the ultimate football season, and you're in control. From the first pre-season game to the playoffs, it's your call, your skill, and your team. Can you get to and win the Super Bowl? Tecmo Super Bowl is the only football game to feature onside kicks, blocked passes and kicks, fumbles, pitchout to the running back, player injuries, time-outs, and much, much more! For 1 or 2 players. All 28 NFL teams! Four running plays, four passing plays. Team stats and data. Create your own Pro Bowl. Coach Mode, where you call the plays!"},{"t":"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles II: Back from the Sewers","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Action, platform","r":"November 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teenage_Mutant_Ninja_Turtles_II:_Back_from_the_Sewers","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5gu7.jpg","s":"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles II: Back from the Sewers, released as Teenage Mutant Hero Turtles II: Back from the Sewers in Europe, and simply Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles 2 in Japan, is a 1991 Game Boy game by Konami. It is the sequel to Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles: Fall of the Foot Clan."},{"t":"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles III: The Manhattan Project","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"December 13, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teenage_Mutant_Ninja_Turtles_III:_The_Manhattan_Project","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3wou.jpg","s":"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles III: The Manhattan Project is a side-scrolling beat-'em-up. The game features play mechanics similar to the previous game, Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles II: The Arcade Game, but it is an original title for the NES without any preceding arcade version."},{"t":"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles: Manhattan Missions","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Distinctive Software","u":"Konami","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"June 1, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teenage_Mutant_Ninja_Turtles:_Manhattan_Missions"},{"t":"Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles: Turtles in Time","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teenage_Mutant_Ninja_Turtles:_Turtles_in_Time","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co987w.jpg","s":"The original arcade version of Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles: Turtles in Time, which is shorter than the latter console version."},{"t":"Teki Paki","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Toaplan","u":"Tecmo, Honest Trading Co., Spacy Co. Ltd","g":"Puzzle","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teki_Paki"},{"t":"Tenchi wo Kurau II: Shokatsu Kōmei Den","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Role-playing","r":"April 5, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tenchi_wo_Kurau_II:_Shokatsu_K%C5%8Dmei_Den"},{"t":"Terminator 2","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","MS-DOS","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Dementia","u":"Ocean Software","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terminator_2_(computer_game)"},{"t":"Terminator 2","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Bits Studios","u":"LJN","g":"Action-adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terminator_2_(Game_Boy_video_game)"},{"t":"Terminator 2: Judgment Day","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Midway","u":"Midway","g":"Light gun shooter","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terminator_2:_Judgment_Day_(arcade_game)"},{"t":"Tex Murphy: Martian Memorandum","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Access Software","u":"Access Software, Nightdive Studios","g":"Point-and-click, Puzzle, Adventure","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/tex-murphy-martian-memorandum","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5zo6.jpg","s":"The year is 2033. Your name is Tex Murphy, Private Investigator in San Francisco. You've been hired by the beautiful daughter of a university professor to uncover the facts about his death. Beginning your investigation you uncover the deaths of several prominent members of the scientific community. Are these deaths coincidental or is there something more sinister going on?\n\nAnd when you'll end this case, you will have a chance to try yourself in the next one, this time on... Mars!"},{"t":"The Adventures of Quik & Silva","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Kaiko","u":"Amiga Fun","g":"Platform","r":"May 10, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Quik_&_Silva"},{"t":"The Adventures of Rad Gravity","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Interplay Productions","u":"Activision","g":"Platformer","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Rad_Gravity","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5agl.jpg","s":"The Adventures of Rad Gravity is a video game for the Nintendo Entertainment System developed by Interplay and published by Activision in 1990. The game is a platformer with a wacky storyline in which the eponymous main character explored multiple planets and fought enemies while jumping through levels. The game was notable for the planet Turvia, where gravity works in reverse. In that level the protagonist was drawn upside down, as were the enemies and the entire screen. The main character has a comically large head and strongly resembles actor Bruce Campbell. His early game attack consists of a sort of a lightsaber."},{"t":"The Adventures of Robin Hood","p":["MS-DOS","Atari ST","Amiga"],"d":"Millennium Interactive","u":"Millennium Interactive","g":"RPG, adventure","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Robin_Hood_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Adventures of Star Saver","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"A-Wave","u":"King Records, Taito","g":"Platform","r":"May 17, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Star_Saver"},{"t":"The Adventures of Willy Beamish","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Dynamix","u":"Sierra On-Line, Activision","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Willy_Beamish","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2t8b.jpg","s":"Willy Beamish is a kid who likes to goof off and have fun. Naturally all the grown-ups get in the way by making him go to school, making him clean his room, etc. But now that's school's out for the summer, Willy is determined to have a good time and maybe even have his pet frog win the frog jumping contest. However Willy has a habit of getting into trouble, and naturally that means it will be a challenge to get out of it.\n\nThe Adventures of Willy Beamish is a point-and-click graphical adventure. You play Willy Beamish, an eight year-old kid who is just trying to get through life without losing his lunch money. But he'll have to deal with parents, teachers, babysitters and bratty sisters. The player must solve different puzzles to ensure Willy can progress, get out of trouble and generally avoid ending up grounded.\n\nThe in-game time progresses even if no action is taken. This means many puzzles have to be solved in a certain time frame or rely on being in the right place at the right time. A special aspect of this game is a bar which shows Willy's relationship to his parents. It gets affected by the way certain situations are resolved, e.g. it increases if Willy refuses to play with his sister, and when it is full they send Willy to a military school and the game is lost."},{"t":"The Amazing Spider-Man vs. The Kingpin","p":["Sega Master System","Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Technopop","u":"Sega, Tec Toy","g":"Platform","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/the-amazing-spider-man-vs-the-kingpin","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co85oo.jpg","s":"Spider-Man vs. The Kingpin is a video game produced by Sega of America and developed by Technopop initially on the Mega Drive/Genesis. It was ported internally by Sega of America for the Master System and Game Gear consoles, the latter being published by Acclaim Entertainment through its Flying Edge division. An updated version was also released for the Sega CD under the name of The Amazing Spider-Man vs. The Kingpin.\n\nAll versions of the video game are side-scrolling platform games, whereby the player controls superhero Spider-Man to battle various supervillains (Doctor Octopus, Sandman, The Lizard, Hobgoblin, Vulture, Mysterio, Electro, and Venom) to obtain the keys needed to disarm a nuclear bomb that the Kingpin has not only framed Spider-Man for stealing but plans to detonate within twenty-four hours. In the middle of the game, Spider-Man's wife Mary Jane Watson is also kidnapped by Venom."},{"t":"The Atlas","p":["PC-9800 series","FM Towns","MS-DOS"],"d":"Artdink","u":"Artdink, Pack-In-Video","g":"Simulation w/ strategy elements","r":"August 2, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Atlas_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Bard's Tale Construction Set","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Interplay","u":"Interplay","g":"Role-playing, game creation system","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bard's_Tale_Construction_Set"},{"t":"The Berlin Wall","p":["Arcade","Game Gear"],"d":"Kaneko, Inter State","u":"Kaneko","g":"Platform","r":"November 29, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Berlin_Wall_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Blue Marlin","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Hot-B","u":"Hot-B","g":"Fishing","r":"December 27, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blue_Marlin"},{"t":"The Blues Brothers","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga","Commodore 64","Atari ST"],"d":"Titus France","u":"Titus France","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blues_Brothers_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5ago.jpg","s":"The Blues Brothers video game was designed by Titus Software and is based on the comedic sketch band of the same name. The platformer-style game was released in 1991 for MS-DOS. It was subsequently ported to a number of other platforms, including the Amiga, Atari ST, and NES."},{"t":"The Brainies","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","MS-DOS","Apple IIGS","macOS"],"d":"Atreid Concept, Titus France<","u":"Loriciel, Titus France","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Brainies"},{"t":"The Bugs Bunny Crazy Castle 2","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Kemco","u":"Kemco, Seika, Nintendo, Laguna Video Games","g":"Action","r":"April 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bugs_Bunny_Crazy_Castle_2"},{"t":"The Cardinal of the Kremlin","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Capstone Software","u":"IntraCorp, Accolade","g":"Government simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Cardinal_of_the_Kremlin_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Cyber Shinobi","p":["Sega Master System"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Platform, hack and slash","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Cyber_Shinobi","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co30io.jpg","s":"The Cyber Shinobi is the SEGA Master System exclusive sequel to Shinobi. In some context the game has the subtitle Shinobi Part 2. It was the first Shinobi game to not have an Arcade counterpart."},{"t":"The Fidelity Ultimate Chess Challenge","p":["Atari Lynx"],"d":"Telegames","u":"Telegames","g":"Chess","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Fidelity_Ultimate_Chess_Challenge"},{"t":"The Flash","p":["Sega Master System","Game Boy"],"d":"Probe Software, Equilibrium","u":"Sega, THQ","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Flash_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Flintstones","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","MSX","Sega Master System","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Teque Software Development, Tiertex","u":"Grandslam Entertainments","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Flintstones_(1988_video_game)"},{"t":"The Flintstones: The Rescue of Dino & Hoppy","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Taito, Sol Corporation","u":"Taito","g":"Action platformer","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Flintstones:_The_Rescue_of_Dino_&_Hoppy","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6v38.jpg","s":"It's prehistoric pandemonium as your favorite cave family, the Flintstones, get a visit from the 30th century. The clever scientist, Dr. Butler, needs some dinosaurs for the Orbit City Zoo, and he's invented a time machine to capture Dino and Hoppy. Can Fred find all the pieces that the Great Gazoo needs to build a time machine of his own? Can he make a jump into the future with the help of Wilma, Barney and Betty and rescue his prehistoric pals? Who says history has to be boring?"},{"t":"The G.G. Shinobi","p":["Game Gear"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Platform, hack and slash","r":"April 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_G.G._Shinobi"},{"t":"The Godfather","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Creative Materials","u":"U.S. Gold","g":"Run and gun","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Godfather_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"The Gold Sheet: Pro Football Analyst","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gold_Sheet:_Pro_Football_Analyst"},{"t":"The Hunt for Red October","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Beam Software, Riedel Software Productions","u":"Hi-Tech Expressions, Inc.","g":"Side scroller","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Hunt_for_Red_October_(console_game)"},{"t":"The King of Dragons","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Beat 'em up, action role-playing","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_King_of_Dragons","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co1y8f.jpg","s":"The game has 16 levels, though many are quite short. Much like Capcom's Knights of the Round, King of Dragons features an role-playing video game-like level advancement system. Points scored for killing monsters and picking up gold count towards experience, and the character gains levels at regular intervals. With each level, the character's health bar increases, other attributes such as range improve, and the character also becomes invulnerable for a few seconds. Along the way, different weapon and armor upgrades for each character may also be picked up.\n\nKing of Dragons features a simple control system that consists of a single attack button, and a jump button. By pressing both buttons, the character unleashes a magical attack that strikes all enemies in screen (its strength varies according to the character used) at the expense of losing energy. The fighter, cleric and dwarf can also use their shield to block certain attacks by tilting the joystick back right before the impact.\n\nThis is one of the many Capcom games to feature the yashichi power-up item. There are three such power-ups hidden throughout the game, each giving the player an extra continue."},{"t":"The Legend of the Mystical Ninja","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Action-adventure","r":"July 19, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_the_Mystical_Ninja"},{"t":"The Legend of Zelda: A Link to the Past","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nintendo EAD","u":"Nintendo","g":"Action-adventure","r":"November 21, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Legend_of_Zelda:_A_Link_to_the_Past","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3vzn.jpg","s":"Venture back to Hyrule and an age of magic and heroes. The predecessors of Link and Zelda face monsters on the march when a menacing magician takes over the kingdom. Only you can prevent his evil plot from shattering the land of Hyrule.\n\nIn your quest, you'll venture into twisting mazes, dungeons, palaces and shadowy forests. Test your mettle with mighty swords and magical weapons. Or heft a boulder and hurl it at your enemies. If the going gets tough, dive into a river - you can swim to escape! Learn powerful spells, locate magical artifacts and solve the mysteries of the evil magician and the hidden realm of Hyrule."},{"t":"The Little Mermaid","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Game Boy"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Action","r":"July 19, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Little_Mermaid_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Lone Ranger","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Action-adventure","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Lone_Ranger_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Lost Treasures of Infocom","p":["Amiga","Apple IIGS","macOS","MS-DOS"],"d":"Infocom","u":"Activision","g":"Interactive fiction","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Lost_Treasures_of_Infocom"},{"t":"The Lucky Dime Caper Starring Donald Duck","p":["Game Gear","Sega Master System"],"d":"Sega","u":"Sega","g":"Platform","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Lucky_Dime_Caper_Starring_Donald_Duck","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co7tyb.jpg","s":"Sorceress Magica De Spell and her rotten ravens have kidnapped Huey, Dewey and Louie! Worse yet, she's snatched Uncle Scrooge's Lucky Dime! Join Donald Duck in his quest to prove that Magica is no match for him."},{"t":"The Magic Candle II: The Four and Forty","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Mindcraft","u":"Electronic Arts","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Magic_Candle_II:_The_Four_and_Forty"},{"t":"The Magnetic Scrolls Collection","p":["Acorn Archimedes","Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Magnetic Scrolls","u":"Virgin Games, Inc.","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Magnetic_Scrolls_Collection"},{"t":"The Ninja Kids","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Taito","u":"Taito","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ninja_Kids"},{"t":"The Oath","p":["Amiga"],"d":"Attic Entertainment","u":"Attic Entertainment","g":"Scrolling shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Oath_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Palace of Deceit","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Game Syndicate Productions","u":"InnerVision Software","g":"Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Palace_of_Deceit"},{"t":"The Perfect General","p":["MS-DOS","Amiga"],"d":"White Wolf Productions","u":"Quantum Quality Productions, Ubi Soft, Kirin Entertainment, Killer Bee Software","g":"Wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Perfect_General"},{"t":"The Punisher","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"LJN","g":"Rail shooter","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Punisher_(1991_NES_video_game)"},{"t":"The Punisher: The Ultimate Payback!","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Beam Software","u":"Acclaim Entertainment","g":"Shoot 'em up","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Punisher:_The_Ultimate_Payback!"},{"t":"The Return of Medusa","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"X-Ample","u":"Starbyte Software","g":"Role-playing, strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Return_of_Medusa"},{"t":"The Rocketeer","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Realtime Associates, Ironwind Software","u":"Bandai","g":"Platform","r":"May 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Rocketeer_(NES_video_game)"},{"t":"The Shadow of Yserbius","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Ybarra Productions","u":"Sierra On-Line","g":"MUD, RPG","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Shadow_of_Yserbius"},{"t":"The Simpsons","p":["Arcade","Commodore 64","MS-DOS"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"March 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Simpsons_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Simpsons Arcade Game","p":["Arcade","Commodore 64","MS-DOS"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Hack and slash/Beat 'em up, Arcade","r":"March 4, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/the-simpsons-arcade-game","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co497q.jpg","s":"The Simpsons is basically a side-scrolling beat-em-up featuring the characters from The Simpsons TV show. The player can choose to play as each of the four Simpson characters. Homer, who punches and kicks; Marge, who swings her vacuum cleaner; Bart, who wields his skateboard; and Lisa, who uses a jump rope as a whip. Other weapons such as hammers and bowling balls are also available to use.\n\nThe game's levels are Downtown Springfield, Krustyland, Springfield Discount Cemetery, Moe's Tavern, Springfield Gorge, Dreamland, the Channel 6 studio, and the Power Plant. Each level ends with a boss whose speed and strength will increase after taking a certain amount of damage.\n\nBetween levels, the player can compete against the other characters in two mini-games to be first to complete a task (inflating a balloon and waking up their character)."},{"t":"The Simpsons: Bart vs. the Space Mutants","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System","Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Imagineering","u":"Acclaim Entertainment, Ocean Software","g":"Platform","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Simpsons:_Bart_vs._the_Space_Mutants","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co49fo.jpg","s":"Bart vs. The Space Mutants is a platform game where the player goes into the role of Bart Simpson (from the TV show The Simpsons) and must stop the Space Mutants from invading Springfield. On each of the five levels, Bart must collect (or get rid of) the ingredients that the Space Mutants are planning to use to build their machine, such as purple objects or balloons. He also has to collect enough \"proof\" of the aliens existence (brown coins left behind when they are jumped on), so his family members will help him when he meets a boss (characters such as Nelson and Sideshow Bob). This won't be easy since the Space Mutants are \"using\" human bodies as disguise. In order to discover who are the real Space Mutants, Bart must use his X-ray Specs."},{"t":"The Simpsons: Bart vs. the World","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Imagineering","u":"Acclaim Entertainment, Virgin Interactive Entertainment","g":"Platform","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Simpsons:_Bart_vs._the_World","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co49fk.jpg","s":"The Simpsons: Bart vs. the World is a side-scrolling platform that compiles smaller games belonging to different genres. In each stage, Bart must navigate through the area, collecting items such as firecracker balls for self-defense and Squishees to restore health By grabbing a cape, Bart can become his superheroic alter-ego, Bartman, and fly for limited periods. The most important items in each stage are Krusty-brand souvenirs. There is one in every stage, and Bart must find them all in order to get the best ending. There are also several mini-games in each area, with puzzles such as a matching-card game and a trivia game based on events in actual episodes."},{"t":"The Space Adventure","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Hudson Soft","u":"Hudson Soft","g":"Adventure","r":"June 7, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Space_Adventure_(video_game)"},{"t":"The Sword of Hope","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Kemco","u":"Kemco, Seika Corporation","g":"Role-playing","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sword_of_Hope"},{"t":"The Terminator (DOS)","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Bethesda Softworks","u":"Bethesda Softworks","g":"Action-adventure","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Terminator_(DOS)"},{"t":"The Treehouse","p":["MS-DOS","Apple II","macOS","FM Towns","Windows"],"d":"Broderbund","u":"Broderbund","g":"Educational","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Treehouse_(video_game)"},{"t":"Thrash Rally","p":["Arcade","Neo Geo AES"],"d":"Alpha Denshi","u":"SNK, ADK","g":"Racing","r":"November 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrash_Rally"},{"t":"Three Wonders","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom, Xing Entertainment","g":"Platformer, scrolling shooter, puzzle","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three_Wonders"},{"t":"Thunder Cross II","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunder_Cross_II"},{"t":"Thunder Dragon","p":["Arcade"],"d":"NMK","u":"Tecmo","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"August 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunder_Dragon"},{"t":"Time Cruise","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Face","u":"Face, Turbo Technologies Inc.","g":"Pinball","r":"November 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_Cruise"},{"t":"Time Traveler","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Virtual Image Productions, GTE Interactive Media","u":"Sega, Digital Leisure","g":"Interactive movie","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_Traveler_(video_game)"},{"t":"Time Twist: Rekishi no Katasumi de...","p":["Famicom Disk System"],"d":"Nintendo R&D4, Pax Softnica","u":"Nintendo","g":"Adventure","r":"July 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_Twist:_Rekishi_no_Katasumi_de..."},{"t":"Timequest","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Legend Entertainment","u":"Legend Entertainment","g":"Interactive fiction, Adventure","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timequest"},{"t":"Tiny Toon Adventures","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platformer","r":"December 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiny_Toon_Adventures_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2rp2.jpg","s":"Tiny Toon Adventures is a platform video game for the NES. It was published and developed by Konami and released in 1991. It is the first Tiny Toon Adventures video game to be released for a video game console."},{"t":"Tip Off","p":["Amiga","Atari 2600","Game Boy"],"d":"Anco Software","u":"Imagineer","g":"Sports game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tip_Off"},{"t":"Titus the Fox","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","MS-DOS","Game Boy"],"d":"Titus France","u":"Titus France","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Titus_the_Fox"},{"t":"ToeJam & Earl","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Johnson Voorsanger Productions","u":"Sega","g":"Action, roguelike","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ToeJam_&_Earl","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6yry.jpg","s":"ToeJam & Earl is an early 1990s dungeon crawler. The game features two alien rappers, who have crash-landed on Earth and parodies 1980s urban culture. The game's design was heavily influenced by the role-playing video game Rogue, and took from it such features as the random generation of levels and items. Played from a 2D-top-down perspective the game contains both single-player and two-player cooperative modes. The latter displays a single screen when both characters are near each other, but splits it apart when they are not. Playing the game with two players reveals dialogue and jokes between the characters not heard in the single player game."},{"t":"Tom & Jerry: The Ultimate Game of Cat and Mouse!","p":["MS-DOS","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Albino Frog Software, Software Creations","u":"Hi Tech Expressions, Altron","g":"Platform","r":"December 1, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/tom-and-jerry-the-ultimate-game-of-cat-and-mouse","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6oxr.jpg","s":"Tom has mouse-napped Jerry's nephew Tuffy and locked him in a trunk in the attic. You and your furry little buddy Jerry must dash, jump, and climb from the basement up through every level of the house to rescue him in time.\n\nJerry's main defense is his unlimited supply of marbles. Pick up other items such as bubble gum, meat cleaver, cups of water, moth balls, hammer, invisible ink and drill to overcome the enemies and obstacles in your way. Make sure you outwit Tom whenever he appears.\n\nLook for cheese power-ups and mouse hole bonus stages along the way."},{"t":"Tom and Jerry","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Riedel Software Productions","u":"Playtronic, Hi Tech Expressions","g":"Platform","r":"December 1, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/tom-and-jerry","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4hg9.jpg","s":"Based on MGM's world-known cartoon, the player controls Jerry, the Mouse (in 2-Player Mode, P2 will control Jerry's nephew, Tuffy) as he explores four different worlds (movie theater, junkyard, a toy-based stage and the house inner). Game controls are simple: B Button makes Jerry/Tuffy to jump (tapping it again lightly upon landing will activate their Super Jumps), while A/Y/X Buttons will make them to throw marbles upward (Y Button) and downward (X/Y Buttons) in the enemies.\n\nTo earn extra lives throughout the game, players have two available options: the rarest and, sometimes, hardest way is to find a 1-Up icon; however, the easiest mode is to collect 100 of small balls of cheese scattered throughout the levels (Cheese Bits). Also, Jerry and Tuffy's health meters are displayed in the form of four hearts, that can be replenished through collectible larger cheese wedges (Cheese Chunks). At the end of each world, Jerry and Tuffy will take a confrontation with Tom, the Cat."},{"t":"Tony La Russa Baseball","p":["Commodore 64","Genesis / Mega Drive","MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Beyond Software, Inc.","u":"Strategic Simulations, Inc., Maxis, Electronic Arts, Inc.","g":"Sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_La_Russa_Baseball"},{"t":"Tony La Russa's Ultimate Baseball","p":["Commodore 64","MS-DOS"],"d":"Beyond Software, Inc.","u":"Strategic Simulations, Inc.","g":"Sport","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_La_Russa's_Ultimate_Baseball"},{"t":"Top Banana","p":["Acorn Archimedes","Amiga CDTV","Atari ST"],"u":"Hex","g":"Platform","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Top_Banana_(video_game)"},{"t":"Top Gun: Danger Zone","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Distinctive Software","u":"Konami","g":"Combat flight simulator","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Top_Gun:_Danger_Zone"},{"t":"Track Meet","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Interplay Productions","u":"Interplay Productions, Hiro","g":"Olympic sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Track_Meet"},{"t":"Traders","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"André Wüthrich","u":"Merit Software","g":"Business simulation","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traders_(video_game)"},{"t":"Tranquility","p":["macOS","Windows"],"d":"William A. Romanowski","u":"TQworld, LLC.","g":"Platformer, Music, Art","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tranquility_(video_game)"},{"t":"Trax","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"HAL Laboratory","u":"HAL Laboratory, Electro Brain","g":"Shooter","r":"January 8, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trax_(video_game)"},{"t":"Treasure Master","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Software Creations","u":"ASC Games","g":"Platform","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treasure_Master"},{"t":"Trex Warrior","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Thalion Software","u":"Thalion Software","g":"Action, sports","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trex_Warrior"},{"t":"Tri Peaks","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tri_Peaks_(game)"},{"t":"Tricky Kick","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Alfa System","u":"IGS","g":"Puzzle","r":"January 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tricky_Kick"},{"t":"Troika","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Paragon Software","u":"Paragon Software","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Troika_(video_game)"},{"t":"True Golf Classics: Waialae Country Club","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","PC-9800 series"],"d":"T&E Soft","u":"T&E Soft, Sega","g":"Traditional golf simulation","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/True_Golf_Classics:_Waialae_Country_Club"},{"t":"Trump Castle II","p":["Amiga","MS-DOS"],"d":"Capstone Software","u":"Capstone Software","g":"Gambling","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trump_Castle_II"},{"t":"Tsuppari Wars","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sammy","u":"Sammy","g":"Action","r":"June 28, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsuppari_Wars"},{"t":"Tumblepop","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Data East","u":"Data East, Leprechaun Inc., Mitchell Corporation","g":"Platform","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tumblepop","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co314w.jpg","s":"Tumblepop is a 1991 platform arcade video game developed by Data East first published in Japan by Namco, then in North America by Leprechaun Inc. and later in Europe by Mitchell Corporation. Starring two ghosthunters, players are tasked with travelling across different countries, capturing enemies and throwing them as bouncing ball, jumping on and off platforms to navigate level obstacles while dodging and defeating monsters in order to save the world."},{"t":"Turbo Charge","p":["Commodore 64","Amiga","Atari ST"],"u":"System 3","g":"Racing game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turbo_Charge"},{"t":"Turbo Force","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Video System","u":"Namco, Mc O' River","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turbo_Force"},{"t":"Turrican II: The Final Fight","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","Amstrad CPC","Amiga CDTV","Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Rainbow Arts, Factor 5, Enigma Variations, Sun-Project","u":"Rainbow Arts, Accolade","g":"Run and gun","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turrican_II:_The_Final_Fight","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2l0r.jpg","s":"Turrican II: The Final Fight is a platformer/shooter as typically seen on consoles, but designed for home computers from the ground up.\n\nIts plot is - naturally - very simple: an evil mega-robot called The Machine attacks the United Planets Ship Avalon 1, slaughtering all who resist. All - except Bren McGuire, who manages to escape and slips into the experimental Turrican bionic armor. As Turrican, he strives to show The Machine who's the boss.\n\nUnlike other games of its type, this game contains three levels of horizontal shooter action in the spirit of R-Type or the developers' own Katakis. Six large worlds are to be explored, where you are basically free to go everywhere you want, since there is no automatic scrolling and the levels are packed with hidden extra lives and weapons. All those levels are very different: the first one in the rock desert is pretty colorful with parallaxing rainbow background and happy music, while the following worlds get darker and darker."},{"t":"Twilight: 2000","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Paragon Software","u":"MicroProse","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twilight:_2000_(video_game)"},{"t":"Tyrants: Fight Through Time","p":["Atari ST","Amiga"],"d":"Sensible Software","u":"Image Works, Imagineer, Virgin Games, CSK Research Institute, Tec Toy","g":"Real Time Strategy (RTS), Simulator, Strategy","r":"December 31, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/tyrants-fight-through-time","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5zfs.jpg","s":"First you choose one out of four different characters to represent you, Scarlet (red army), Oberon (yellow army), Caesar (green army), or Madcap (blue army). The game is split into 10 levels - the first 9 are groups of 3 territories which can be completed in any order, while the final 'Mother of All Battles' has its own surprises.\n\nLand is mostly of sand, grass or ice. You start with 100 men and you can use whatever quantity you find necessary on each island. The rest men left will go to another level with another new 100. In the second half of the game you can freeze men to save them for the Mother of All Battles. Once you select your island and place for your HQ, you must do some research for better weapons and defences. On the roof of your buildings you can put protectors like archers, gunmen, boiling oil men, and later on even laser turrets. If you send a team of unarmed men into an unoccupied square, they will build an additional base there, with larger armies completing this more quickly.\n\nLater on higher levels, you'll also build mines and factories, as you'll need to dig a certain material for higher level weapons, depending on whether the area you're on is rich with a certain material or not. With each next level, you'll start from a higher technology level and be able to progress to one higher level up than at the previous level"},{"t":"Ufouria: The Saga","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Sunsoft","u":"Sunsoft","g":"Action-adventure","r":"September 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ufouria:_The_Saga"},{"t":"Ultima: Runes of Virtue","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Origin Systems","u":"Pony Canyon, FCI","g":"Role-playing","r":"December 14, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultima:_Runes_of_Virtue"},{"t":"Ultima: Worlds of Adventure 2: Martian Dreams","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Origin Systems","u":"Origin Systems","g":"Role-playing","r":"May 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultima:_Worlds_of_Adventure_2:_Martian_Dreams","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co4atv.jpg","s":"I write these words aboard a colossal bullet rocketing from the Earth to Mars. Wondrous as this is (and it is, I assure you, quite wondrous), I am even more amazed by the fact that it is the year 1895! Tesla - yes, the noted scientist of years past, Nikola Tesla! -- is unsure how long the voyage will take so I have decided to pass the time recording what mankind (circa 1895) knows of our destination - Mars. How I came by this information is a story in itself."},{"t":"Ultra Golf","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami, Ultra Games","g":"Sports","r":"November 1, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultra_Golf"},{"t":"Ultraman: Towards the Future","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Nova","u":"Bandai","g":"Fighting","r":"April 6, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultraman:_Towards_the_Future_(video_game)"},{"t":"Utopia: The Creation of a Nation","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Celestial Software","u":"Gremlin Interactive, Jaleco","g":"Real-time strategy, City-building game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utopia:_The_Creation_of_a_Nation","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co5zfw.jpg","s":"Utopia takes the Sim City gameplay concept and adds a military element to it. The game is set in the distant future, where your job is nothing less than Colony Administrator for a new settlement on some far-flung planet. Your aim is to provide the highest Quality of Life rating for the colonists.\n\nYou do so by ensuring that there is enough life support, power, housing, employment, police and entertainment to keep the citizens of your colony happy. Hindering your goal are random events such as solar eclipses (which can render your solar panels inactive) and also a different, hostile alien race that inhabits the planets in the many scenarios at offer.\n\nNot only do you have to be a good mayor but you also have to be a good general. Resources must not only be attributed to civilian research but into weapons research, intelligence and the building of tanks and spaceships, if you wish to survive long enough to see your colony flourish into a metropolis. As a rule, you will have built a strong colony before the aliens come, but the final push towards a QOL of 80% or higher can only be achieved once there is no outside threat."},{"t":"V for Victory: D-Day Utah Beach","p":["MS-DOS","macOS"],"d":"Atomic Games","u":"Three-Sixty Pacific","g":"Computer wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V_for_Victory:_D-Day_Utah_Beach"},{"t":"Valis IV","p":["PC Engine CD"],"d":"Laser Soft","u":"Telenet Japan, Atlus Software","g":"Action, platform","r":"August 23, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valis_IV"},{"t":"Vandyke","p":["Arcade"],"d":"UPL","u":"UPL","g":"Hack and slash","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vandyke_(video_game)"},{"t":"Vendetta","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Beat 'em up","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vendetta_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Vengeance of Excalibur","p":["MS-DOS","macOS","Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"Synergistic Software","u":"Virgin Games","g":"Role-playing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vengeance_of_Excalibur"},{"t":"Verytex","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Opera House","u":"Asmik Ace Entertainment","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"April 5, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verytex"},{"t":"Vice: Project Doom","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Aicom","u":"Sammy Corporation","g":"Platformer, driving, shooter","r":"April 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vice:_Project_Doom","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3wp2.jpg","s":"Vice: Project Doom is a game that features side-scrolling platform sequences, overhead driving modes and rail-shooter stages. In driving stages Hart's vehicle can accelerate or slow down and fire his weapon straight ahead. During side-scrolling sequences, Hart can jump, duck, climb or use his selected weapon. Weapons that can be selected include a Katana sword, a pistol and a grenade-type weapon. These weapons feature limited ammunition with the exception of the katana. Power-ups include ammunition, coins, and health. In rail-shooting stages, similar to Operation Wolf, Hart can shoot enemies with his gun or throw grenades and pick up items by shooting at them."},{"t":"Videomation","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"FarSight Studios","u":"Toy Headquarters","g":"Productivity","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Videomation"},{"t":"Viking Child","p":["Atari ST","Amiga","Atari Lynx","Game Boy","MS-DOS"],"d":"Imagitec Design Limited","u":"Atari Corporation","g":"Action","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viking_Child"},{"t":"Vimana","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Toaplan","u":"Tecmo, Toaplan","g":"Vertically scrolling shooter","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vimana_(video_game)"},{"t":"Virtuality (product)","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtuality_(product)"},{"t":"Viz: The Game","p":["Commodore 64","ZX Spectrum","Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Windows"],"u":"Virgin Interactive","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viz:_The_Game"},{"t":"Vroom","p":["Amiga","Atari ST","MS-DOS"],"d":"Lankhor","u":"Lankhor, Ubi Soft","g":"Racing","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vroom_(video_game)"},{"t":"Wacky Races","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Atlus","g":"Platform","r":"December 25, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/wacky-races--1","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3zx5.jpg","s":"Wacky Races is a platform game for the Nintendo Entertainment System, developed and published by Atlus. The game is based on the Hanna-Barbera cartoon Wacky Races and features Muttley and Dick Dastardly as the main characters.\n\nAn unrelated Amiga and Atari ST game of the same name was released in 1991 and a racing game of the same name came out in 2000."},{"t":"Wai Wai World 2: SOS!! Parsley Jō","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platform","r":"January 5, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wai_Wai_World_2:_SOS!!_Parsley_J%C5%8D"},{"t":"Warrior of Rome","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Micronet","u":"Micronet","g":"Real-time strategy","r":"February 24, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warrior_of_Rome"},{"t":"Warsong","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","PC Engine CD","Windows"],"d":"Masaya Games","u":"Nippon Computer Systems, Treco","g":"Tactical role-playing game","r":"April 26, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warsong","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3fv7.jpg","s":"\"The Dalsis Empire has laid seige upon the Castle of Baltia to seize the legendary sword WARSONG. In a desperate attempt, King Alfador sends his only son, Prince Garett, into the countryside, away from impending death.\nNow Garett must rally his allies from across the kingdom, and lead their valiant armies in a battle against the Empire to retrieve the sacred sword!\nBut, time is running short!\n\nCommand troops and choose your mission commanders. Unite with loyal allies and charge into campaigns that forge the destiny of a realm. Between you and the path to victory lie dangerous rogues, the steel of Dalsis, and a confrontation in the ultimate showdown against evil!\""},{"t":"Western Front: The Liberation of Europe 1944–1945","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Strategic Simulations","u":"Strategic Simulations","g":"Computer wargame","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Front:_The_Liberation_of_Europe_1944%E2%80%931945"},{"t":"Where in America's Past Is Carmen Sandiego?","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Broderbund","u":"Broderbund","g":"Educational","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Where_in_America's_Past_Is_Carmen_Sandiego%3F"},{"t":"Where's Waldo?","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Bethesda Softworks","u":"THQ","g":"Hidden object game","r":"July 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Where's_Waldo%3F_(video_game)"},{"t":"Who Framed Roger Rabbit","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Capcom","u":"Capcom","g":"Adventure","r":"November 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Who_Framed_Roger_Rabbit_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Who Shot Johnny Rock?","p":["Arcade"],"d":"American Laser Games, Origin Systems","u":"American Laser Games, Philips, Digital Leisure","g":"Interactive movie, light gun shooter","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Who_Shot_Johnny_Rock%3F"},{"t":"Wild Wheels","p":[],"d":"Red Rat Software","u":"Ocean Software","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wild_Wheels"},{"t":"Wing Commander II: Vengeance of the Kilrathi","p":["MS-DOS","FM Towns"],"d":"Origin Systems","u":"Origin Systems, Mindscape","g":"Space combat simulation","r":"September 7, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing_Commander_II:_Vengeance_of_the_Kilrathi","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co2kka.jpg","s":"You may be the hottest pilot in the fleet, but this will cool your jets! In Wing Commander you blast your way through the Vega Campaign. You'd better be as good as your reputation because the stakes are too high to play it safe. Just when you think that the action can't get any more intense, or the opposition any stiffer, you'll plunge into Wing Commander II. Disgraced and unjustly court-martialed, you have to prove yourself once again and earn the respect of the Confederation High Command. Of course, there is also the little matter of preventing the destruction of the Terran home-worlds."},{"t":"Wings of Wor","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive"],"d":"Masaya","u":"Tec Toy, Dreamworks, Sega, NCS Corporation","g":"Shooter, Arcade","r":"January 25, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/wings-of-wor","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co3xz6.jpg","s":"Gynoug, known in North America as Wings of Wor, is a 1991 scrolling shooter video game developed by Masaya and published by NCS Corporation for the Sega Genesis. The game was released in North America and Europe in 1991 and in Japan on January 25, 1991, and re-released on the Wii Virtual Console exclusively in Japan on May 20, 2008."},{"t":"Wolverine","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Software Creations","u":"LJN","g":"Action, platform","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolverine_(1991_video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co980y.jpg","s":"Wolverine is an action video game. It is a licensed game based on the Marvel Comics superhero of the same name, as well as the X-Men."},{"t":"Wonder Boy in Monster World","p":["Genesis / Mega Drive","PC Engine"],"d":"Westone, Shimada Kikaku","u":"Sega","g":"Platform, Action role-playing, Action-adventure, Metroidvania","r":"October 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wonder_Boy_in_Monster_World","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co52cu.jpg","s":"Monsters have taken over the land, and every day the citizens fear for their lives, hoping that a hero will step forth and bring peace to the embattled country."},{"t":"Wordtris","p":["MS-DOS","Game Boy","macOS","Super Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Armenica, Realtime Associates","u":"Spectrum HoloByte","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wordtris"},{"t":"WordZap","p":["Windows"],"d":"Realtime Associates, MICA","u":"Jaleco Entertainment","g":"Puzzle","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WordZap"},{"t":"World Circuit: The Grand Prix Race Simulation","p":["Amiga","Atari ST"],"d":"MicroProse Software, Inc.","u":"MicroProse Software, Inc., Hasbro Interactive","g":"Racing, Sport","r":"November 1, 1991","l":"https://www.igdb.com/games/world-circuit-the-grand-prix-race-simulation","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co98rz.jpg","s":"Formula 1 Grand Prix (known as World Circuit in the US) is the first installment of Geoff Crammond's Grand Prix series. The game includes all 16 international GP circuits of 1991. Players may drive them in quick race mode, single race or a full championship. The 18 teams and 35 drivers and their performance are based on the 1991 season, but the game doesn't include real names. It is possible to edit and save team and driver names by hand.\n\nThe extensive tuning feature influences the performance of the car. In-race setup allows players to adjust gear ratios, brake balance, wing downforce and tires during each visit to the pit box. Six optional driving aids help F1 rookies finish their race: players can toggle best line and suggested gear displays to learn track tactics, or have auto brakes, auto gears, self-righting spins and indestructibility to improve their driving. A replay system allows players to watch race scenes from three different views, including track-side camera footage.\n\nThe game features 3D graphics and various details and effects such as working rear mirrors, collisions with flying debris, crude damage models (wings are visibly bent after crashes), and wet weather that influences traction, including a fog effect that obscures vision when driving in another car's splash water.\n\nWhile the Amiga version allows multiplayer races in hotseat mode, the PC version originally had solo races only. In 1993, the v1.05 update added modem support and a few other improvements."},{"t":"World Empire","p":["MS-DOS","Windows"],"d":"Viable Software Alternatives","u":"Viable Software Alternatives","g":"Turn-based strategy","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Empire"},{"t":"World League Soccer","p":["Super Nintendo Entertainment System","Sharp X68000"],"d":"C-Lab","u":"Imagineer, Mindscape","g":"Sports","r":"September 20, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_League_Soccer"},{"t":"Wrath of the Demon","p":["Atari ST","Amiga","Amiga CDTV","MS-DOS","Commodore 64"],"d":"Abstrax","u":"ReadySoft","g":"Hack and slash","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrath_of_the_Demon"},{"t":"Wurm: Journey to the Center of the Earth","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Cyclone System","u":"SOFEL, Asmik","g":"Action game","r":"November 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wurm:_Journey_to_the_Center_of_the_Earth"},{"t":"WWF Superstars","p":["Game Boy"],"d":"Rare","u":"LJN","g":"Wrestling","r":"April 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWF_Superstars_(handheld_video_game)"},{"t":"WWF WrestleFest","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Technōs Japan","u":"Technōs Japan, Tecmo","g":"Sports, professional wrestling","r":"June 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWF_WrestleFest"},{"t":"WWF WrestleMania","p":["Amiga","Amstrad CPC","Atari ST","Commodore 64","MS-DOS","ZX Spectrum"],"d":"Twilight","u":"Ocean Software","g":"Fighting","r":"December 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WWF_WrestleMania_(1991_video_game)"},{"t":"Xak: The Tower of Gazzel","p":["MSX","PC-8800 series","PC-9800 series"],"d":"Microcabin","u":"Microcabin","g":"Action role-playing game, dungeon crawl","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xak:_The_Tower_of_Gazzel"},{"t":"Xexex","p":["Arcade"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Scrolling shooter","r":"October 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xexex"},{"t":"Xyllomer","p":[],"d":"Project community","g":"Medieval fantasy RP MUD","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xyllomer"},{"t":"Yo Bro","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"ICOM Simulations","u":"NEC","g":"Run and gun","r":"September 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yo_Bro"},{"t":"Yoshi","p":["Game Boy","Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Game Freak","u":"Nintendo","g":"Puzzle","r":"December 14, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoshi_(video_game)","i":"https://images.igdb.com/igdb/image/upload/t_cover_big/co6fy4.jpg","s":"Yoshi is a puzzle game featuring the titular character. In this game, the player must stack two of the same Mario enemy on top of one another to clear them. The concept of the game was inspired by Tetris."},{"t":"Yume Penguin Monogatari","p":["Nintendo Entertainment System"],"d":"Konami","u":"Konami","g":"Platformer, shooter","r":"January 25, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yume_Penguin_Monogatari"},{"t":"Zarathrusta","p":["Amiga"],"d":"The Whiz Kidz","u":"Hewson Consultants","g":"Multidirectional shooter","r":"February 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zarathrusta"},{"t":"Zero4 Champ","p":["PC Engine"],"d":"Media Rings","u":"Media Rings","g":"Racing video game","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero4_Champ"},{"t":"ZZT","p":["MS-DOS"],"d":"Potomac Computer Systems","u":"Potomac Computer Systems","g":"Action-adventure, game creation system, puzzle","r":"January 15, 1991","l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ZZT"},{"t":"Él","p":[],"l":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89l_(video_game)"}];
